\":11000}));\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1788732221.548441,"turn_id":"01a078ac-bf7f-7050-adcc-4d223220149d"},"name":"exec","status":"completed","type":"custom_tool_call"},{"call_id":"call_DROGrV7m0FiPNrgRl47Jtcbw","id":"ctco_01a078bf-c7f1-7820-bf5c-f444eaa64176","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1788732229.617129,"turn_id":"01a078ac-bf7f-7050-adcc-4d223220149d"},"output":[{"text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.1 seconds\nOutput:\n","type":"input_text"},{"text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"f999fb\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.00000275,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":5753,\"output\":\"diff --cc docs/history.md\\nindex a549e4291,1cbd09671..000000000\\n--- a/docs/history.md\\n+++ b/docs/history.md\\n@@@ -300,21 -290,18 +300,33 @@@ existing checkpoints are included (onl\\n  committed in the repository's history, which stops the connection unless\\n  `--allow-committed-private` is passed (rewriting history is your decision).\\n  \\n- **What leaves the machine, in plain text.** Two things, both readable by\\n- anyone who can read the repository: the setup branch (the shared\\n+ **What leaves the machine.** Two things. The setup branch (the shared\\n  configuration, the sources it references, and the shared version of every\\n++\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c HEAD\\n +tracked entry, per its policies) and this machine's recovery refs\\n +(`refs/mise-history/\u003cmachine\u003e/…`: a snapshot of every tracked file with\\n +`backup = true`, with private paths and paths with `backup = false` removed\\n +from the snapshot, the metadata, and the descriptions). `share = false`\\n +keeps a file out of the setup branch and out of other machines;\\n +`backup = false` keeps it out of the recovery refs; `*.local.toml` and\\n +credential stores default to both `share = false` and `backup = false`\\n +unless a per-file declaration says otherwise.\\n +Everything else stays on this machine. Encrypted recovery refs are not\\n +implemented yet: `--encrypt-backups` and `encrypt_backups = true` are refused\\n +rather than silently uploading in plain text, so a file you would only back\\n +up encrypted is a file to track with `--no-backup` for now.\\n++=======\\n+ tracked entry, per its policies) is always plain text: it is merged and read\\n+ as configuration by every machine, so anyone who can read the repository\\n+ can read it. This machine's recovery refs (`refs/mise-history/\u003cmachine\u003e/…`:\\n+ a snapshot of every tracked file with `backup = true`, with private paths\\n+ and paths with `backup = false` removed from the snapshot, the metadata, and\\n+ the descriptions) are plain text by default, or encrypted with\\n+ `--encrypt-backups` (below). `share = false` keeps a file out of the setup\\n+ branch and out of other machines; `backup = false` keeps it out of the\\n+ recovery refs; `*.local.toml` and credential stores are both unless a\\n+ per-file declaration says otherwise. Everything else stays on this machine.\\n++\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e 4febbceee (feat(dotfiles): encrypted machine backups)\\n  The declaration goes to `[history.origin]` in `config.local.toml` next to the\\n  global config (machine-local, never published: each machine names the\\n  repository the way it reaches it, and a fresh machine's own declaration\\ndiff --cc src/cli/doctor/mod.rs\\nindex 873be3b22,e675587e1..000000000\\n--- a/src/cli/doctor/mod.rs\\n+++ b/src/cli/doctor/mod.rs\\n@@@ -90,33 -82,13 +90,43 @@@ struct DotfilesDiagnosis \\n      /// is longer than a few fetch intervals (a transient error is not).\\n      sync_error: Option\u003cString\u003e,\\n      sync_failing_for_secs: Option\u003cu64\u003e,\\n++\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c HEAD\\n +    /// Failed syncs in a row, since the last success.\\n +    sync_failures: u32,\\n +}\\n +\\n +/// How long syncs have been failing: since the current run of failures\\n +/// began, or, for a record from before that was kept, since the last\\n +/// success. `None` when nothing is known.\\n +fn sync_failure_duration(\\n +    status: \u0026crate::system::history::sync::run::SyncStatus,\\n +    now: chrono::DateTime\u003cchrono::Utc\u003e,\\n +) -\u003e Option\u003cu64\u003e {\\n +    let since = status\\n +        .failing_since\\n +        .as_deref()\\n +        .and_then(|at| chrono::DateTime::parse_from_rfc3339(at).ok())\\n +        .or_else(|| {\\n +            [\u0026status.last_fetch, \u0026status.last_publish]\\n +                .into_iter()\\n +                .flatten()\\n +                .filter_map(|at| chrono::DateTime::parse_from_rfc3339(at).ok())\\n +                .max()\\n +        })?;\\n +    Some(\\n +        (now - since.with_timezone(\u0026chrono::Utc))\\n +            .num_seconds()\\n +            .max(0) as u64,\\n +    )\\n++=======\\n+     /// This machine's recovery refs are encrypted.\\n+     backups_encrypted: bool,\\n+     /// Uploads are being skipped (encryption on without usable recipients).\\n+     backups_error: Option\u003cString\u003e,\\n+     /// Whether an identity here could restore this machine's own encrypted\\n+     /// backups (`None` when they are not encrypted).\\n+     backups_restorable: Option\u003cbool\u003e,\\n++\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e 4febbceee (feat(dotfiles): encrypted machine backups)\\n  }\\n  \\n  enum SystemLoginShellDiagnosis {\\n@@@ -757,7 -723,9 +767,13 @@@ impl Doctor \\n              sync_conflicts: vec![],\\n              sync_error: None,\\n              sync_failing_for_secs: None,\\n++\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c HEAD\\n +            sync_failures: 0,\\n++=======\\n+             backups_encrypted: false,\\n+             backups_error: None,\\n+             backups_restorable: None,\\n++\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e 4febbceee (feat(dotfiles): encrypted machine backups)\\n          };\\n          if let Some(reason) = unavailable {\\n              self.errors.push(format!(\\n@@@ -798,17 -766,34 +814,46 @@@\\n          }\\n          // the setup repository, read from what the last sync persisted:\\n          // nothing is fetched, applied, or asked here\\n-         if let Ok(Some(_)) = crate::system::history::config::origin() {\\n+         if let Ok(Some((_, origin))) = crate::system::history::config::origin() {\\n              use crate::system::history::sync::{apply, run};\\n++\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c HEAD\\n +            let status = match run::read_status(\u0026state_dir) {\\n +                Ok(status) =\u003e status,\\n +                Err(err) =\u003e {\\n +                    let message = format!(\\\"dotfiles: {err:#}\\\");\\n +                    diagnosis.sync_error = Some(message.clone());\\n +                    self.errors.push(message);\\n +                    run::SyncStatus::default()\\n +                }\\n +            };\\n++=======\\n+             let status = run::read_status(\u0026state_dir);\\n+             if origin.encrypt_backups {\\n+                 diagnosis.backups_encrypted = true;\\n+                 if let Some(error) = \u0026status.backup_error {\\n+                     diagnosis.backups_error = Some(error.clone());\\n+                     self.warnings.push(format!(\\n+                         \\\"dotfiles: machine backups are not being uploaded ({error}).\\\\n     Local checkpoints continue; nothing from this machine is recoverable from the repository until recipients are configured.\\\\n     Inspect with: mise bootstrap dotfiles status\\\"\\n+                     ));\\n+                 }\\n+                 // can this machine read what it uploads? decided from the\\n+                 // identities here, without touching the network\\n+                 let loaded = crate::agecrypt::load_all_identities().await;\\n+                 let only_age = origin.recipients.iter().all(|r| r.starts_with(\\\"age1\\\"));\\n+                 let among = origin\\n+                     .recipients\\n+                     .iter()\\n+                     .any(|r| loaded.x25519_public.iter().any(|mine| mine == r));\\n+                 let restorable = loaded.usable() \u003e 0 \u0026\u0026 (!only_age || among);\\n+                 diagnosis.backups_restorable = Some(restorable);\\n+                 if !restorable \u0026\u0026 !origin.recipients.is_empty() {\\n+                     self.warnings.push(\\n+                         \\\"dotfiles: machine backups are encrypted, but no age identity on this machine can decrypt them: this machine could not restore its own backups.\\\\n     Put an identity among the recipients in ~/.config/mise/age.txt, settings.age.key_file, or MISE_AGE_KEY, or add this machine's key with: mise bootstrap dotfiles origin set \u003curl\u003e --encrypt-backups --recipient \u003ckey\u003e\\\"\\n+                             .to_string(),\\n+                     );\\n+                 }\\n+             }\\n++\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e 4febbceee (feat(dotfiles): encrypted machine backups)\\n              if let Some(error) = \u0026status.validation_error {\\n                  diagnosis.sync_error = Some(error.clone());\\n                  self.errors.push(format!(\\\"dotfiles: incoming setup is invalid: {error}. Correct the setup repository and sync again.\\\"));\\ndiff --cc src/system/history/shadow.rs\\nindex 8033fdb80,d51748b4c..000000000\\n--- a/src/system/history/shadow.rs\\n+++ b/src/system/history/shadow.rs\\n@@@ -194,6 -195,7 +195,10 @@@ impl HistoryRepo \\n          tree\\n      }\\n  \\n++\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c HEAD\\n++=======\\n+     /// One level of tree from an `ls-tree`-style listing.\\n++\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e 4febbceee (feat(dotfiles): encrypted machine backups)\\n      pub(crate) fn mktree(\u0026self, listing: \u0026str) -\u003e Result\u003cString\u003e {\\n          self.output_str(PlumbingCall::new([\\\"mktree\\\"]).stdin(listing.as_bytes()))\\n      }\\ndiff --cc src/system/history/sync/backup.rs\\nindex 8a217a289,1425c14b9..000000000\\n--- a/src/system/history/sync/backup.rs\\n+++ b/src/system/history/sync/backup.rs\\n@@@ -7,10 -14,13 +14,17 @@@\\n  \\n  use std::collections::{BTreeMap, BTreeSet};\\n  \\n- use eyre::Result;\\n+ use eyre::{Result, bail};\\n  \\n++\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c HEAD\\n +use super::network::{MACHINES_PREFIX, PushOutcome, REMOTE_MACHINES_PREFIX, Remote};\\n++=======\\n+ use super::encrypted::{self, BackupHeader};\\n+ use super::network::{PushOutcome, REMOTE_MACHINES_PREFIX, Remote};\\n++\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e 4febbceee (feat(dotfiles): encrypted machine backups)\\n  use super::privacy::mask_checkpoint;\\n+ use super::run::SyncStatus;\\n+ use crate::system::history::config::OriginTomlConfig;\\n  use crate::system::history::shadow::{HistoryRepo, Overlay};\\n  use crate::system::history::store::{Entry, OperationStatus};\\n  use crate::system::history::tracked::display_to_tree_path;\\n@@@ -91,19 -197,20 +213,30 @@@ pub(crate) fn upload\\n  ) -\u003e Result\u003cusize\u003e {\\n      let mut count = 0;\\n      for entry in entries {\\n -        if uploaded.contains(\u0026entry.checkpoint.uuid) || !eligible(entry) {\\n +        let uuid = \u0026entry.checkpoint.uuid;\\n +        if uploaded.contains(uuid) || !eligible(entry) {\\n +            continue;\\n +        }\\n +        if mirrored(repo, machine_id, uuid)?.is_some() {\\n +            uploaded.insert(uuid.clone());\\n              continue;\\n          }\\n++\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c HEAD\\n +        let commit = filtered_commit(repo, entry)?;\\n +        let refspec = format!(\\\"{commit}:{}\\\", remote_ref(machine_id, uuid));\\n++=======\\n+         let commit = wrapper_commit(repo, entry, encryption)?;\\n+         // forced: the ref is this machine's alone, and a checkpoint\\n+         // uploaded again after a scheme change is a parentless rewrite of\\n+         // the same content, not a fast-forward\\n+         let refspec = format!(\\n+             \\\"+{commit}:{}\\\",\\n+             remote_ref(machine_id, \u0026entry.checkpoint.uuid)\\n+         );\\n++\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e 4febbceee (feat(dotfiles): encrypted machine backups)\\n          match remote.push(\u0026[refspec], None)? {\\n              PushOutcome::Done =\u003e {\\n -                uploaded.insert(entry.checkpoint.uuid.clone());\\n +                uploaded.insert(uuid.clone());\\n                  count += 1;\\n              }\\n              PushOutcome::Rejected(reason) =\u003e {\\n@@@ -159,158 -265,78 +293,233 @@@ pub(crate) fn remote_refs(remote: \u0026Remo\\n  \\n  #[cfg(test)]\\n  mod tests {\\n++\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c HEAD\\n +    use std::process::Command;\\n +\\n +    use super::*;\\n +    use crate::system::history::checkpoint::test_checkpoint;\\n +    use crate::system::history::store::CoverageEntry;\\n +\\n +    fn repo(tmp: \u0026std::path::Path) -\u003e HistoryRepo {\\n +        HistoryRepo::open_or_init_in(\u0026tmp.join(\\\"state\\\"))\\n +            .unwrap()\\n +            .expect(\\\"git is required for these tests\\\")\\n +    }\\n +\\n +    fn origin(tmp: \u0026std::path::Path) -\u003e String {\\n +        let path = tmp.join(\\\"origin.git\\\");\\n +        let status = Command::new(\\\"git\\\")\\n +            .args([\\\"init\\\", \\\"-q\\\", \\\"--bare\\\", \\\"-b\\\", \\\"main\\\"])\\n +            .arg(\u0026path)\\n +            .status()\\n +            .unwrap();\\n +        assert!(status.success());\\n +        format!(\\\"file://{}\\\", path.display())\\n +    }\\n +\\n +    fn origin_refs(url: \u0026str) -\u003e Vec\u003cString\u003e {\\n +        let output = Command::new(\\\"git\\\")\\n +            .args([\\\"ls-remote\\\", \\\"--quiet\\\", url])\\n +            .output()\\n +            .unwrap();\\n +        String::from_utf8(output.stdout)\\n +            .unwrap()\\n +            .lines()\\n +            .filter_map(|line| line.split('\\\\t').nth(1).map(str::to_string))\\n +            .collect()\\n +    }\\n +\\n +    /// An eligible checkpoint: content, and one entry with `backup = true`.\\n +    fn entry(repo: \u0026HistoryRepo, uuid: \u0026str) -\u003e Entry {\\n +        let blob = repo.hash_blob(b\\\"alias ll='ls -l'\\\\n\\\").unwrap();\\n +        let tree = repo\\n +            .mktree(\u0026format!(\\\"100644 blob {blob}\\\\t.zshrc\\\\n\\\"))\\n +            .unwrap();\\n +        let mut checkpoint = test_checkpoint(uuid, Some(\u0026tree));\\n +        checkpoint.tree.coverage.entries.push(CoverageEntry {\\n +            path: \\\"~/.zshrc\\\".into(),\\n +            mode: \\\"track\\\".into(),\\n +            variant: None,\\n +            source: None,\\n +            autosave: true,\\n +            share: true,\\n +            backup: true,\\n +            state: \\\"live\\\".into(),\\n +            promotion: None,\\n +            private: None,\\n +            declared_in: None,\\n +        });\\n +        let commit = repo\\n +            .write_checkpoint_commit(Some(\u0026tree), \u0026checkpoint, \u0026BTreeMap::new())\\n +            .unwrap();\\n +        Entry {\\n +            id: 1,\\n +            commit,\\n +            checkpoint,\\n++=======\\n+     use super::*;\\n+ \\n+     fn encryption(recipients: \u0026[\u0026str]) -\u003e BackupEncryption {\\n+         BackupEncryption {\\n+             recipients: vec![],\\n+             strings: recipients.iter().map(|s| s.to_string()).collect(),\\n+         }\\n+     }\\n+ \\n+     fn origin(encrypt: bool, recipients: \u0026[\u0026str]) -\u003e OriginTomlConfig {\\n+         OriginTomlConfig {\\n+             url: \\\"file:///origin.git\\\".into(),\\n+             branch: \\\"main\\\".into(),\\n+             encrypt_backups: encrypt,\\n+             recipients: recipients.iter().map(|s| s.to_string()).collect(),\\n++\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e 4febbceee (feat(dotfiles): encrypted machine backups)\\n +        }\\n +    }\\n +\\n +    #[test]\\n++\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c HEAD\\n +    fn a_backup_the_fetch_mirrored_is_recorded_not_pushed() {\\n +        if crate::git::plumbing_binary().is_none() {\\n +            return;\\n +        }\\n +        let tmp = tempfile::tempdir().unwrap();\\n +        let repo = repo(tmp.path());\\n +        let url = origin(tmp.path());\\n +        let remote = Remote::new(\u0026repo, \u0026url);\\n +        let entry = entry(\u0026repo, \\\"u1\\\");\\n +        repo.update_ref(\u0026format!(\\\"{MACHINES_PREFIX}m/u1\\\"), \u0026entry.commit, None)\\n +            .unwrap();\\n +        let mut uploaded = BTreeSet::new();\\n +        assert_eq!(\\n +            upload(\u0026remote, \u0026repo, \u0026[entry], \\\"m\\\", \u0026mut uploaded).unwrap(),\\n +            0\\n +        );\\n +        assert_eq!(uploaded, BTreeSet::from([\\\"u1\\\".to_string()]));\\n +        assert!(origin_refs(\u0026url).is_empty());\\n +    }\\n +\\n +    #[test]\\n +    fn a_forgotten_upload_is_found_on_the_origin_instead_of_pushed_again() {\\n +        if crate::git::plumbing_binary().is_none() {\\n +            return;\\n          }\\n +        let tmp = tempfile::tempdir().unwrap();\\n +        let repo = repo(tmp.path());\\n +        let url = origin(tmp.path());\\n +        let remote = Remote::new(\u0026repo, \u0026url);\\n +        let entry = entry(\u0026repo, \\\"u1\\\");\\n +        let mut uploaded = BTreeSet::new();\\n +        assert_eq!(\\n +            upload(\\n +                \u0026remote,\\n +                \u0026repo,\\n +                std::slice::from_ref(\u0026entry),\\n +                \\\"m\\\",\\n +                \u0026mut uploaded\\n +            )\\n +            .unwrap(),\\n +            1\\n +        );\\n +        assert_eq!(origin_refs(\u0026url), vec![remote_ref(\\\"m\\\", \\\"u1\\\")]);\\n +        // the local record is lost; the next sync's fetch mirrors the ref\\n +        uploaded.clear();\\n +        remote.fetch(\\\"main\\\").unwrap();\\n +        assert_eq!(\\n +            upload(\u0026remote, \u0026repo, \u0026[entry], \\\"m\\\", \u0026mut uploaded).unwrap(),\\n +            0\\n +        );\\n +        assert_eq!(uploaded, BTreeSet::from([\\\"u1\\\".to_string()]));\\n +        assert_eq!(origin_refs(\u0026url), vec![remote_ref(\\\"m\\\", \\\"u1\\\")]);\\n      }\\n  \\n      #[test]\\n +    fn a_differing_backup_on_the_origin_is_kept() {\\n +        if crate::git::plumbing_binary().is_none() {\\n +            return;\\n +        }\\n +        let tmp = tempfile::tempdir().unwrap();\\n +        let repo = repo(tmp.path());\\n +        let url = origin(tmp.path());\\n +        let remote = Remote::new(\u0026repo, \u0026url);\\n +        // another build of the same checkpoint is on the origin already\\n +        let other = entry(\u0026repo, \\\"u1\\\");\\n +        let refspec = format!(\\\"{}:{}\\\", other.commit, remote_ref(\\\"m\\\", \\\"u1\\\"));\\n +        assert_eq!(remote.push(\u0026[refspec], None).unwrap(), PushOutcome::Done);\\n +        let mut entry = entry(\u0026repo, \\\"u1\\\");\\n +        entry.checkpoint.description = \\\"rebuilt\\\".into();\\n +        let mut uploaded = BTreeSet::new();\\n +        assert_eq!(\\n +            upload(\\n +                \u0026remote,\\n +                \u0026repo,\\n +                std::slice::from_ref(\u0026entry),\\n +                \\\"m\\\",\\n +                \u0026mut uploaded\\n +            )\\n +            .unwrap(),\\n +            0\\n +        );\\n +        assert!(uploaded.is_empty());\\n +        let output = Command::new(\\\"git\\\")\\n +            .args([\\\"ls-remote\\\", \\\"--quiet\\\", \u0026url, \u0026remote_ref(\\\"m\\\", \\\"u1\\\")])\\n +            .output()\\n +            .unwrap();\\n +        assert!(String::from_utf8_lossy(\u0026output.stdout).starts_with(\u0026other.commit));\\n++=======\\n+     fn scheme_ignores_order_and_repeats() {\\n+         assert_eq!(scheme(None), PLAIN_SCHEME);\\n+         let a = scheme(Some(\u0026encryption(\u0026[\\\"age1a\\\", \\\"age1b\\\"])));\\n+         let b = scheme(Some(\u0026encryption(\u0026[\\\"age1b\\\", \\\"age1a\\\", \\\"age1a\\\"])));\\n+         assert_eq!(a, b);\\n+         assert!(a.starts_with(\\\"age:\\\"), \\\"{a}\\\");\\n+         assert_ne!(a, scheme(Some(\u0026encryption(\u0026[\\\"age1a\\\"]))));\\n+     }\\n+ \\n+     #[test]\\n+     fn reconcile_scheme_forgets_uploads_only_on_a_change() {\\n+         let mut status = SyncStatus::default();\\n+         status.uploaded.insert(\\\"u1\\\".into());\\n+         // an old status without a scheme is plaintext: no change, no re-upload\\n+         assert!(!reconcile_scheme(\u0026mut status, PLAIN_SCHEME));\\n+         assert_eq!(status.uploaded.len(), 1);\\n+         assert_eq!(status.backup_scheme.as_deref(), Some(PLAIN_SCHEME));\\n+ \\n+         let age = scheme(Some(\u0026encryption(\u0026[\\\"age1a\\\"])));\\n+         assert!(reconcile_scheme(\u0026mut status, \u0026age));\\n+         assert!(status.uploaded.is_empty());\\n+         assert_eq!(status.backup_scheme.as_deref(), Some(age.as_str()));\\n+ \\n+         status.uploaded.insert(\\\"u1\\\".into());\\n+         assert!(!reconcile_scheme(\u0026mut status, \u0026age));\\n+         assert_eq!(status.uploaded.len(), 1);\\n+ \\n+         assert!(reconcile_scheme(\u0026mut status, PLAIN_SCHEME));\\n+         assert!(status.uploaded.is_empty());\\n+     }\\n+ \\n+     #[test]\\n+     fn resolve_refuses_encryption_without_usable_recipients() {\\n+         assert!(\\n+             BackupEncryption::resolve(\u0026origin(false, \u0026[]))\\n+                 .unwrap()\\n+                 .is_none()\\n+         );\\n+         let err = BackupEncryption::resolve(\u0026origin(true, \u0026[]))\\n+             .unwrap_err()\\n+             .to_string();\\n+         assert!(err.contains(\\\"names no recipients\\\"), \\\"{err}\\\");\\n+         assert!(!err.contains(\\\"experimental\\\"), \\\"{err}\\\");\\n+         let err = BackupEncryption::resolve(\u0026origin(true, \u0026[\\\"not-a-key\\\"]))\\n+             .unwrap_err()\\n+             .to_string();\\n+         assert!(err.contains(\\\"neither an age public key\\\"), \\\"{err}\\\");\\n+         let resolved = BackupEncryption::resolve(\u0026origin(\\n+             true,\\n+             \u0026[\\\"age1ql3z7hjy54pw3hyww5ayyfg7zqgvc7w3j2elw8zmrj2kg5sfn9aqmcac8p\\\"],\\n+         ))\\n+         .unwrap()\\n+         .unwrap();\\n+         assert_eq!(resolved.recipients.len(), 1);\\n++\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e 4febbceee (feat(dotfiles): encrypted machine backups)\\n      }\\n  }\\ndiff --cc src/system/history/sync/onboard.rs\\nindex 731d729cd,f748173eb..000000000\\n--- a/src/system/history/sync/onboard.rs\\n+++ b/src/system/history/sync/onboard.rs\\n@@@ -219,20 -243,25 +253,42 @@@ pub(crate) async fn run(store: \u0026Store, \\n      }\\n  \\n      // the connection: declared machine-locally, recorded for the watcher\\n++\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c HEAD\\n +    let newest = store\\n +        .list()?\\n +        .into_iter()\\n +        .last()\\n +        .map(|entry| entry.checkpoint.created_at);\\n +    run::update_status(state_dir, run::STATUS_LOCK_WAIT, |status| {\\n +        status.origin_url = Some(onboarding.origin.clone());\\n +        status.origin_branch = Some(onboarding.branch.clone());\\n +        status.disconnected = false;\\n +        if status.upload_since.is_none() {\\n +            status.upload_since = Some(newest.unwrap_or_else(hstore::now_rfc3339));\\n +        }\\n +    })?;\\n +    super::origin::write_config(\u0026onboarding.origin, \u0026onboarding.branch, None)?;\\n++=======\\n+     let mut status = run::read_status(state_dir);\\n+     status.origin_url = Some(onboarding.origin.clone());\\n+     status.origin_branch = Some(onboarding.branch.clone());\\n+     status.disconnected = false;\\n+     if status.upload_since.is_none() {\\n+         let newest = store\\n+             .list()?\\n+             .into_iter()\\n+             .last()\\n+             .map(|entry| entry.checkpoint.created_at);\\n+         status.upload_since = Some(newest.unwrap_or_else(hstore::now_rfc3339));\\n+     }\\n+     run::write_status(state_dir, \u0026status)?;\\n+     super::origin::write_config(\\n+         \u0026onboarding.origin,\\n+         \u0026onboarding.branch,\\n+         None,\\n+         backups.as_ref(),\\n+     )?;\\n++\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e 4febbceee (feat(dotfiles): encrypted machine backups)\\n  \\n      let applied = apply::apply(store, \u0026tracked, \u0026ApplyRequest::automatic()).await?;\\n      // a conflict (a file that exists here and differs) is not pending: it\\ndiff --cc src/system/history/sync/origin.rs\\nindex f57127f54,f095bcde5..000000000\\n--- a/src/system/history/sync/origin.rs\\n+++ b/src/system/history/sync/origin.rs\\n@@@ -271,7 -402,31 +403,35 @@@ async fn set_inner\\n      // say, so `mise settings set history.sync …` keeps working afterwards\\n      let mode =\\n          (opts.mode.as_str() != crate::config::Settings::get().history.sync).then_some(opts.mode);\\n++\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c HEAD\\n +    write_config(\u0026opts.url, \u0026opts.branch, mode)?;\\n++=======\\n+     let backup_config = match backups {\\n+         BackupPlan::Plain =\u003e BackupConfig {\\n+             encrypt: false,\\n+             recipients: vec![],\\n+         },\\n+         BackupPlan::Encrypted {\\n+             mut recipients,\\n+             generate,\\n+         } =\u003e {\\n+             if let Some(path) = generate {\\n+                 let public = crate::agecrypt::generate_identity_file(\u0026path)?;\\n+                 miseprintln!(\\n+                     \\\"Generated an age identity at {}; its public key is {public}. Copy the file to every machine that should restore these backups (mise never uploads it).\\\",\\n+                     display_path(\u0026path)\\n+                 );\\n+                 recipients = vec![public];\\n+             }\\n+             BackupConfig {\\n+                 encrypt: true,\\n+                 recipients,\\n+             }\\n+         }\\n+     };\\n+     write_config(\u0026opts.url, \u0026opts.branch, mode, Some(\u0026backup_config))?;\\n+     let mut status = run::read_status(state_dir);\\n++\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e 4febbceee (feat(dotfiles): encrypted machine backups)\\n      // another repository or branch starts from a clean slate: the previous\\n      // one's per-path state, pending changes, and conflicts would read its\\n      // absence of a file as a deletion\\n\"}","type":"input_text"}],"type":"custom_tool_call_output"}],"latest_token_usage_record":{"response_id":"resp_02be90ffdd5dff4a016a9de346342c87d084d9db418ffc1549","root_turn_id":"01a078ac-bf7f-7050-adcc-4d223220149d","session_id":"01a07177-9855-71a2-89cd-4a9a37cdbfcb","thread_id":"01a07177-9855-71a2-89cd-4a9a37cdbfcb","thread_token_usage":{"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":160804864,"input_tokens":164737542,"output_tokens":438372,"reasoning_output_tokens":83944,"total_tokens":165175914},"turn_id":"01a078ac-bf7f-7050-adcc-4d223220149d","turn_token_usage":{"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":17583232,"input_tokens":17713104,"output_tokens":24884,"reasoning_output_tokens":4770,"total_tokens":17737988},"usage":{"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":222080,"input_tokens":228845,"output_tokens":4642,"reasoning_output_tokens":0,"total_tokens":233487}},"message":"","previous_window_id":"01a07886-0305-7c01-8140-5a79595963dc","replacement_history":[{"content":[{"text":"what do you think of this pr stack? [https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/12819](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/12819)\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07177-9f17-7a32-a2c4-314ff8e90c2f","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788610060.055681,"turn_id":"01a07177-9976-7be2-8023-b5f544431749"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"i'm more wondering from a product perspective. is this useful? the goal is to be a better solution for omarchy for config than nix could be. are there alternatives you think might be better? will users find this easy to use and useful?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0717b-29eb-75d3-8281-48fad94b3f32","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788610292.203893,"turn_id":"01a0717b-292e-7da0-bc0c-5cd7b1cc488f"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"one idea, since omarchy is an agent-based os, maybe we could persist things like hyprland edits with sort of a debounce loop that makes a commit to their dotfile with a cheap agent giving it a good commit message so users don't need to manage individual commits\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07182-2113-7c71-8b74-76d5a1e5c3c0","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788610748.691143,"turn_id":"01a07182-2042-7f22-913c-304ca279b551"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"The main wrinkle is that a pause doesn’t necessarily mean the user finished. Someone might save an intermediate broken config and stop to investigate. So automatic checkpoints should mean “saved state,” with a separate “known working” designation based on explicit confirmation or relevant validation.\n\nyou know i'm not so sure! i feel like in video games this is dealt with via automatic saves just fine, between timestamps and good messages they should be able to roll back\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07183-8d3a-7822-a3e3-629ab5baa993","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788610841.914645,"turn_id":"01a07183-8c76-7013-b245-208a1b71e397"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"how does that change our direction?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07183-f002-7aa2-858c-4f0d020d3d3c","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788610867.202376,"turn_id":"01a07183-ef53-7fd0-abdf-38ec184c5d34"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"i think we need to consider what happens if a user adds files to their setup that are constantly changing\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07184-ad4e-7240-b359-63314bc07129","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788610915.662632,"turn_id":"01a07184-ac9d-7702-bd80-b10b12424769"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"ok write up a plan I can send to my agent working on this, but not just this auto saving thing, make any other changes you think should be made to this\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07185-71d5-7d13-93d2-8ab7dc87ab86","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788610965.973355,"turn_id":"01a07185-7125-7083-b358-d89f2ef6505f"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"\n# Files pasted by the user:\n\n## \"# Plan: `mise bootstrap` generations + rollback (PR stack) ## Context Omarchy i…\": /Users/jdx/.codex/attachments/e445b525-8e13-46f7-8081-0b760405104a/pasted-text.txt\n\n## My request:\nhere's his plan:\n\n\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0719e-35a4-7211-97af-44a4b31b9b80","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788612588.964547,"turn_id":"01a0719e-34d0-7a40-b22a-7b9b477476a3"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"\n# Files pasted by the user:\n\n## \"# Plan: `mise history` — automatic configuration history (single authoritative …\": /Users/jdx/.codex/attachments/28d9172c-99b9-440b-96ea-44897dadf15a/pasted-text.txt\n\n## My request:\nrevision 2\n\n\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071b0-2169-7b53-a92b-e596ffc6cbf4","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788613763.433202,"turn_id":"01a071b0-2056-7d71-abe3-daf5474a71a5"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"explain the plan a little bit, give me a summary\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071b2-1f24-74e2-8f83-6918a3059d60","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788613893.924369,"turn_id":"01a071b2-1e4f-7692-8574-0e3e3dc9b94e"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"ok so it's mise owned but under the hood is it a git repo in the home directory?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071b2-e51c-7641-888b-33939ca59fa8","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788613944.604075,"turn_id":"01a071b2-e459-7a22-95c5-f0a9aa3c3abd"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"how does this interact with current bootstrap functionality like dotfiles?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071b3-420a-7e02-ad68-f1e2ebe6e540","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788613968.394265,"turn_id":"01a071b3-4152-7a40-8e00-e7d95464dafa"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"how does the user configure it? how do they add an existing config file to it?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071b3-de84-7ba3-917b-0ff8a307e2ac","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788614008.452403,"turn_id":"01a071b3-ddcf-7b11-be92-831a39bcb4a3"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"i'm wondering if this should be a setting, should it be something like `mise history paths add`?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071b4-796f-7871-b698-eb1d0cf3da1d","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788614048.111721,"turn_id":"01a071b4-78ba-78e2-a2bc-d0aea265abda"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"so if i am a current user of mise, all i need to do is `mise history paths add ~/.zshrc` and those changes will now be tracked assuming i setup the daemon?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071b5-24d5-7c51-a789-d78007195fe1","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788614091.989175,"turn_id":"01a071b5-241e-76f2-8625-a3deecc5a78d"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"yeah we should warn if the daemon is off and tell them how to set it up via bootstrap.\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071b5-755b-7052-8571-e19c9caefe2c","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788614112.603402,"turn_id":"01a071b5-74a7-74f0-a9e8-d552efbf4d4e"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"so i know some of the changes we talked about are about the current state like after i install brew packages which we should be able to roll back with this right? are those changes tracked in the git repo? how does that work with multiple machines sharing the same repo?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071b6-150f-7cc0-8f00-4f30dd6e3448","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788614153.487712,"turn_id":"01a071b6-1457-7951-8433-9709fb647b67"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"ok so the operation records are not in this repo?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071b6-a698-7ed1-b5f6-b00a1f099a69","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788614190.744988,"turn_id":"01a071b6-a5d6-7882-91f3-583462609130"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"so if i want to roll back what happens?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071b7-9b85-7690-ac9c-f8b243a457f1","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788614253.445177,"turn_id":"01a071b7-9acc-7bd3-9bdd-67c897feb529"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"how do i trigger a rollback?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071b7-deb5-7562-b65a-a8ba46430854","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788614270.645457,"turn_id":"01a071b7-ddfc-7bd2-9d4a-ddda8cb5fb0a"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"so how does it decide to roll back since there are git changes but also checkpoints?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071b8-45ce-7341-b321-f77e9460e4e0","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788614297.038465,"turn_id":"01a071b8-4512-7bf1-bcb0-5b0812cdab57"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"ok so if i want to rollback a change to zshrc what happens\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071b9-389a-7142-8bdf-f4bc93fd200e","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788614359.194236,"turn_id":"01a071b9-37dd-7150-bf20-ea1b27900ec7"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"seems like rollback could optionally take a path argument\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071b9-c933-75e3-a434-1f6f6b35fa36","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788614396.211318,"turn_id":"01a071b9-c86d-7811-b12b-cf5ffcfd785b"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"ok let's talk about syncing. i think we should have a way to explicitly sync and also a way to automatically sync to origin, probably enabled by default\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071ba-5a89-7691-8c2b-cbe2a0bd44a2","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788614433.41744,"turn_id":"01a071ba-59cc-7041-b4b3-1cd746c9ebe7"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"i'm also wondering what happens in a rollback situation, let's say on one machine i roll back a change and on another i am already on i\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071bb-2a42-7d61-a829-b7a02bce1283","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788614486.594375,"turn_id":"01a071bb-2987-7da1-9ae7-c2e30f9cbdd2"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"syncing needs to be part of this plan\n\ntell me what to tell the other agent based on these last few messages\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071bb-d4d1-7510-bc99-d80e12f3878f","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788614530.257542,"turn_id":"01a071bb-d41d-7cb3-b7be-103d9efe4ca1"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"how could setting up the origin work?\\\\\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071bd-8a5a-7b70-ae0e-a2c24b3df0c5","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788614642.266189,"turn_id":"01a071bd-898f-7413-bd3d-e72799e87266"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"i think this is a better setup than manually modifying dotfiles like in the current mise bootstrap, we probably don't want to deprecate that functionality but I think we should encourage mise history\n\nalso, what will mise config look like here, and can the global mise config be part of the history easilly? will this also integrate well with initial setup with `mise bootstrap --from-git`?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071be-d53d-7773-8826-7bad5f8b3e23","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788614726.973897,"turn_id":"01a071be-d477-7b51-aba3-a7dad6e90d54"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"should these be settings or no?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071bf-93cf-7c72-a450-d551a6a3d61c","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788614775.759034,"turn_id":"01a071bf-9319-7451-a630-904e25b02cde"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"should it be part of bootstrap?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071bf-ef30-7d82-879f-15a35721bedf","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788614799.152423,"turn_id":"01a071bf-ee67-72f2-9ba8-38b594c0a5ca"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"\n# Files pasted by the user:\n\n## \"# Plan: `mise history` — automatic configuration history (single authoritative …\": /Users/jdx/.codex/attachments/df5fc5e2-08d5-4fff-aea6-cb8b018fcc1e/pasted-text.txt\n\n## My request:\nhere's the latest plan: \n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071c1-6f3a-7b01-9272-c7610d83e241","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788614897.46648,"turn_id":"01a071c1-6e6b-7712-a9af-3fd60a2c89e8"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"so you think we should use \\~/.config/mise/config.local.toml?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071c2-acb3-7733-ac3b-29c01c9e20f5","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788614978.739601,"turn_id":"01a071c2-abfe-7a92-b8d5-211f769c26ee"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"ok give me the instructions to send to him\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071c3-00b7-7bc3-ac5c-039acc3caa77","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788615000.247458,"turn_id":"01a071c2-fff2-7ba1-8e98-3bb5e648da98"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"don't we already support config.local.toml?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071c3-70c0-7341-8d74-10aa911ae646","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788615028.928039,"turn_id":"01a071c3-7009-7a01-8cf2-c4310db10376"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"ok give me the full revision instructions i can send to him\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071c3-dd69-77b2-ba5d-3fa29a8c9c10","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788615056.745959,"turn_id":"01a071c3-dcb5-7993-ba98-d01a9ab86339"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"what are some of the features people like from chezmoi we might be lacking with this setup? I'm thinking that users may want to have tags, platform-specific config, secrets, what else is lacking?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071c5-26c4-7dc0-8451-732beb260032","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788615141.060622,"turn_id":"01a071c5-2600-7d80-8140-57992b9b5745"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"how could we even offer this with this setup?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071c7-3253-7fb1-af45-11da74615497","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788615275.091932,"turn_id":"01a071c7-3189-7c83-8f8a-1092c8196e30"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"i'm thinking like `mise history paths add ~/.zshrc --platform macos-arm64`? what do you think?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071c8-1801-7593-a4d0-89c2c045fccd","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788615333.889248,"turn_id":"01a071c8-174e-74c0-856f-1462026e1554"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"we have some prior art with things like mise.macos.toml\n\nbut how would this work under the hood? would I still be able to edit \\~/.zshrc and have it autosync just for macs?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071c8-d7a1-7850-8ef9-ecc5e4376ae1","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788615382.945137,"turn_id":"01a071c8-d6ec-7a22-9248-ed12d9595e3d"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"i think users would prefer if the platform was inside the same config file\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071c9-b133-76a1-9e2f-7a7a90466ef6","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788615438.643792,"turn_id":"01a071c9-b080-7bb2-bac3-8b66bba68168"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"ok does that model work for other chezmoi gaps?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071c9-fbd7-7e62-9d26-5691c7d44d00","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788615457.75196,"turn_id":"01a071c9-fb24-71b0-b932-2285a9e7ae62"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"\n# Files pasted by the user:\n\n## \"# Plan: `mise history` — automatic configuration history (single authoritative …\": /Users/jdx/.codex/attachments/313b84f6-06c6-46ce-bcd9-b0ed8d2ae348/pasted-text.txt\n\n## My request:\nok here's the latest draft, tell me how to improve it based on what we discussed since: \n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071ca-d9c6-71a2-8f39-cb9611775af5","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788615514.56633,"turn_id":"01a071ca-d910-7070-9681-36c16f7b21fd"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"what does backup encrypted and share false do?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071cc-97fc-7a43-8fcc-9d51650216d2","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788615628.796985,"turn_id":"01a071cc-9751-70c0-a1d9-3c6a46adc9d4"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"how do those work under the hood?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071cc-fe02-7581-bb30-c9ae4e64192b","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788615654.9148,"turn_id":"01a071cc-fd3e-7ac1-b722-16bcfffc634d"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"i think another useful config would be for noisy files, like i might want to share a file but not have it automatically persist\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071cd-d37d-7680-8c34-56c0d7892ea3","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788615709.565342,"turn_id":"01a071cd-d2bd-78c2-be5f-d851021fe664"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"i think another thing we should account for is having something like templates. I was thinking we already solve that with bootstrap dotfiles so we probably just need to make sure they can use both of them together\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071ce-9f51-7130-9074-952fae50a84b","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788615761.745528,"turn_id":"01a071ce-9e9b-7313-b43a-4c5904bee6a5"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"ok how does this plan compare to chezmoi then? also, users will want the templates and the history stuff to be in the same git repo, how will that work?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071cf-400f-7d30-a80f-5608f920ba38","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788615802.895281,"turn_id":"01a071cf-3f50-7181-b623-451d8dba0a07"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"\n# Files pasted by the user:\n\n## \"# Plan: `mise history` — automatic configuration history with sync (single auth…\": /Users/jdx/.codex/attachments/1e482590-f4d5-4b5a-8148-73194a87903c/pasted-text.txt\n\n## My request:\nhere's the latest revision, tell me what to modify, I think it missed some of the stuff we talked about \n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071d0-0e4b-70d1-bc2d-e08cbf8e6634","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788615855.691127,"turn_id":"01a071d0-0d83-7a83-8305-ee35df4436f5"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"i am kind of wondering if we should flatten this into bootstrap.dotfiles\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071d4-0486-7e23-a8de-fc5403266ed5","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788616115.334975,"turn_id":"01a071d4-03b1-7760-96ca-f6c4a1c91553"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"i don't understand, doesn't that example config conflict with the current dotfiles setup?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071d4-e7d4-77e0-a9fe-ff851e8ebfa7","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788616173.524847,"turn_id":"01a071d4-e72e-7e51-bd7e-0f6405122cf5"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"we should restructure the docs too because we'll want them to use tracking since it's the better way to manage the dotfiles. we should provide a golden path setup for bootstrap (btw the bootstrap docs might need to be restructred and cleaned up anyhow) but the golden path should be tracked config files, setting up the origin and all that. I think the current setup is useful as an alternative or fallback for things like templating\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071d6-8039-7e01-805a-fd9a5ccf2a81","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788616278.073427,"turn_id":"01a071d6-7f6f-7501-8bc6-30f5a7dc353f"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"i think you should read the current bootstrap docs and see how best to merge it with this concept\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071d7-2a54-7f13-a431-de843b38d2b7","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788616321.620509,"turn_id":"01a071d7-2997-7a62-9b2a-54ff35d7f92e"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"could we have separate docs per os? or perhaps maybe some examples that show things like omarchy, ubuntu, macos, and windows? we could have cookbooks too but i think those are a bit hidden but we could link to them for more esoteric setups\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071d8-b36d-7282-8299-642f63a94ac7","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788616422.253996,"turn_id":"01a071d8-b2a7-7eb2-9259-f2370fc52296"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"\n# Files pasted by the user:\n\n## \"# Plan: `mise history` — configuration history, sharing, and setup (single auth…\": /Users/jdx/.codex/attachments/f8b2dcb1-fa5a-4d4c-af9e-6444dbeda97f/pasted-text.txt\n\n## My request:\nok based on what we discussed since the last review, tell me how to modify the latest plan \n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071d9-c908-7ea2-a406-074e1eb2f50f","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788616493.320486,"turn_id":"01a071d9-c833-7b91-a564-4f5be771061c"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"in \\~/src/blog, write a blog post assuming we launched this\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071db-b323-73f0-a488-76931e79ed6c","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788616618.78736,"turn_id":"01a071db-b26b-71f2-b554-a82f19ce88d5"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"\n# Files pasted by the user:\n\n## \"# Plan: bootstrap adopts and recreates your setup; `[dotfiles]` declares how ea…\": /Users/jdx/.codex/attachments/876266e9-46aa-4d5b-8150-6dd8e9abd323/pasted-text.txt\n\n## My request:\nhow does this plan look now \n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071e2-028e-73c2-b637-226702d0de02","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788617032.334099,"turn_id":"01a071e2-0179-70d2-aa7a-311127da97c2"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"i like the idea of `watch = t`rue but i think it should live in bootstrap.services instead unless you disagree\n\n\n\n### 2. Fetch must not update the active global configuration\n\nwhy not? i'm not so sure\n\n\n\nI think we should prevent users from adding .local.toml files but maybe give them a setting to opt out of that\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071e4-fff9-7881-9841-88892fd08602","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788617228.281127,"turn_id":"01a071e4-ff34-7bd2-ae86-91db6458b725"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"do we not support user-level services too? i thought we did, if not, that'll be a prereq for this stack\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071e5-e132-7dc3-82ca-35bf3c5d23f1","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788617285.938672,"turn_id":"01a071e5-e073-7e51-84f3-4251db875b92"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"mise bootstrap history apply\n\nmise history status\n\n- that is weird\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071e6-5822-70d3-b566-9940368fa241","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788617316.386237,"turn_id":"01a071e6-5760-7e43-a848-f4932ed38b4a"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"ok give me the instructions to send back to the other agent\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071e7-1007-7f00-b86a-2b098b1c9789","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788617363.463932,"turn_id":"01a071e7-0f51-79e1-bee8-84b9d98d9cd7"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"also update your blog post\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071e8-000a-73d1-b6ab-8ca2a501ee05","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788617424.906856,"turn_id":"01a071e7-ff64-7801-a774-8ced7e53c96b"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"i think in the blog post we can explicitly call out tools like chezmoi as an example and where it falls flat, but also be honest if there are gaps with mise\n\nsecrets may be one, but we also have secrets in env and fnox so it is less important\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071eb-4d87-7651-9468-b72f45874c4d","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788617641.351204,"turn_id":"01a071eb-4cc5-7242-83fb-345f82178664"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"mise is never capitalized btw\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071ec-969a-7850-b12a-4cc673ba1d36","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788617725.59467,"turn_id":"01a071ec-95f2-7d10-b9a1-8458f6016ae7"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"i used to use a dotfile manager called rcm, maybe worth showing my former workflow in the intro. i had this `git-sync.sh` thing that I manually installed as a launchagent, you might be able to find jdx/dotfiles-old or something with it\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071ef-4e26-7b62-a9c6-9db3a0c9ba59","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788617903.654426,"turn_id":"01a071ef-4c3e-76e1-8c2b-8eb6a20e5b23"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"that is not a public repo\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071f1-fe09-7c21-af08-a3fa8298bd79","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788618079.753048,"turn_id":"01a071f1-fbf7-72f3-9457-0a3df2c9aea2"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"the rcm involved a lot of custom flaky code and complex cli commands, it was a pain to onboard new boxes too. mise not only simplifies that but it has much more capability\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071f3-9fac-7e90-adb8-a1d6d0c90799","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788618186.668571,"turn_id":"01a071f3-9e5a-7b40-829a-5323e4ea8a06"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"i think we should hit on integrating with mise bootstrap so setup is just `mise bootstrap --from-git`\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071f4-3df7-7401-9336-f2dc79df2e7e","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788618227.191596,"turn_id":"01a071f4-3d2d-72b3-a014-63717c451580"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"saying that though, it's not enough since my dotfiles repo will be private. so there will be some initial setup like installing mise and setting up git auth before i can use it, could we improve that workflow with mise?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071f5-0f90-74d0-9392-e18a23ceeb1b","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788618280.848926,"turn_id":"01a071f5-0db4-7ea2-994a-15e681b94e59"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"maybe read through my past blog posts to make this sound like i am writing it too\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071f6-19b8-7f42-ab51-851bfd4da8f0","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788618348.98467,"turn_id":"01a071f6-18da-7f53-bc17-3250d9acf58a"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"i like your suggestion about --from-git improvements, let's include that in the plan, but before that, but before that maybe we could think about the current GITHUB\\_TOKEN setup stuff? we have a lot of ways to configure that\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071fa-b84e-7693-a40f-59aa242f4866","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788618651.726768,"turn_id":"01a071fa-b793-75c2-bc53-4642c3e2b72c"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"personally i would probably want to go with the oauth thing\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071fc-a14e-7be2-a960-32a74231b812","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788618776.910471,"turn_id":"01a071fc-a0a0-7a12-a1de-868a787d0155"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"i think this could go into the blog post, and maybe as a callout tip for the docs but generally the oauth app would be optinoal, i think users would need to setup gh and auth themselves—which they can install with mise\n\nso i think `curl mise.run, mise x gh -- gh auth login`, mise bootstrap --form-git\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071fd-eacd-77b2-a1eb-996733f54563","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788618861.261747,"turn_id":"01a071fd-ea15-7f61-9f79-0ac7634d9740"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"or alternatively we should have a workflow for mise bootstrap remote, ideally where we can somehow share the token with the remote box. i think what would be really cool in fact is if we didn't need to put the token on the box and instead could use the host as a proxy to talk to github\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a071ff-131b-7030-a3f5-4aed4285648b","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788618937.115176,"turn_id":"01a071ff-1268-79a3-b250-d5a95b9d1692"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"\n# Files pasted by the user:\n\n## \"# Plan: bootstrap adopts and recreates the setup; `[dotfiles]` declares file ma…\": /Users/jdx/.codex/attachments/e48b166e-161b-49a3-9d17-7c4072d06ac9/pasted-text.txt\n\n## My request:\nneat idea, i have another agent working on it now.\n\nNow, take what we've discussed and review the latest plan draft\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07200-d4aa-7252-aab7-90aeccdc0f63","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788619052.202824,"turn_id":"01a07200-d3e2-7901-8d8b-e0fffa61dcaa"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"question, when they use --from-git does it get stored as a bare repo?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07207-6c07-7342-9df1-c086bb20cdf9","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788619484.167703,"turn_id":"01a07207-6b43-72c0-a2b7-71529f2f0bb9"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"yeah let's simplify it, but we need backwards compat\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07207-c328-7b83-9438-5736c71705a7","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788619506.47214,"turn_id":"01a07207-c277-7cf0-868b-80483eded2c5"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"ok but if they use a bare repo, how are they able to use something like templates?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07208-c193-7081-aa2c-0c30e99f4555","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788619571.603653,"turn_id":"01a07208-c0e2-7981-a1e4-ab12890522e9"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"so they'd also probably need to track the templates?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07209-8758-7ef0-8ce5-8e0bb8b4222a","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788619622.23257,"turn_id":"01a07209-86a7-7c53-a49e-001dab1bbb3c"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"agreed. so it sounds like if they want tracking they need to use bare\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0720a-4590-7d82-9574-2f1099450607","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788619670.928459,"turn_id":"01a0720a-44d0-7003-8d68-d8852a3fac33"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"show me some exapmles\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0720a-c7a8-7d42-b948-61e9c91a705c","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788619704.232919,"turn_id":"01a0720a-c6f7-77d2-87a3-e0724fdc0290"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"oh so it's fine because we never actually managed the git repo before\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0720b-ec41-7333-a87a-409cfd0f93c7","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788619779.137851,"turn_id":"01a0720b-eb8c-7750-b552-2024a1f14171"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"ok give me the full instructions to send to the other agent including your last corrections\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0720c-2f47-7d83-825e-8341cf0b303b","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788619796.295168,"turn_id":"01a0720c-2e95-7741-8779-12b0e88a3d29"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"\n# Files pasted by the user:\n\n## \"# Plan: bootstrap adopts and recreates the setup; `[dotfiles]` declares file ma…\": /Users/jdx/.codex/attachments/e4d99c20-8c58-438f-8ec9-d7f84685c0a4/pasted-text.txt\n\n## My request:\nreview \n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0721e-1748-71d1-88e8-d50fb16b2706","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788620969.800992,"turn_id":"01a0721e-1689-77a2-900c-63c2c3b82ba8"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"make a pr for that blog post\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a072e7-edfa-7c20-a213-ad4942c9ef99","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788634197.498089,"turn_id":"01a072e7-ec26-7300-9c9a-a4025ab5295a"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"rebase the blog pr\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a072e8-0e8f-7411-ac1d-f697e67f25ac","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788634205.839443,"turn_id":"01a072e7-ec26-7300-9c9a-a4025ab5295a"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"i believe all the prs are up, how does it look? how does it compare to the blog post?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07307-f793-7242-ae30-ac6e498dc712","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788636297.107288,"turn_id":"01a07307-f67e-7aa2-a103-fc48da7a7417"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"i'm wondering if it should be `mise history` or `mise bootstrap history`\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07348-9d82-72d0-8f35-1cb7fc6eeba4","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788640533.890321,"turn_id":"01a07348-9c97-7dc1-be2a-a4ddbb3f55b4"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"on the blog post\n\nI think we're explaining too much about rcm details\n\n\"and the part I'm most excited about is the integration with `mise bootstrap`\" it is part of mise bootstrap so that sounds weird\n\nsee the pr i landed with `mise ssh`, we should incorporate that into the blog post and later the pr stack\n\"private, btw,\" - remove the \"btw\"\n\n\"mise installs and runs `gh`, so you don't need to install it separately\" - obviously, but we should use the proxy thing from the mise ssh thing anyhow\n\nwe should show output for commands like \"mise bootstrap\"\n\n\"If the watcher isn't running, mise warns you and tells you how to set it up. Adding a file saves its current contents, but obviously it can't keep saving changes without something watching it.\" i think we can strike this\u0026#x20;\n\n\"After a short pause,\" how does the debouncing work exactly? I think it should be fairly complex, like save reasonably quick after a change unless the same file keeps beign modified or something, like have some sort of backoff? it's probably also worth explaining what happens if the user adds a noisy file (which we should tell them not to)\n\n\"Files ending in `.local.toml`, including mise's `config.local.toml`, are protected from sharing by default—even when you enroll their parent directory. They can still have local recovery history, and their remote backups follow the backup and encryption policies. If you deliberately want to share one, you can explicitly override that protection for the file.\" remove that\n\n\"Connecting an existing repository also checks outgoing history for private files that were already committed. Adding an ignore rule today doesn't make yesterday's commit private.\" - remove\n\n\nremove the section on chezmoi\n\nmake the section about secrets a lot shorter\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0736d-6ba9-7e13-82fc-05caacbd1e75","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788642945.961264,"turn_id":"01a0736d-6a8a-7773-ac4f-584d4500af7b"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"that seems way too short, also, how does warning actually work when it's running as a service?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07370-910b-7893-937d-d50a3e131571","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788643152.139706,"turn_id":"01a07370-903b-76b1-a7da-36cec20f393b"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"i think it should go up to like 24 hours if the file continuously changes\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07371-0ac0-7552-95e3-3bf1285f28b5","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788643183.296302,"turn_id":"01a07371-09f5-7f72-86c0-729423541b84"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"ok edit the blog post to mention this, it doesn't need a lot of detail, i'll later have you instruct the other agent about this decision\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07371-7c12-7bd3-b45e-b6def9c1541e","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788643212.306618,"turn_id":"01a07371-7b50-7cb0-8378-37b5ea3403a7"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"the first time we mention mise bootstrap we should link to it\n\nI don't think we should use `mise ssh`, we should use `mise bootstrap remote` with the right flags for the github proxy\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07372-cf65-73e2-a6c3-0b98f9bd637a","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788643299.173311,"turn_id":"01a07372-ce80-7131-b302-8e2a3827cff0"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"make this non-draft\n\nuse 'jdx/dotfiles' as my repo name\n\nwhat happens when there is a conflict when the service is running?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07376-2f97-7b22-ac73-2ed539030471","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788643520.40775,"turn_id":"01a07376-2ed7-7143-a8f1-03f0b1614350"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"The GitHub token stays on my laptop, and borrowed access ends when the bootstrap run finishes. This currently works between Linux and macOS machines. It’s read-only setup access, not permanent sync authentication: a remote machine that should push its own changes later still needs write credentials.\n\nmention after this `mise ssh` can be used for permanent sessions\n\n\"plus mise-managed recovery history for individual machines\" - is this sent to the origin repo?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07379-41fe-7fe0-b71c-839b946bd250","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788643721.726146,"turn_id":"01a07379-412a-7d53-87cc-3ca28df3adc5"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"\"You don’t need a second repository just for templates.\" - this doesn't need to be said\n\nthe `mise ssh` thing can go into a tip callout\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0737b-7694-7e71-a607-62291e84c951","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788643866.260493,"turn_id":"01a0737b-75b6-7e22-abea-31c6e80a0c3c"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"i'm wondering if we conflated dotfile history with history of bootstrap apply things like installing packages\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0737c-6f99-7db2-9c78-59b7ccfc5c67","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788643930.009396,"turn_id":"01a0737c-6eda-7ef0-b918-3fd62ff6eedf"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"not really sure `mise history` makes sense, users would expect it to apply to things like `mise install` and `mise set`\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0737d-2404-7f53-b5b2-ce691eff326e","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788643976.196335,"turn_id":"01a0737d-2348-78f1-af8c-de230abba0f0"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"i also wonder if we should remove the journaling and stuff for non-file cahnges\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0737d-99ac-7600-8238-70e1b92f03a9","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788644006.316862,"turn_id":"01a0737d-98f2-77b2-aec3-5df661c0d2c9"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"in that case I think it should all live inside `mise bootstrap` commands too\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0737e-4869-73f1-b3a7-805b2e8ce6c2","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788644051.049767,"turn_id":"01a0737e-479d-7d91-8c4b-9744b093f8bf"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"give me some instructions for the agent about this and the mise ssh stuff since it's in progress\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0737e-ac5a-7381-b563-806d9946f3ef","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788644076.634472,"turn_id":"01a0737e-ab89-7703-852e-bb746aaf852e"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"also edit the blog post to reflect this\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0737f-764f-7fc0-8872-ae402cbf7c78","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788644128.335156,"turn_id":"01a0737f-758c-7303-b0c9-9ac7f8e975b7"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"## 5. Make background problems visible\n\nhow on earth are we going to achieve this?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07380-2d4d-7952-a113-2e16850f7f57","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788644175.181632,"turn_id":"01a07380-2c90-76d0-be86-b438c1261a9e"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"we could use mise doctor\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07380-8245-7781-8c49-ed7ecae219c1","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788644196.933533,"turn_id":"01a07380-817a-7b90-af6a-34fcce586871"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"ok give me the full instructions again\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07380-b864-7951-bca3-aa19cd1ec001","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788644210.788339,"turn_id":"01a07380-b791-71a2-83ed-8ee57a284a28"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Omarchy was a big motivation for this. I want users to keep editing their native config files, including having agents edit them, and have mise take care of the history and setup. You shouldn’t need to translate your configuration into another language to get that. This doesn’t automatically merge your customizations with distro updates though, or replace an OS snapshot.\n\n- remove that paragraph\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07387-6158-7343-ab81-76f1b5b86b8a","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788644647.256247,"turn_id":"01a07387-5f77-7743-b919-562cca7273b1"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"You can also select platform-specific variants. - we should show an example of this\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07387-f4ef-7500-8697-8f60102eeec5","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788644685.039369,"turn_id":"01a07387-f429-78d1-8664-59cf1e17d782"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"These work together. The template source travels with the shared setup, and bootstrap renders it using the destination machine’s inputs. The generated `.gitconfig` isn’t shared as though it were the source.\n\nIf you edit the generated file, local history can help recover that edit, but the next render may replace it. mise doesn’t pretend it can reverse a rendered file back into a template.\n\nExisting symlink, copy, and managed-edit workflows still work. You don’t have to migrate a setup you’re happy with. There are also recipes for mixed-platform setups, templates, and moving an existing dotfiles repository to tracking.\n\n- that section is confusing to me\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07388-6f93-70b3-85b7-5eec9664a7ee","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788644716.435732,"turn_id":"01a07388-6ed0-7e42-8a09-237a1f8c1fd6"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"ok do that, then give the entire post a read through and make improvmeent suggestions\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07388-e96d-7573-aed4-dfe1fa1b6f98","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788644747.629587,"turn_id":"01a07388-e8b4-7ce2-8345-dbf484d062a1"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"do all of that\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07389-f0b6-7ad1-a9e5-72d9e5025dd5","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788644815.030702,"turn_id":"01a07389-efeb-74d0-b6b0-dcef9389f100"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"background history service ? remove that\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0738c-6062-71c2-939e-be54dcc65df8","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788644974.690759,"turn_id":"01a0738c-5faa-7b73-ab44-6d93a9f5ab50"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"This is file history, not a record of everything mise does. Bootstrap saves configuration before and after changing it, but restoring those files doesn’t reverse package installations or service state changes. Rolling back an `ffmpeg` declaration, for example, leaves the installed package alone.\n\n- no user would assume this\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0738d-e3a9-7b73-9778-e3dbf42b2a1f","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788645073.833476,"turn_id":"01a0738d-e2da-7bb2-ab0b-e929cc75866d"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"maybe show the example config for configuring it with claude\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0738e-5d9c-7453-8e9c-39e3dc2d5e1e","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788645105.05287,"turn_id":"01a0738e-5ce3-7082-8ce1-468c17181e11"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"i think we should have a setting for optional desktop notifications on sync conflicts\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0738f-26c2-7b01-9970-17e8fe3cfe2c","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788645156.546013,"turn_id":"01a0738f-25fc-79b1-b737-08d74520d422"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"add that to the blog, just a brief mention\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0738f-7aba-74e3-8645-37fe7d2735f9","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788645178.04221,"turn_id":"01a0738f-7a01-73a0-8185-1297a2de28b6"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"review the pr stack\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07400-afd5-7ec2-828e-f320ccbcf4bd","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788652597.205801,"turn_id":"01a07400-aea6-70a1-b6b4-e8a3bf84b419"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"post comments\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07402-1594-75d0-a304-f67339be4807","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788652688.788864,"turn_id":"01a07402-14ca-7311-a89c-d39b9c2488bd"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"post the comments, including the gaps and alignment issues, why can't you use gh?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07402-d91a-7483-8e2d-cf9a836faa15","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788652738.842006,"turn_id":"01a07402-d852-7043-8d30-4870c1fd9dca"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"were those the only ones that needed comments?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07405-8594-7531-bbbd-9a18551bc088","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788652914.06827,"turn_id":"01a07405-84c6-7ac3-ab1a-d4bd9c5a8162"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"ok look at all of the prs\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07405-f418-7320-a0b8-cc9a2d132521","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788652942.360742,"turn_id":"01a07405-f35d-7971-a9c9-4b757f525c8d"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"post comments\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0740a-f88b-7df1-b91b-56a2f910c3e5","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788653271.179205,"turn_id":"01a0740a-f7bb-7c01-a437-621f4990b698"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"how is the stack looking now?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a074e9-f23c-7e41-9a86-ab42df6d83c3","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788667884.092092,"turn_id":"01a074e9-f029-7b41-9d5b-15393cc141f0"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"post comments\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a074ec-fc37-74c0-b8df-4d09b70172d5","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788668083.255237,"turn_id":"01a074ec-fb3a-7160-80ce-1cf4ecb0fe8d"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"give it another pass, post comments\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07545-ab74-7600-aeb9-346c3511708b","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788673895.284972,"turn_id":"01a07545-a993-7112-87cf-c926ae1d5134"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"are there gaps?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07548-8155-7210-8ff5-0d860b19e3a8","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788674081.109154,"turn_id":"01a07548-8077-7b63-a03f-e67f62bdd635"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"ok give me instructions for the agent to close the gaps\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07549-99af-79b3-b211-7642ae906637","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788674152.879463,"turn_id":"01a07549-98e7-7e42-b49a-91290a55dcd5"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"how does the stack look now?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07744-b60c-7252-a02c-c727bdb84656","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788707386.892536,"turn_id":"01a07744-b3c8-76e0-81d3-d3d855644ad2"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"the goal was to have it sync individual files even if some were conflicted, right?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07748-5782-7853-a8cd-174e7f0a7cff","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788707624.834069,"turn_id":"01a07748-56c2-7e20-b4ff-4dff4121c93a"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"i'm wondering if we should keep that or have everything move atomically\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07748-c321-7241-80a2-db0910994ff8","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788707652.385885,"turn_id":"01a07748-c24c-72c1-ad65-85ec7f832d0e"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"i kind of think \"sync stopped working\" is good since without the notifications users won't know they have conflicts and they're not going to bother with atomic groups\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07749-753f-7742-a6f0-c1bcd9838436","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788707697.983667,"turn_id":"01a07749-747e-7080-a0e4-96ca8555a97b"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"i think we should turn on notifications by default. if we get rid of partial sync does that make things simpler or harder?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0774a-485a-7c71-8195-7d2fdd590023","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788707752.026807,"turn_id":"01a0774a-478b-7463-8a04-94897d52f450"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"ok\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0774a-fe9c-7d01-8068-b5033e0ee88d","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788707798.684623,"turn_id":"01a0774a-fdcb-7792-8eb7-c035721bc8dc"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"ok i want you to take over working on these PRs now. \n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0774b-677c-7bf2-9038-a448cc9a6cfd","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788707825.532997,"turn_id":"01a0774b-66c1-7263-b21d-973c6f6715af"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"PLEASE IMPLEMENT THIS PLAN:\n# Take over the dotfiles PR stack\n\n## Summary\n\nTake exclusive ownership of the six existing PRs, finish implementation and review fixes, and deliver a verified, merge-ready stack. The other agent stops pushing. Do not merge the PRs.\n\nPreserve the established design: native-file editing, mise-owned history, bootstrap onboarding, platform variants, and template compatibility.\n\n## Implementation\n\n### Whole-setup conflict handling\n\nReplace partial synchronization with this contract:\n\n- Any unresolved conflict pauses shared-branch publication and incoming application for the entire setup.\n- Local checkpoints, fetching, and eligible machine-recovery backups continue.\n- Compute conflicts across the complete eligible setup before publishing or writing incoming files.\n- Keep per-file resolution choices, but record those choices without partially applying the setup. Resume only after all conflicts are resolved.\n- Recompute and validate the plan against current local and remote versions before resuming. A later edit invalidates any resolution based on an older version.\n- Preserve explicit path selection for local rollback; it must not become a way to bypass the global sharing pause.\n\nPreflight the complete incoming batch, including newly declared files and required template sources. Keep protective checkpoints and journaling for mid-write failures. Attempt safe recovery without overwriting concurrent user edits; persist an actionable failure if recovery cannot complete. Describe this as all-or-nothing application, not truly atomic filesystem writes.\n\n### Notifications and health\n\n- Default `history.notify` to true; preserve explicit false settings.\n- Notify once when sharing enters a conflict-paused state. Retries remain silent.\n- Reset notification deduplication after recovery so a later conflict can notify again.\n- Notification failures and headless environments never block operations.\n- Status and doctor report the whole-setup pause, conflicting paths, resolution commands, and last successful application. Local-history and backup health remain distinct.\n\n### Existing review findings\n\nReverify every outstanding finding against current heads; fix valid issues in the earliest owning PR and propagate changes upward.\n\nPrioritize stale pull plans overwriting newer saved edits, missing synchronization baselines for initially identical files, declined-origin state leakage, portable config-directory mapping, and permission-metadata consistency. Validate automated findings rather than accepting them solely because a review tool reported them.\n\nKeep encrypted backups explicitly deferred and rejected when requested; do not silently upload plaintext instead.\n\n## Stack and documentation\n\nMaintain the existing PR order:\n\n1. #12849 — foundation\n2. #12835 — rollback\n3. #12838 — services\n4. #12843 — watcher\n5. #12865 — origin/sync\n6. #12882 — automatic sync/onboarding\n\nUse isolated working branches, preserve unrelated work, and update the existing PR branches. Restack with expected-head checks and force-with-lease only where rebasing requires it; stop if unexpected pushes appear.\n\nUpdate CLI help, generated docs, setup examples, PR descriptions, and the existing blog PR to reflect whole-setup pauses and default-on notifications. Remove claims that unrelated files continue syncing during conflicts. Keep unsupported encryption clearly distinguished from shipped functionality and always lowercase “mise.”\n\n## Verification and completion\n\nAdd or update tests for:\n\n- One conflicted file blocking all shared publication and incoming application while local history, fetching, and eligible backups continue.\n- Resolving multiple conflicts incrementally without partially applying changes.\n- Saved or unsaved edits made after planning never being overwritten by a stale pull.\n- Initially identical machines establishing a baseline and correctly handling subsequent edits.\n- Configuration and referenced sources being validated before application.\n- Mid-application failure, recovery, and concurrent edits.\n- Notification defaults, explicit opt-out, deduplication, and unavailable notifiers.\n- Permission-only changes, selective-save permission isolation, and excluded throttled files.\n- Existing two-machine sync, offline recovery, rollback propagation, platform variants, templates, and local/remote bootstrap workflows.\n\nRun focused tests during development, repository-required lint/render checks before commits, and CI on every updated PR head. Inspect substantive failure logs rather than relying on aggregate status.\n\nReply to review threads with the fix and verification evidence; resolve only findings actually addressed or demonstrated invalid. Finish with all six PRs ready for review, a concise validation report, and explicit remaining limitations. Do not merge.\n\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0774e-73de-7f93-9ac2-a0803ed77c35","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788708025.310217,"turn_id":"01a0774e-7311-7183-bbe0-5d429a7b050b"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"use a worktree\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07767-6118-7bd3-b717-4e6e85624eef","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788709658.904918,"turn_id":"01a07767-5e9f-7c30-a85f-2921c05466b6"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"ok is there feedback on the prs that needs to be addressed?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0776b-d93f-7d41-9f8e-504e2242078c","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788709951.807571,"turn_id":"01a0776b-d6fe-7063-9528-850ccb33e91d"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"ok reply to false positives and address feedback, keep checking for feedback, failing checks, and merge conflicts until the entire stack settles\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0776f-8da7-74d3-b917-ecf1c7382bd6","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788710194.599302,"turn_id":"01a0776f-8b86-7383-9180-4a703e38ce72"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"how does the stack and blog post compare now?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a077bb-abe7-74c3-89b8-92056406f170","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788715183.079516,"turn_id":"01a077bb-ab0c-7253-9092-b7dbbd644e8d"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"\n# Response annotations:\nEach item contains text selected from an earlier Codex response and may include a user comment. Treat items as Annotation 1, Annotation 2, and so on in array order. Use every selection as context and address every comment. For every annotation you address, include its inline directive `:codex-annotation{index=\"N\"}`, where N is its one-based array position (for example, `:codex-annotation{index=\"1\"}`). Do not use unstructured annotation labels.\n\u003cresponse-annotations\u003e\n[{\"text\":\"The blog promises executable configuration requires trust approval. That isn’t fully enforced for synchronized history.describe_command settings. This needs an implementation fix, not just softer wording.\",\"source\":{\"messageId\":\"msg_0749c281f1ff31a6016a9da1059b0487d08d2ef7615898cbcd\",\"startOffset\":564,\"endOffset\":768}}]\n\u003c/response-annotations\u003e\n\n## My request:\nwhat is this?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a077be-f5a1-71a1-8df1-09dbb6642280","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788715398.561845,"turn_id":"01a077be-f4a5-7821-9ab6-b14af70720cd"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"is this the claude -p thing?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a077bf-cc83-76f1-8526-2281e4a48883","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788715453.571856,"turn_id":"01a077bf-cb59-7973-a88e-65cb000207e9"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"is it an escaping problem potentially?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a077c0-1702-7192-9c2d-7d647bd679bf","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788715472.642674,"turn_id":"01a077c0-1625-7b33-8ede-bac0f178de43"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"ok what should we do?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a077c0-7063-77a0-8fd6-07e5afc55497","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788715495.523049,"turn_id":"01a077c0-6f87-7ab0-b43b-ae4da2f7b2ed"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"ok add that to one of the prs in the stack\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a077c1-4815-7332-be85-9750128b9011","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788715550.741295,"turn_id":"01a077c1-472f-7723-ad8b-101b0407bd85"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"well is this necessary? if a user is adding a dotfile repo they will be able to get shell execution for sure\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a077c1-de12-7b73-b385-8365376637f1","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788715589.138385,"turn_id":"01a077c1-dd3d-7a73-ba44-2d9518554995"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"i don't even think it deserves disclosure\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a077c2-9018-7bc3-bfbc-10703128b387","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788715634.712651,"turn_id":"01a077c2-8f37-78d3-a848-062401fb8a21"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"ok fix the blog post, then let's talk about remote preview, what is it?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a077c3-084e-7b52-aa78-c9f97c42b651","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788715665.486939,"turn_id":"01a077c3-077d-7841-9dc8-8aa22ff57ec9"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"explain the other discrepencies and give a proposed solution\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a077c4-9f31-7412-92ee-9955fe6a0a9c","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788715769.649301,"turn_id":"01a077c4-9e67-7912-a253-e8ba2cbac16a"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"how is the stack looking now?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07812-c2de-7160-954e-7f53482267a9","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788720890.590896,"turn_id":"01a07812-c0c9-7891-ab33-63f7eadd24a0"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"an agent is working on the encryption pr\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07816-138c-7551-8d60-b8a8db2b3631","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788721107.852073,"turn_id":"01a07816-12ae-7601-bfbc-cb79ce8a673b"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"no\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07817-59e8-7960-a2d1-cd1b85cb0c54","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788721191.400247,"turn_id":"01a07817-58e5-7452-b9ae-1628c2fc7906"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"what about from a product perspective or tricky parts of this, do you have any questions for me?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07817-b91d-7e20-ad4b-56c8e9e80bcb","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788721215.773665,"turn_id":"01a07817-b848-7bc1-a012-f7761e8dd7a5"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"prompt me for these questions and fix the PRs, leave the encryption one alone \n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07819-ad1a-7f33-b25e-f3ded8a99f95","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788721343.770661,"turn_id":"01a07819-ac32-7782-a512-d3e0a35b975e"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"because this is getting quite complex, tracking should be marked experimental, modify these existing prs to mark it as experimental and gate on ensure_experimental\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0781f-2c0f-70d0-ab95-2df647465a1c","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788721703.951924,"turn_id":"01a0781f-2b35-7f13-bd06-952e347cb9f8"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"be careful with backwards compatibility, this stack will be merged together in the same release and it's been a few days since the last release. be careful no backwards compatibility cruft is in this stack that isn't necessary since no users have this functionality and it will all be shipped together\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07821-e758-7e53-ab6c-912476eecc7f","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788721882.968288,"turn_id":"01a07821-e65a-79d3-8b70-98cd2b81d2d4"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"yes\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07822-24ab-7833-8602-5037b868e853","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788721898.667108,"turn_id":"01a07822-23d7-7c00-a58f-bd5acb0fe8dd"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"start building bro\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07822-8548-7572-99cf-678441d19269","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788721923.400499,"turn_id":"01a07822-8479-7bf3-a8d6-b1867ce0cd21"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"ok go\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07822-a4b6-7c33-b40d-8c77c0cb3567","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788721931.446517,"turn_id":"01a07822-a3e5-7e32-8185-08e4b3a34f79"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"what are the blog changes?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07846-621c-76c2-b3bb-60e76aab355c","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788724273.692029,"turn_id":"01a07846-5f24-7332-8e00-f405474b90b6"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"what did the other agent change?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07849-be29-72d0-a6d1-80a0905ddc84","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788724493.865263,"turn_id":"01a07849-bce5-7283-9aba-92d2015f49e5"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"push all the changes including yours\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0784a-f938-78c3-ac69-be0d9797d083","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788724574.520426,"turn_id":"01a0784a-f83d-7222-8d22-de51d10941f7"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"is there feedback on the stack?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0784c-5f1e-7bd2-9b4f-a9a0009f9809","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788724666.142911,"turn_id":"01a0784c-5dbd-7f21-b728-2d01af15e259"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"keep addressing feebdack until it settles, feel free to stop if you need clarification\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0784e-24d2-7bb2-8560-d5eae3ac2437","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788724782.290289,"turn_id":"01a0784e-23cc-77b0-8fe9-f11e1d8bfc76"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"can you add the mise logo to the notifications?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0785f-465f-7310-a873-7ace0e970def","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788725904.991502,"turn_id":"01a0784e-23cc-77b0-8fe9-f11e1d8bfc76"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"keep addressing feebdack until it settles, feel free to stop if you need clarification\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0786f-dbaa-7d32-9396-b6987365648d","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788726991.786784,"turn_id":"01a0786f-d930-7653-bdfb-564e4c117620"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"\u003csend_user_message_question_reply\u003e\n[{\"questionItemId\":\"[\\\"request_user_input_async\\\",\\\"call_pFiietOWwlygFSZGWAfdn2DZ\\\",0]\",\"question\":\"One rollback edge case needs a product call: Git snapshots don’t record empty directories. Should rolling back a parent directory leave unrecorded empty folders alone, or should we extend history to track empty directories too? I recommend leaving them alone; explicitly selected empty folders can still be removed and restored with undo.\",\"answer\":\"Leave unrecorded empty folders alone\"}]\n\u003c/send_user_message_question_reply\u003e","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07882-4b8a-7412-ac9e-67ec16c0a066","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788728200.074042,"turn_id":"01a0786f-d930-7653-bdfb-564e4c117620"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"\u003capp-context\u003e\n# Codex desktop context\n- You are running inside the Codex (desktop) app, which allows some additional features not available in the CLI alone:\n\n### Images/Visuals/Files\n- In the app, the model can display images, videos, and audio using standard Markdown image syntax: ![alt](url)\n- When an app or connector generates or edits media, prefer native media already displayed inline or a local output file already returned by the tool. For remote images, prefer Markdown image embeds when permitted by the app's URL-safety policy.\n- For media that cannot be displayed directly, including remote video and audio, use the app's preview or display tool when available. Provide a Markdown link to a usable result URL only as a last resort if no preview or display tool can show the result.\n- Do not download remote media to work around display restrictions.\n- When sending or referencing a local image, video, or audio file, always use an absolute filesystem path in the Markdown image tag (e.g., ![alt](/absolute/path.png)); relative paths and plain text will not render the media.\n- When a user asks to play an audio file, render it using Markdown image syntax with an absolute path (e.g., ![audio](/absolute/path.mp3)).\n- When referencing code or workspace files in responses, always use full absolute file paths instead of relative paths.\n- If a user asks about an image, or asks you to create an image, it is often a good idea to show the image to them in your response.\n- Return web URLs as Markdown links (e.g., [label](https://example.com)).\n\n### Pull request diff links\nWhen referencing code from a GitHub PR, you can link directly to its diff in the app using:\n[label](codex://review?pr=PR_URL\u0026path=FILE_PATH\u0026line=LINE\u0026side=right)\nURL-encode PR_URL and the repository-relative FILE_PATH. Use a verified one-based LINE from the current PR diff. Use side=left for the original code or side=right for the updated code. Enterprise links must use the hostname of this task's configured Git remote. Use ordinary file links for workspace code.\n\n### Workspace Dependencies\n- For sheets, slides, and documents, call `load_workspace_dependencies` to find the bundled runtime and libraries.\n\n### Automations\n- This app supports recurring automations, reminders, monitors, follow-ups, and thread wakeups. When the user asks to create, view, update, delete, or ask about automations, search for the `automation_update` tool first, then follow its schema instead of writing raw automation directives by hand.\n- For heartbeat monitors, preserve the user's notification intent in the saved prompt. Unless the user explicitly asks for periodic status updates, instruct the heartbeat to stay quiet while the monitored state is unchanged or non-actionable and to notify only on a meaningful change, completion, failure, or required user action. Do not add instructions such as \"leave a brief status update\" on every run.\n- When an automation should archive a Codex thread on completion, use `set_thread_archived` instead of emitting raw archive directives.\n\n### Thread Coordination\n- Treat the terms \"task\", \"thread\", \"chat\", and \"conversation\" as synonyms when they clearly refer to Codex. Tool names use the term \"thread\" and Codex uses \"task\" in the UI. When providing user-facing responses, use \"task\".\n- When the user asks to create, fork, inspect, continue, hand off, pin, archive, unarchive, rename, or otherwise manage Codex threads, search for the relevant thread tool first: `create_thread`, `fork_thread`, `list_threads`, `list_archived_threads`, `read_thread`, `wait_threads`, `send_message_to_thread`, `handoff_thread`, `set_thread_pinned`, `set_thread_archived`, or `set_thread_title`.\n- When following another task's progress, prefer compact `wait_threads` snapshots over repeated `read_thread` calls. Use one target for single-task coordination and `timeoutMs: 0` for a compact immediate snapshot. `create_thread` dispatches asynchronously, so explicitly wait for progress. Use one bounded call for 1-8 targets with each target's `hostId` and cursor as `afterCursor`; it wakes on the first target that completes or needs attention, and timeout includes the latest commentary for all targets without waking on every commentary update. An up-to-date cursor suppresses already-delivered final text. Separate waits from one task may run serially. Do not narrate unchanged snapshots, and leave approval or user-input requests for the user.\n- Only use `create_thread` when the user explicitly asks to create a new thread. Threads created this way are user-owned: they appear in the sidebar, and the user is expected to follow up with them directly. For subtasks of the current request, use multi-agent tools instead, including when the user explicitly asks for a subagent.\n- After a successful `create_thread` call, emit `::created-thread{threadId=\"...\"}` for a created thread or `::created-thread{clientThreadId=\"...\"}` for queued worktree setup on its own line in your final response.\n\n### Inline Code Comments\n- Use the ::code-comment{...} directive when you need to attach feedback directly to specific code lines.\n- Emit one directive per inline comment; emit none when there are no actionable inline comments.\n- Required attributes: title (short label), body (one-paragraph explanation), file (path to the file).\n- Optional attributes: start, end (1-based line numbers), priority (0-3).\n- file should be an absolute path or include the workspace folder segment so it can be resolved relative to the workspace.\n- Keep line ranges tight; end defaults to start.\n- Example: ::code-comment{title=\"[P2] Off-by-one\" body=\"Loop iterates past the end when length is 0.\" file=\"/path/to/foo.ts\" start=10 end=11 priority=2}\n\n### Git\n- Branch prefix: `codex/`. Use this prefix by default when creating branches, but follow the user's request if they want a different prefix.\n\u003c/app-context\u003e","type":"input_text"},{"text":"## Memory\n\nYou have access to a memory folder with guidance from prior runs. It can save\ntime and help you stay consistent. Use it whenever it is likely to help.\n\nDecision boundary: should you use memory for a new user query?\n\n- Skip memory ONLY when the request is clearly self-contained and does not need\n  workspace history, conventions, or prior decisions.\n- Hard skip examples: current time/date, simple translation, simple sentence\n  rewrite, one-line shell command, trivial formatting.\n- Use memory by default when ANY of these are true:\n  - the query mentions workspace/repo/module/path/files in MEMORY_SUMMARY below,\n  - the user asks for prior context / consistency / previous decisions,\n  - the task is ambiguous and could depend on earlier project choices,\n  - the ask is a non-trivial and related to MEMORY_SUMMARY below.\n- If unsure, do a quick memory pass.\n\nMemory layout (general -\u003e specific):\n\n- /Users/jdx/.codex/memories/memory_summary.md (already provided below; do NOT open again)\n- /Users/jdx/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md (searchable registry; primary file to query)\n- /Users/jdx/.codex/memories/skills/\u003cskill-name\u003e/ (skill folder)\n  - SKILL.md (entrypoint instructions)\n  - scripts/ (optional helper scripts)\n  - examples/ (optional example outputs)\n  - templates/ (optional templates)\n- /Users/jdx/.codex/memories/rollout_summaries/ (per-rollout recaps + evidence snippets)\n  - The paths of these entries can be found in /Users/jdx/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md or /Users/jdx/.codex/memories/rollout_summaries/ as `rollout_path`\n  - These files are append-only `jsonl`: `session_meta.payload.id` identifies the session, `turn_context` marks turn boundaries, `event_msg` is the lightweight status stream, and `response_item` contains actual messages, tool calls, and tool outputs.\n  - For efficient lookup, prefer matching the filename suffix or `session_meta.payload.id`; avoid broad full-content scans unless needed.\n\nQuick memory pass (when applicable):\n\n1. Skim the MEMORY_SUMMARY below and extract task-relevant keywords.\n2. Search /Users/jdx/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md using those keywords.\n3. Only if MEMORY.md directly points to rollout summaries/skills, open the 1-2\n   most relevant files under /Users/jdx/.codex/memories/rollout_summaries/ or\n   /Users/jdx/.codex/memories/skills/.\n4. If above are not clear and you need exact commands, error text, or precise evidence, search over `rollout_path` for more evidence.\n5. If there are no relevant hits, stop memory lookup and continue normally.\n\nQuick-pass budget:\n\n- Keep memory lookup lightweight: ideally \u003c= 4-6 search steps before main work.\n- Avoid broad scans of all rollout summaries.\n\nDuring execution: if you hit repeated errors, confusing behavior, or suspect\nrelevant prior context, redo the quick memory pass.\n\nHow to decide whether to verify memory:\n\n- Consider both risk of drift and verification effort.\n- If a fact is likely to drift and is cheap to verify, verify it before\n  answering.\n- If a fact is likely to drift but verification is expensive, slow, or\n  disruptive, it is acceptable to answer from memory in an interactive turn,\n  but you should say that it is memory-derived, note that it may be stale, and\n  consider offering to refresh it live.\n- If a fact is lower-drift and expensive to verify, it is usually fine to\n  answer from memory directly.\n\nWhen answering from memory without current verification:\n\n- If you rely on memory for a fact that you did not verify in the current turn,\n  say so briefly in the final answer.\n- If that fact is plausibly drift-prone or comes from an older note, older\n  snapshot, or prior run summary, say that it may be stale or outdated.\n- If live verification was skipped and a refresh would be useful in the\n  interactive context, consider offering to verify or refresh it live.\n- Do not present unverified memory-derived facts as confirmed-current.\n- Prefer a short refresh offer for interactive questions, especially about prior\n  results, commands, timing, or older snapshots.\n\nMemory citation requirements:\n\n- If ANY relevant memory files were used: append exactly one\n`\u003coai-mem-citation\u003e` block as the VERY LAST content of the final reply.\n  Normal responses should include the answer first, then append the\n`\u003coai-mem-citation\u003e` block at the end.\n- Use this exact structure for programmatic parsing:\n```\n\u003coai-mem-citation\u003e\n\u003ccitation_entries\u003e\nMEMORY.md:234-236|note=[responsesapi citation extraction code pointer]\nrollout_summaries/2026-02-17T21-23-02-LN3m-example.md:10-12|note=[weekly report format]\n\u003c/citation_entries\u003e\n\u003crollout_ids\u003e\n019c6e27-e55b-73d1-87d8-4e01f1f75043\n019c7714-3b77-74d1-9866-e1f484aae2ab\n\u003c/rollout_ids\u003e\n\u003c/oai-mem-citation\u003e\n```\n- `citation_entries` is for rendering:\n  - one citation entry per line\n  - format: `\u003cfile\u003e:\u003cline_start\u003e-\u003cline_end\u003e|note=[\u003chow memory was used\u003e]`\n  - use file paths relative to the memory base path (for example, `MEMORY.md`,\n    `rollout_summaries/...`, `skills/...`)\n  - only cite files actually used under the memory base path (do not cite\n    workspace files as memory citations)\n  - if you used `MEMORY.md` and then a rollout summary/skill file, cite both\n  - list entries in order of importance (most important first)\n  - `note` should be short, single-line, and use simple characters only (avoid\n    unusual symbols, no newlines)\n- `rollout_ids` is for us to track what previous rollouts you find useful:\n  - include one rollout id per line\n  - rollout ids should look like UUIDs (for example,\n    `019c6e27-e55b-73d1-87d8-4e01f1f75043`)\n  - include unique ids only; do not repeat ids\n  - an empty `\u003crollout_ids\u003e` section is allowed if no rollout ids are available\n  - you can find rollout ids in rollout summary files and MEMORY.md\n  - do not include file paths or notes in this section\n  - For every `citation_entries`, try to find and cite the corresponding rollout id if possible\n- Never include memory citations inside pull-request messages.\n- Never cite blank lines; double-check ranges.\n\nUpdating memories:\n\nYou can update the memories **only** when explicitly asked by the user. This must always come from a direct request from the user.\n- Write your update in /Users/jdx/.codex/memories/extensions/ad_hoc/notes/\n- Each update must be one small file containing what you want to add/delete/update from the memories.\n- The name of this file must be `\u003ctimestamp\u003e-\u003cshort slug\u003e.md`\n- Do not try to edit the memory files yourself, only add one update note in /Users/jdx/.codex/memories/extensions/ad_hoc/notes/\n\n========= MEMORY_SUMMARY BEGINS =========\nv1\n\n## User Profile\n\nWorks in `/Users/jdx/src/communique` and investigates GitHub Actions/release automation failures. In CI diagnosis, they value a concrete causal explanation from the detailed job logs.\n\n## User preferences\n\n- For “why did this fail” CI questions with a job URL, inspect the detailed/raw job log and report the first substantive nested error, not merely `Process completed with exit code 1`.\n\n## General Tips\n\n- For release-plz failures, trace `release-plz release-pr` through `cargo package` and any historical checkout before blaming the visible changed file.\n- A mise Cargo wrapper can fail before `MBX_DISABLE=1` reaches mbx; check wrapper executable/version resolution first.\n- In `/Users/jdx/src/communique`, use `gh run view \u003crun\u003e --job \u003cjob\u003e --log` for the actionable error chain; see `MEMORY.md` before proposing a fix.\n\n## What's in Memory\n\n### /Users/jdx/src/communique\n\n#### 2026-09-05\n\n- Diagnose Release-plz PR failure: GitHub Actions, release-plz, cargo package, mise, mr-boxington, MBX_DISABLE\n  - desc: Search first for failures in the communique `Release-plz PR` workflow, especially historical checkouts with a Cargo wrapper.\n  - learnings: The historical `mbx` executable can be unavailable before mbx reads `MBX_DISABLE`; raw job logs expose the decisive nested error.\n\n### Older Memory Topics\n========= MEMORY_SUMMARY ENDS =========\n\nWhen memory is likely relevant, start with the quick memory pass above before\ndeep repo exploration.\n","type":"input_text"},{"text":"\u003cskills_instructions\u003e\n## Skills\nA skill is a set of local instructions to follow that is stored in a `SKILL.md` file. Below is the list of skills that can be used. Each entry includes a name, description, and a short path that can be expanded into an absolute path using the skill roots table.\n### Skill roots\n- `r0` = `/Users/jdx/.codex/skills/.system`\n- `r1` = `/Users/jdx/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-bundled/sites/0.1.57/skills`\n- `r2` = `/Users/jdx/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-bundled`\n- `r3` = `/Users/jdx/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/cloudflare/0.1.2/skills`\n- `r4` = `/Users/jdx/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote`\n- `r5` = `/Users/jdx/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/google-drive/0.1.16/skills`\n- `r6` = `/Users/jdx/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-primary-runtime`\n- `r7` = `/Users/jdx/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-primary-runtime/spreadsheets/26.904.11930/skills`\n- `r8` = `/Users/jdx/.codex/worktrees/141d/mise/.agents/skills`\n### Available skills\n- imagegen: Generate or edit raster images when the task benefits from AI-created bitmap visuals such as photos, illustrations, textures, sprites, mockups, or transparent-background cutouts. Use when Codex should create a brand-new image, transform an existing image, or derive visual variants from references, and the output should be a bitmap asset rather than repo-native code or vector. Do not use when the task is better handled by editing existing SVG/vector/code-native assets, extending an established icon or logo system, or building the visual directly in HTML/CSS/canvas. (file: r0/imagegen/SKILL.md)\n- openai-docs: Use for Codex models/pricing, scheduled tasks, skills, settings, setup, troubleshooting, customization, automations, and self-knowledge—including 'you,' 'your,' 'this app,' or 'this coding agent' when they refer to Codex—and for OpenAI APIs/products and ChatGPT Work. Also use for model choice/migration, prompting, SDKs, Responses, Realtime, agents, evals, and Chat/Work/Codex comparisons. Do not use for generic app/software tasks that merely mention Codex. (file: r0/openai-docs/SKILL.md)\n- plugin-creator: Create and scaffold plugin directories for Codex with a required `.codex-plugin/plugin.json`, optional plugin folders/files, valid manifest defaults, and personal-marketplace entries by default. Use when Codex needs to create a new personal plugin, add optional plugin structure, generate or update marketplace entries for plugin ordering and availability metadata, or update an existing local plugin during development with the CLI-driven cachebuster and reinstall flow. (file: r0/plugin-creator/SKILL.md)\n- skill-creator: Create or update a Codex skill with appropriately scoped instructions and any needed supporting resources. (file: r0/skill-creator/SKILL.md)\n- skill-installer: Install Codex skills into $CODEX_HOME/skills from a curated list or a GitHub repo path. Use when a user asks to list installable skills, install a curated skill, or install a skill from another repo (including private repos). (file: r0/skill-installer/SKILL.md)\n- entire-search: Search Entire checkpoint history and transcripts with `entire search --json`. Use proactively when the user asks about previous work, commits, sessions, prompts, or historical context in this repository. (file: r8/entire-search/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare:agents-sdk: Build AI agents on Cloudflare Workers using the Agents SDK. Load when creating stateful agents, durable workflows, real-time WebSocket apps, scheduled tasks, MCP servers, or chat applications. Covers Agent class, state management, callable RPC, Workflows integration, and React hooks. Biases towards retrieval from Cloudflare docs over pre-trained knowledge. (file: r3/agents-sdk/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare:building-ai-agent-on-cloudflare: Builds AI agents on Cloudflare using the Agents SDK with state management, real-time WebSockets, scheduled tasks, tool integration, and chat capabilities. Generates production-ready agent code deployed to Workers. Use when: user wants to \"build an agent\", \"AI agent\", \"chat agent\", \"stateful agent\", mentions \"Agents SDK\", needs \"real-time AI\", \"WebSocket AI\", or asks about agent \"state management\", \"scheduled tasks\", or \"tool calling\". Biases towards retrieval from Cloudflare docs over pre-trained knowledge. (file: r3/building-ai-agent-on-cloudflare/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare:building-mcp-server-on-cloudflare: Builds remote MCP (Model Context Protocol) servers on Cloudflare Workers with tools, OAuth authentication, and production deployment. Generates server code, configures auth providers, and deploys to Workers. Use when: user wants to \"build MCP server\", \"create MCP tools\", \"remote MCP\", \"deploy MCP\", add \"OAuth to MCP\", or mentions Model Context Protocol on Cloudflare. Also triggers on \"MCP authentication\" or \"MCP deployment\". Biases towards retrieval from Cloudflare docs over pre-trained knowledge. (file: r3/building-mcp-server-on-cloudflare/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare:cloudflare: Comprehensive Cloudflare platform skill covering Workers, Pages, storage (KV, D1, R2), AI (Workers AI, Vectorize, Agents SDK), networking (Tunnel, Spectrum), security (WAF, DDoS), and infrastructure-as-code (Terraform, Pulumi). Use for any Cloudflare development task. Biases towards retrieval from Cloudflare docs over pre-trained knowledge. (file: r3/cloudflare/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare:durable-objects: Create and review Cloudflare Durable Objects. Use when building stateful coordination (chat rooms, multiplayer games, booking systems), implementing RPC methods, SQLite storage, alarms, WebSockets, or reviewing DO code for best practices. Covers Workers integration, wrangler config, and testing with Vitest. Biases towards retrieval from Cloudflare docs over pre-trained knowledge. (file: r3/durable-objects/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare:sandbox-sdk: Build sandboxed applications for secure code execution. Load when building AI code execution, code interpreters, CI/CD systems, interactive dev environments, or executing untrusted code. Covers Sandbox SDK lifecycle, commands, files, code interpreter, and preview URLs. Biases towards retrieval from Cloudflare docs over pre-trained knowledge. (file: r3/sandbox-sdk/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare:web-perf: Analyzes web performance using Chrome DevTools MCP. Measures Core Web Vitals (FCP, LCP, TBT, CLS, Speed Index), identifies render-blocking resources, network dependency chains, layout shifts, caching issues, and accessibility gaps. Use when asked to audit, profile, debug, or optimize page load performance, Lighthouse scores, or site speed. Biases towards retrieval from current documentation over pre-trained knowledge. (file: r3/web-perf/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare:workers-best-practices: Reviews and authors Cloudflare Workers code against production best practices. Load when writing new Workers, reviewing Worker code, configuring wrangler.jsonc, or checking for common Workers anti-patterns (streaming, floating promises, global state, secrets, bindings, observability). Biases towards retrieval from Cloudflare docs over pre-trained knowledge. (file: r3/workers-best-practices/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare:wrangler: Cloudflare Workers CLI for deploying, developing, and managing Workers, KV, R2, D1, Vectorize, Hyperdrive, Workers AI, Containers, Queues, Workflows, Pipelines, and Secrets Store. Load before running wrangler commands to ensure correct syntax and best practices. Biases towards retrieval from Cloudflare docs over pre-trained knowledge. (file: r3/wrangler/SKILL.md)\n- deep-research-work:deep-research: Conduct thorough research using authoritative sources and produce evidence-backed findings with citations. Use this skill only when the user asks specifically for Deep research, uses $deep-research, or selects Deep research in Work mode. Do not use it for ordinary research requests. (file: r4/deep-research-work/0.1.14/skills/deep-research/SKILL.md)\n- documents:documents: Create, edit, redline, and comment on `.docx`, Word, and Google Docs-targeted document artifacts inside the container, with a strict render-and-verify workflow. Use `render_docx.py` to generate page PNGs (and optional PDF) for visual QA, then iterate until layout is flawless before delivering the final document. (file: r6/documents/26.904.11930/skills/documents/SKILL.md)\n- google-drive:google-docs: Prompt- and template-complete Google Docs creation and editing with explicit-instruction-authoritative structural preservation, including semantic roles, relationships, comparison dimensions, and instructed extensions; full-topology native-copy routing; source-grounded per-tab adaptation for past/example references; style-preserving hyperlink and table edits; canonical smart-chip-first authoring for dates and relevant supported people or Google resources; a file-backed advisory trusted read before existing-document writes; automatic protected-control awareness; direct connector APIs by default; DOCX-first import only when no supplied Google Doc template/reference constrains the output; and checked-in code mode only for exact native dropdown mutation. Use when Codex must create, edit, fill, adapt, redesign, or verify Google Docs without overriding explicit user/template instructions, adding unrequested document scope, or carrying stale reference facts into a new deliverable. (file: r5/google-docs/SKILL.md)\n- google-drive:google-drive: Use connected Google Drive as the single entrypoint for Drive, Docs, Sheets, and Slides work. Use when the user wants to find, fetch, organize, share, export, copy, or delete Drive files, or summarize and edit Google Docs, Google Sheets, and Google Slides through one unified Google Drive plugin. (file: r5/google-drive/SKILL.md)\n- google-drive:google-drive-comments: Write, reply to, and resolve Google Drive comments on Docs, Sheets, Slides, and Drive files with evidence-backed location context. Use when the user asks to leave comments, review a file with comments, respond to comment threads, or resolve Drive comments. (file: r5/google-drive-comments/SKILL.md)\n- google-drive:google-sheets: Analyze and edit connected Google Sheets with range precision. Use when the user wants to create Google Sheets, find a spreadsheet, inspect tabs or ranges, search rows, plan formulas, create or repair charts, clean or restructure tables, write concise summaries, or make explicit cell-range updates. (file: r5/google-sheets/SKILL.md)\n- google-drive:google-slides: Route Google Slides authoring requests and derive a design system from a native template or reference deck. Use this skill when the user provides an existing native Google Slides deck as a template, reference, or prior-period source, or asks to edit, update, repair, restyle, or clean up an existing native Google Slides deck. Use the Presentations skill instead for net-new presentation creation when no existing native Google Slides deck must be followed. (file: r5/google-slides/SKILL.md)\n- pdf:pdf: Read, create, inspect, render, and verify PDF files where visual layout matters, including fillable AcroForms. Use Poppler rendering plus Python tools such as reportlab, pdfplumber, and pypdf for generation and extraction. (file: r6/pdf/26.904.11930/skills/pdf/SKILL.md)\n- plugin-management:plugin-management: Discover and suggest relevant plugins, inspect app permissions and dependencies, and manage plugin connections or removal. Use when the user asks about plugins or when a task would materially benefit from an external app, account, service, or data source that available tools cannot access. (file: r4/plugin-management/0.1.0/skills/plugin-management/SKILL.md)\n- presentations:Presentations: Read, create or edit PowerPoint or Google Slides decks. Use for presentation, slide deck, PowerPoint, PPT, PPTX, or Google Slides requests. (file: r6/presentations/26.904.11930/skills/presentations/SKILL.md)\n- sites:sites-building: Use Sites to build websites, including landing pages, portfolios, dashboards, portals, trackers, hubs, and internal tools. Always use Sites when the project contains `.openai/hosting.json`. (file: r1/sites-building/SKILL.md)\n- sites:sites-hosting: Host websites with Sites. Always use after `sites-building`, and use for website publishing, deployment, hosting management, or projects containing `.openai/hosting.json`. (file: r1/sites-hosting/SKILL.md)\n- spreadsheets:Spreadsheets: Create, edit, analyze, and verify standalone spreadsheet files or Google Sheets-ready workbooks, including .xlsx, .xls, .csv, and .tsv. Do not use for live controlling Microsoft Excel app or a live Excel session. (file: r7/spreadsheets/SKILL.md)\n- spreadsheets:excel-live-control: Control an open or active Microsoft Excel workbook through the ChatGPT add-in or connected session. Use when the user tags the Microsoft Excel app in Codex or follows up on an established live Excel task. Do not use for standalone spreadsheet files or Google Sheets. (file: r7/excel-live-control/SKILL.md)\n- template-creator:template-creator: Create or update a reusable personal Codex artifact-template skill. Use when the user invokes $template-creator or asks in natural language to create a reusable template from a reference document, presentation, spreadsheet, Google Docs, Slides, or Sheets link, ImageGen or Product Design image, email, Slack message, or Site project, or explicitly asks to edit or update a passed artifact-template skill. Do not use for one-off creation from an existing template. (file: r6/template-creator/26.904.11930/skills/template-creator/SKILL.md)\n- visualize:visualize: Create visualizations and interactive tools directly in conversation. Proactively use to show how something works; explore 'what happens when', 'what changes', or 'help me understand'; compare or inspect; create simulations, maps, charts, graphs, and mockups. Use standard tools for static scientific figures. (file: r2/visualize/1.0.29/skills/visualize/SKILL.md)\n\u003c/skills_instructions\u003e","type":"input_text"},{"text":"\u003cpermissions instructions\u003e\nFilesystem sandboxing defines which files can be read or written. `sandbox_mode` is `danger-full-access`: No filesystem sandboxing - all commands are permitted. Network access is enabled.\nApproval policy is currently never. Do not provide the `sandbox_permissions` for any reason, commands will be rejected.\n\u003c/permissions instructions\u003e","type":"input_text"},{"text":"\u003ccollaboration_mode\u003e# Collaboration Mode: Default\n\nYou are now in Default mode. Any previous instructions for other modes (e.g. Plan mode) are no longer active.\n\nYour active mode changes only when new developer instructions with a different `\u003ccollaboration_mode\u003e...\u003c/collaboration_mode\u003e` change it; user requests or tool descriptions do not change mode by themselves. Known mode names are Default and Plan.\n\n## request_user_input availability\n\nUse the `request_user_input` tool only when it is listed in the available tools for this turn.\n\nIn Default mode, strongly prefer making reasonable assumptions and executing the user's request rather than stopping to ask questions.\n\nUse the `request_user_input` tool only for optional questions where the answer would materially improve the quality of the work.\n\nIf `request_user_input` returns no answers, continue with best judgment instead of asking again or treating the turn as blocked.\n\nNever use the `request_user_input` tool for permission requests or permission-related escalations.\n\nIf explicit user input is required for another reason before progress can safely continue, do not use the `request_user_input` tool. Ask the user directly with one concise plain-text question instead. Never write a multiple choice question as a textual assistant message.\u003c/collaboration_mode\u003e","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a078c1-f5bf-7210-be7d-6c69a5ffa15e","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["generic.developer_instructions","memories.instructions","host_skills.instructions","permissions.instructions","collaboration_mode.instructions"],"turn_id":"01a078ac-bf7f-7050-adcc-4d223220149d"},"role":"developer","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"\u003cmulti_agent_role\u003eYou are `/root`, the primary agent in a team of agents collaborating to fulfill the user's goals.\n\nAt the start of your turn, you are the active agent.\nYou can spawn sub-agents to handle subtasks, and those sub-agents can spawn their own sub-agents.\nAll agents in the team, including the agents that you can assign tasks to, are equally intelligent and capable, and have access to the same set of tools.\n\nYou can use `spawn_agent` to create a new agent, `followup_task` to give an existing agent a new task and trigger a turn, and `send_message` to pass a message to a running agent without triggering a turn.\n`send_message` calls may be read by a human, so ensure they are legible. Always put proper spaces between words and/or numbers.\nChild agents can also spawn their own sub-agents.\nYou can decide how much context you want to propagate to your sub-agents with the `fork_turns` parameter.\n\nYou will receive messages in the analysis channel in the form:\n```\nMessage Type: MESSAGE | FINAL_ANSWER\nTask name: \u003crecipient\u003e\nSender: \u003cauthor\u003e\nPayload:\n\u003cpayload text\u003e\n```\nThey may be addressed as to=/root\n\nNote that collaboration tools cannot be called from inside `functions.exec`. Call `spawn_agent`, `send_message`, `followup_task`, `wait_agent`, `interrupt_agent`, and `list_agents` only as direct tool calls using the recipient shown in their tool definitions, such as `to=functions.collaboration.spawn_agent`, since they are intentionally absent from the `functions.exec` `tools.*` namespace. Available tools in `functions.exec` are explicitly described with a `tools` namespace in the developer message.\n\nAll agents share the same directory. In detail:\n- All agents have access to the same container and filesystem as you.\n- All agents use the same current working directory.\n- As a result, edits made by one agent are immediately visible to all other agents.\n\nWhen calling `wait_agent`, prefer longer waits (minutes) to avoid busy polling.\n\nThere are 4 available concurrency slots, meaning that up to 4 agents can be active at once, including you.\n\nFull-history forks (`fork_turns` omitted or `\"all\"`) inherit the parent model and reasoning effort and do not accept overrides. Only set `model` or `reasoning_effort` when explicitly requested by the user, applicable `AGENTS.md` instructions, or skill instructions; when doing so, set `fork_turns` to `\"none\"` or a positive integer string.\u003c/multi_agent_role\u003e","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a078c1-f5bf-7210-be7d-6c7aeee1a8ce","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["multi_agent.role_instructions"],"turn_id":"01a078ac-bf7f-7050-adcc-4d223220149d"},"role":"developer","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"\u003cmulti_agent_mode\u003eAny earlier instruction enabling proactive multi-agent delegation no longer applies. Do not spawn sub-agents unless the user or applicable AGENTS.md/skill instructions explicitly ask for sub-agents, delegation, or parallel agent work.\u003c/multi_agent_mode\u003e","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a078c1-f5bf-7210-be7d-6c808e313a6c","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["multi_agent.mode_instructions"],"turn_id":"01a078ac-bf7f-7050-adcc-4d223220149d"},"role":"developer","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"\u003crecommended_plugins\u003e\nHere is a list of plugins that are available but not installed.\n\n- Airtable (airtable@openai-curated-remote)\n- Apollo.io (apollo@openai-curated-remote)\n- Asana (asana@openai-curated-remote)\n- Atlassian Rovo (atlassian-rovo@openai-curated-remote)\n- Base44 (base44@openai-curated-remote)\n- Box (box@openai-curated-remote)\n- Canva (canva@openai-curated-remote)\n- Clay (clay@openai-curated-remote)\n- Codex Security (codex-security@openai-curated-remote)\n- Datadog (Preview) (datadog@openai-curated-remote)\n- Figma (figma@openai-curated-remote)\n- Google Calendar (google-calendar@openai-curated-remote)\n- Granola (granola@openai-curated-remote)\n- HeyGen (heygen@openai-curated-remote)\n- HubSpot (hubspot@openai-curated-remote)\n- Linear (linear@openai-curated-remote)\n- Lovable (lovable@openai-curated-remote)\n- monday.com (monday-com@openai-curated-remote)\n- Notion (notion@openai-curated-remote)\n- OpenAI Developers (openai-developers@openai-curated-remote)\n- Outlook Calendar (outlook-calendar@openai-curated-remote)\n- Outlook Email (outlook-email@openai-curated-remote)\n- PitchBook (pitchbook@openai-curated-remote)\n- PostHog (posthog@openai-curated-remote)\n- Intuit QuickBooks (quickbooks@openai-curated-remote)\n- Semrush (semrush@openai-curated-remote)\n- Sentry (sentry@openai-curated-remote)\n- SharePoint (sharepoint@openai-curated-remote)\n- Supabase (supabase@openai-curated-remote)\n- Superhuman Mail (superhuman@openai-curated-remote)\n- Superpowers (superpowers@openai-curated-remote)\n- Teams (teams@openai-curated-remote)\n- Vercel (vercel@openai-curated-remote)\n- Zoom (zoom@openai-curated-remote)\n- ZoomInfo (zoominfo@openai-curated-remote)\n\u003c/recommended_plugins\u003e","type":"input_text"},{"text":"# AGENTS.md instructions for /Users/jdx/.codex/worktrees/141d/mise\n\n\u003cINSTRUCTIONS\u003e\n# Global preferences\n\n- Create GitHub pull requests ready for review. Never create draft pull requests unless the user explicitly requests a draft.\n\n--- project-doc ---\n\n# Repository Agent Guide\n\n## mbx build cache\n\n`mise install` installs mbx. `mise run` activates the project's transparent\nCargo wrapper, so compilation-heavy mise tasks and hk checks use ordinary\n`cargo` commands. Standalone Cargo commands require an activated mise shell. If\nthe wrapper fails or creates a development papercut, rerun the exact equivalent\ncommand from `CONTRIBUTING.md` with `MBX_DISABLE=1`; this unblocks work without\nweakening the check. If bypassed Cargo succeeds, surface the mismatch and recommend a\n[mr-boxington Discussion](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/discussions) with\nthe repository and commit, OS, `mbx --version`, `mbx doctor`, and both commands\nand outputs. Redact secrets, absolute cache paths, remote URLs, namespaces, and\nother sensitive or identifying details. Do not permanently disable the wrapper,\nand do not post externally without user authorization.\n\nThis file is the canonical agent guide. `CLAUDE.md` is a symlink to `AGENTS.md` for compatibility with existing tooling.\n\n## Registry Submissions: READ THIS FIRST\n\n**Most new registry additions from agents get rejected.** Before adding a new tool to `registry/`, understand the rules:\n\n- **mise does not host self-written, personal, niche, or low-popularity tools.** The registry is curated for tools that are *already* widely used. \"It works\" or \"it has tests\" is not the bar.\n- **There is a high bar for new registry additions: tools generally need thousands of GitHub stars, not hundreds.** jdx will reject projects that do not meet this popularity bar and will not give a reason. Per [contributing.md](docs/contributing.md): \"@jdx won't explain why a given tool wasn't accepted.\" There is no appeal, no checklist, no second chance — the PR is closed and that's it.\n- **Wasted PRs are the default outcome** for tools the agent or user has not vetted against this bar. Do not submit one speculatively.\n\n### Required check for new registry additions\n\nBefore adding a new tool or shorthand to `registry/`, ALWAYS do the following. This check does not apply when editing an existing registry entry; do not ask the user about popularity for maintenance or fixes to tools already in the registry.\n\n1. **Warn the user clearly and ask:** \"New registry additions have a high popularity bar: jdx generally rejects projects without thousands of GitHub stars and will not give a reason. Is this tool already widely used outside your own projects and does it meet that bar?\" If it's the user's own tool, a fork, an internal/company tool, or something with a small audience, **stop and tell them the PR will be rejected.** Do not submit.\n2. **Actively check popularity for every new registry addition — no exceptions.** Look up real numbers; do not guess. Useful sources:\n   - GitHub stars and fork count (`gh repo view owner/repo --json stargazerCount,forkCount`)\n   - Recent release activity / last commit date (`gh repo view owner/repo --json pushedAt,latestRelease`)\n   - Download counts on relevant package registries (npm `npmjs.com/package/x`, crates.io, PyPI, Homebrew analytics, etc.)\n   - Whether the project shows up in third-party docs, awesome-lists, or other tools\n3. **Apply the bar.** Rough signals — very low numbers are disqualifying:\n   - GitHub stars in the thousands, not hundreds\n   - Active maintenance (recent releases, not abandoned)\n   - Real third-party usage (referenced in docs, blog posts, other tools, package registries)\n   - Recognizable in its ecosystem\n4. **Include the popularity data in the PR description.** Every PR adding a new registry tool or shorthand MUST contain a short section like:\n\n   ```\n   ## Popularity\n   - GitHub: 12.3k stars, 480 forks, last release 2026-04-12\n   - crates.io: 1.2M downloads\n   - Used by: \u003cproject A\u003e, \u003cproject B\u003e\n   ```\n\n   This is non-negotiable for new additions — it lets the maintainer evaluate the submission without re-doing the research. New-tool PRs without it look speculative and are more likely to be rejected.\n5. **If the tool is borderline or numbers are low, warn the user clearly** that the PR is likely to be rejected without reason, and ask if they still want to proceed. Do not soften this — users have repeatedly been surprised when their PR was closed, and the agent should have warned them up front.\n6. **Suggest the alternative:** users can install any tool themselves via explicit backend syntax (`mise use aqua:owner/repo`, `mise use github:owner/repo`, `mise use cargo:name`, `mise use npm:name`, etc.) or by writing a [tool plugin](https://mise.jdx.dev/tool-plugin-development.html). The registry is *only* for shorthand convenience for popular tools — not for enabling installation.\n\n### Backend choice: packslip (preferred), then aqua, github, or gitlab\n\nFor registry entries the backend tiers are:\n\n- **Version listing is mandatory.** Before adding a registry entry, run `mise ls-remote \u003cbackend\u003e` and confirm it returns installable versions. A backend that can install only an explicitly pinned version is not sufficient, even if the package exists in an upstream registry. If the preferred backend cannot list versions, use another accepted backend (for example, a custom `http:` backend with a reliable `version_list_url`) or stop.\n- **Tier 1 — preferred:** `packslip:`. Use it when the project publishes signed release manifests; mise verifies the signer and artifact digests without requiring a plugin or separate package manager.\n- **Tier 2 — routinely accepted:** `aqua:`, `github:`, and `gitlab:`.\n  - **Prefer `aqua:`** when the project does not publish packslips and the tool is in the [aqua registry](https://github.com/aquaproj/aqua-registry). Better UX, SLSA verification, and per-version logic.\n  - **Use `github:`** when the tool isn't in aqua but ships GitHub releases.\n  - **Use `gitlab:`** for tools released through GitLab.\n- **Tier 3 — high bar, but lower than tier 4:** `conda:`. Potentially acceptable when the tool can't be supported via packslip/aqua/github/gitlab. The bar is lower than tier 4 because **the conda backend in mise does not require a separately-installed package manager** — mise downloads and extracts packages directly from anaconda.org via rattler, so users don't need conda/mamba on PATH. Still requires a popular, well-maintained tool.\n- **Tier 4 — extremely high bar, almost never accepted:** `npm:`, `pipx:`, `gem:`, `cargo:`, `go:`, `dotnet:`. These all rely on a separately-installed runtime/toolchain being present on PATH (`node`, `python`, `ruby`, `cargo`, `go`, `dotnet`), which is fragile — the wrong version, a missing install, or PATH ordering quirks all break them. `npm:`/`pipx:`/`gem:` are particularly painful because tools installed via them silently bind to whichever `node`/`python`/`ruby` was on PATH at install time. Don't reach for these for a registry PR unless the user has explicitly confirmed @jdx wants it that way for this specific tool.\n- **Not accepted at all:**\n  - **New `asdf:` plugins** — supply-chain security. Use packslip/aqua/github/gitlab instead.\n  - **New `vfox:` plugins** — same reason. Use packslip/aqua/github/gitlab instead.\n  - **`ubi:`** is deprecated and will not be accepted under any circumstances.\n\nUsers can still install via any backend themselves with explicit syntax (`mise use vfox:...`, `mise use cargo:...`, etc.) — they just don't get a registry shorthand for it.\n\n## Development Commands\n\n### Building and Testing\n- `mise run build` or `mise run b` - Build the project with cargo\n- `target/debug/mise` - Run the built binary directly\n- `mise run test` or `mise run t` - Run all tests (unit + e2e)\n- `mise run test:unit` - Run unit tests only\n- `mise run test:e2e` - Run end-to-end tests only\n- `mise run snapshots` - Update test snapshots with `cargo insta`\n\n### Debugging\n- Use `MISE_DEBUG=1` or `MISE_TRACE=1` environment variables to enable debug output (not `RUST_LOG`)\n\n### Code Quality and Testing\n- `mise run lint` - Run all linting tasks\n- `mise run lint-fix` - Run linting and automatically fix issues\n- `mise run format` - Format code (part of CI task)\n- `mise run ci` - Run format, build, and test\n- `mise run test:e2e [test_filename]...` - Run specific e2e tests (use this instead of executing test files directly)\n- `mise --cd crates/vfox run test` - Run tests for the vfox crate\n- `mise --cd crates/vfox run lint` - Run linting for the vfox crate\n- `mise --cd crates/vfox run lint-fix` - Run linting and fix issues for the vfox crate\n- `mise task ls` - List all available tasks\n\n### Clippy Exclusions\n\n- Do not add `#[allow(clippy::...)]`, `#[expect(clippy::...)]`, Cargo lint levels set to `allow`, or `-A clippy::...` command-line flags.\n- Refactor the code so `cargo clippy --workspace --all-features --all-targets -- -D warnings` passes without exclusions.\n- If a feature or fix needs a preparatory refactor to satisfy Clippy cleanly, put that refactor in a prerequisite PR and stack the behavior change on top of it.\n\n### Documentation and Generation\n- `mise run render` - Generate all documentation and completions\n- `mise run render:usage` - Generate CLI usage documentation\n- `mise run render:completions` - Generate shell completions\n- `mise run docs` - Start documentation dev server\n- `mise run docs:build` - Build documentation\n\n### Development\n- `mise run install-dev` - Install development version locally\n- `mise run clean` - Clean cargo build artifacts\n\n## Code Architecture\n\n### High-Level Structure\nMise is a Rust CLI tool that manages development environments, tools, tasks, and environment variables. The codebase follows a modular architecture:\n\n**Core Components:**\n- `src/main.rs` - Entry point and CLI initialization\n- `src/cli/` - Command-line interface implementation with subcommands\n- `src/config/` - Configuration file parsing and management\n- `src/backend/` - Tool backend implementations (aqua, github, cargo, npm, asdf, vfox, …)\n- `src/toolset/` - Tool version management and installation logic\n- `src/task/` - Task execution system\n- `src/plugins/` - Plugin system for extending tool support\n\n**Key Backend Systems** (`src/backend/`):\n- `packslip.rs` — Signed release manifests (preferred for new registry entries)\n- `aqua.rs` — Aqua registry\n- `github.rs` — GitHub / GitLab / Forgejo releases\n- `http.rs`, `s3.rs` — HTTP and S3 backends\n- `cargo.rs`, `npm.rs` (plus embedded aube), `pipx.rs`, `gem.rs`, `go.rs`, `dotnet.rs`, `conda.rs`, `pkgx.rs`, `spm.rs`\n- `asdf.rs`, `vfox.rs` — plugin compatibility layers\n- `ubi.rs` — deprecated; do not add new registry entries\n\n**Core Tools (Built-in):**\n- `src/plugins/core/` — Node, Python, Go, Ruby, Java, Bun, Deno, Elixir, Erlang, Dotnet, Swift, Zig, Rust\n\n**Configuration System:**\n- `mise.toml` files for project configuration\n- `.tool-versions` files for ASDF compatibility\n- Environment variable management and templating\n- Task definition and execution\n\n### Key Design Patterns\n1. **Backend Architecture**: Tools are implemented through a unified backend interface, allowing multiple sources (ASDF plugins, vfox plugins, cargo, npm, etc.)\n2. **Toolset Management**: The `Toolset` manages collections of tool versions and their installation state\n3. **Configuration Layering**: Config files are loaded hierarchically from system → global → local with environment-specific overrides\n4. **Task System**: Tasks can be defined in TOML files with dependencies, environment variables, and multiple execution modes\n\n### Configuration Files\n- `mise.toml` - Main configuration file format\n- `settings.toml` - Global settings definitions (generates code/docs)\n- `registry/` - Tool registry mappings\n- `tasks.toml` - Project task definitions\n\n### Test Structure\n- Unit tests within source files\n- E2E tests in `e2e/` directory organized by feature area (e.g., `e2e/cli/`, `e2e/backend/`)\n- E2E tests are bash scripts using assertion helpers from `e2e/assert.sh` (e.g., `assert`, `assert_contains`, `assert_fail`)\n- E2E tests do not need cleanup steps (rm, etc.) — the test harness handles that\n- Snapshot tests using `insta` crate for CLI output verification\n- Windows-specific tests in `e2e-win/`\n\n### Build System\n- Rust project using Cargo with workspace for `crates/vfox`\n- Custom build script in `build.rs` for generating metadata\n- Multiple build profiles including `release` and `serious` (with LTO)\n- Cross-compilation support via `Cross.toml`\n\n## Development Guidelines\n\n### Conventional Commits (REQUIRED)\nPR titles MUST follow conventional commit format. Intermediate commit subjects\nSHOULD use the same format:\n\n**Format:** `\u003ctype\u003e[optional scope][optional !]: \u003cdescription\u003e`\n\n**Types:**\n- `feat:` - New features\n- `fix:` - Bug fixes that affect the CLI behavior (not CI, docs, or infrastructure)\n- `refactor:` - Code refactoring\n- `docs:` - Documentation changes\n- `style:` - Code style/formatting (no logic changes)\n- `perf:` - Performance improvements\n- `test:` - Testing changes\n- `chore:` - Maintenance tasks, releases, dependency updates, CI/infrastructure changes\n- `security:` - Security-related changes\n- `registry:` - Any changes to `registry/` (do not add a scope)\n- `ci:` - CI and automation changes\n- `revert:` - Reverting a previous change\n\n**Scopes:**\n- For command-specific changes, use the command name: `install`, `activate`, `use`, `exec`, etc.\n- For subsystem changes: `config`, `backend`, `env`, `task`, `vfox`, `python`, `github`, `release`, `completions`, `http`, `schema`, `doctor`, `shim`, `core`, `deps`, `ci`\n- Use `task` (not `run`) for task-related changes, even if the code lives in `src/cli/run.rs` or `src/cmd.rs`\n\n**Description Style:**\n- Start the description with a lowercase character\n- Use imperative mood (\"add feature\" not \"added feature\")\n- Keep it concise but descriptive\n\n**Examples:**\n- `fix(install): resolve version mismatch for previously installed tools`\n- `feat(activate): add fish shell support`\n- `feat(vfox): add semver Lua module for version sorting`\n- `feat(env): add environment caching with module cacheability support`\n- `docs(contributing): update hk usages`\n- `chore: release 2026.1.6`\n- `chore(ci): add FORGEJO_TOKEN for API authentication`\n- `registry: add miller`\n\nCI validates the pull request title and re-runs when it is edited. Intermediate\ncommit subjects are not checked because pull requests are squash-merged. CI\nmechanically checks the allowed type, syntax, and lowercase-leading description;\nimperative mood and breaking-change details remain review rules.\n\n### Pre-commit Process\n1. Run `mise run lint-fix` and `git add` any lint fixes before committing\n2. Use `mise run test:e2e [test_filename]...` for running specific e2e tests\n3. Never run e2e tests by executing them directly — always use the mise task\n\n`hk.pkl` currently defines `check` and `fix` steps only (no git `pre-commit` hook). `hk install --mise` may print that nothing is installed; that is expected. Use `mise run lint` / `mise run lint-fix` (which run hk) instead.\n\n### hk Agent Workflow\n\n- Prefer the hk MCP server for effect-aware plans, checks, fixes, logs, and captured diffs.\n- When invoking hk directly, use `hk run check --safe --format json` for a complete machine-readable result or `--format jsonl` for streaming lifecycle events.\n- Scope checks to changed files. For an exact file list, pass NUL-delimited paths with `--files0-from`; use `--cd` to target another project directory.\n- Never run a command classified as unknown or destructive without explicit user approval. Review the resulting diff after fixes.\n\n### Dependency Updates\n\n- Use the lowest-specificity dependency requirement that expresses compatibility in `Cargo.toml` (for example, prefer `\"1\"` over `\"1.2.3\"`, and `\"0.12\"` over `\"0.12.1\"` for a pre-1.0 crate).\n- Routine dependency updates should only change `Cargo.lock`. If the existing `Cargo.toml` requirement accepts the target version, do not change it merely to force or record the update; use `cargo update -p \u003ccrate\u003e --precise \u003cversion\u003e` instead.\n- Keep lockfile updates focused on the requested dependency and its required transitive changes. Remove unrelated resolver churn before committing.\n\n#### Updating embedded aube\n\n- Update `aube` and `aube-registry` together and refresh all aube workspace crates in `Cargo.lock`.\n- Review the upstream changes for embedder API or behavior changes and make any required mise integration changes.\n- Update the standalone `aube` development tool entry in `mise.lock` to the same version so local and CI workflows exercise the version mise embeds.\n- Run these focused checks:\n  - `cargo check --locked`\n  - `cargo test --locked --bin mise aube`\n  - `cargo test --locked --bin mise task::workspace::node::tests`\n  - `mise run test:e2e e2e/backend/test_npm_aube`\n\n## Deprecation Policy\n\nWhen deprecating a feature, backend, or implicit behavior:\n\n1. **Immediately**: Mark it as deprecated in docs (add a warning banner) and display a CLI warning using the `deprecated_at!` macro from `src/output.rs` (`warn_at` is the current version).\n2. **12 months after warn** (`remove_at`): `debug_assert!` in `deprecated_at!` fires, signaling the deprecated code or behavior should be removed.\n\nDelay the CLI warning for up to 6 months only when migration requires a new setting, syntax, or replacement that older supported mise versions would reject or fail to parse. This compatibility window lets users adopt a configuration that works across old and new clients before warnings begin. Do not delay warnings for a behavior change that requires no new configuration, or when the replacement already works in older clients.\n\nUse mise version format for dates (e.g., `deprecated_at!(\"2026.10.0\", \"2027.10.0\", \"id\", \"message\")`).\n\nIf a compatibility window is required, removal remains 12 months after `warn_at`, not 12 months after the initial documentation notice.\n\n## Important Implementation Notes\n\n### Backend System\nWhen implementing new tool backends, follow the pattern in `src/backend/mod.rs`. Each backend must implement the `Backend` trait with methods for listing versions, installing tools, and managing tool metadata.\n\n### DO NOT ASSUME SEMVER\n**Do not assume tool versions follow semver or any other orderable scheme.** mise manages hundreds of tools with wildly different versioning conventions:\n\n- Date-based: `2024.01.15`, `20241015`\n- Pre-release / ref / tag versions: `tip`, `HEAD`, `nightly`, `edge`, `canary`, `ref:main`, `tag:v1`, `sub-X.Y:...`\n- Non-numeric tags: Python `3.12.0a1`, Ruby `3.2.0-preview1`, Go `1.22rc1`, Node `lts/hydrogen`, `lts-iron`\n- Tool-specific meanings of `latest` (e.g. some exclude pre-releases, some don't)\n\n**Rules:**\n1. Do not call `versions::Versioning::new(...)` (or any other semver comparator) at a new call site to pick the \"newest\" version, \"resolve latest\", or sort a version list. That crate silently returns `None` / arbitrary ordering for non-semver strings, which means wrong versions get chosen for many tools.\n2. To resolve a version request (`latest`, a prefix, a channel name), delegate to the backend via `Backend::latest_version`, `Backend::latest_installed_version`, `Backend::list_versions_matching`, or `ToolRequest::resolve` — the backend knows what \"latest\" means for its tool.\n3. To list installed versions in a meaningful order, use `Backend::list_installed_versions_matching` or the toolset's resolved versions. Do not reorder them yourself.\n4. Lockfile version strings must be treated as opaque — compare with `==`, never with a version ordering. Never write a non-concrete string (`latest`, `lts/*`, a prefix) into the lockfile; resolve first.\n\nA few existing call sites (e.g. runtime symlinks) do use `Versioning` ordering today, but that's legacy behavior and arguably also wrong — do not point at them to justify new semver assumptions.\n\nIf you think you need to pick \"the newest installed version\" at a new call site, stop and ask — that call almost always belongs on the backend, not inline.\n\n### Plugin Development\n- Core tools are implemented in `src/plugins/core/`\n- External plugins use ASDF or vfox compatibility layers\n- Plugin metadata is defined in `mise.plugin.toml` files\n\n### Configuration Parsing\nThe configuration system supports multiple file formats and environment-specific configs. Changes to settings require updating `settings.toml` and running `mise run render:schema`.\n\n### Testing Strategy\n- E2E tests are organized by feature area (cli/, config/, backend/, etc.)\n- Use snapshot testing for CLI output verification\n- Backend-specific tests verify tool installation and version management\n- Slow tests (marked with `_slow` suffix) test actual tool compilation/installation\n\n### Cross-Platform Considerations\n- Windows-specific implementations in files ending with `_windows.rs`\n- Platform-specific tool installation logic in core plugins\n- Shim system varies by platform (especially Windows)\n- we don't chmod mise e2e tests to be executable\n\n### Windows PATH boundaries\n\n- Do not invoke subprocesses to translate paths in activation hooks, shims, or `mise x`/task execution.\n- Keep PATH in native Windows form inside mise and when launching child processes, including child MSYS2, Git Bash, or Cygwin processes. Never pre-convert the PATH inherited by a child emulator.\n- Convert PATH only when native Windows mise emits an assignment into an already-running, positively identified MSYS2/Cygwin shell. Only persisted mounts from the runtime defaults, `fstab`, and `fstab.d` are available; session-only `mount` changes cannot be reconstructed without entering the running process.\n- Never infer an emulator from the word `bash` alone. Require MSYS/Cygwin runtime evidence, and explicitly exclude WSL and BusyBox Bash.\n- Windows activation tests must execute a command through the emitted PATH and verify a native Windows grandchild receives a valid semicolon-delimited PATH. Output-shape assertions alone are insufficient.\n\n## GitHub Interactions\n\nNever open pull requests against the `release` branch. Default PRs to `main` unless the user explicitly names a different non-`release` base branch. If a change appears to belong on `release`, stop and ask for the intended branch strategy instead of opening a PR against `release`.\n\nWhen AI contributes GitHub content—including a pull request description, review, pull request\ncomment, or discussion post—append this disclosure:\n\n`*AI-assisted — Tool: \u003ctool\u003e; model: \u003cprovider\u003e/\u003cmodel\u003e; version: \u003cversion-or-unavailable\u003e.*`\n\nUse the exact model and version identifiers exposed by the runtime. Never infer or guess them; use\n`unavailable` when either value is not exposed.\n\n## Documentation\n\n### URL Structure\nWhen referencing mise documentation URLs, use the correct path structure based on the `docs/` directory layout:\n\n- **Dev tools \u0026 backends**: `mise.jdx.dev/dev-tools/backends/\u003cbackend\u003e.html` (e.g., `mise.jdx.dev/dev-tools/backends/s3.html`)\n- **Configuration**: `mise.jdx.dev/configuration/...`\n- **Tasks**: `mise.jdx.dev/tasks/...`\n- **Environments**: `mise.jdx.dev/environments/...`\n- **CLI reference**: `mise.jdx.dev/cli/...`\n\nDo NOT use shortened paths like `mise.jdx.dev/backends/...` - always include the full path matching the `docs/` directory structure.\n\n## Cursor Cloud specific instructions\n\nCloud Agents bootstrap from `.cursor/environment.json`, which runs `.cursor/install.sh`. Draft environment builds often run as `ubuntu` rather than `root`; the script handles both (passwordless sudo, cargo/rustup permissions, world-writable `/tmp/fslock`).\n\nThe install script:\n\n- cds to the repository root derived from the script path before reading `Cargo.toml` or building\n- installs host packages needed to build mise and to run most e2e tests (openssl, pkg-config, zsh, fish, direnv, python3 + venv, jq, git, build-essential, and compile-time libs). It does **not** install a JDK or GUI libraries; those live in `packaging/e2e/Dockerfile`. `apt-get` is invoked as `sudo -n env DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive apt-get …` so the frontend reaches apt when elevation is required\n- selects the Rust toolchain from the root `Cargo.toml` `rust-version`, including `rustfmt` and `clippy` (do not hardcode the MSRV)\n- builds `target/debug/mise` and symlinks it to `/usr/local/bin/mise`\n- keeps `GITHUB_TOKEN`, `MISE_GITHUB_TOKEN`, and `GH_TOKEN` in sync via one `sync_github_tokens` helper (prefer any already-set token; fall back to `gh auth token` only when all three are empty)\n- runs `MISE_SAFE=1 /usr/local/bin/mise install` with the just-built binary so checkout-controlled hooks/templates/`[env]` and tool-level `postinstall` / `install_env` cannot run with those tokens, then `mise trust` for later agent commands\n- runs `hk install --mise` (`hk.pkl` has no git hook, so this may report that nothing is installed)\n- persists mise shims and token sync in one `/etc/profile.d/mise-dev-env.sh` (shims first, then `sync_github_tokens`) and rewrites the Cloud Agent block in `/etc/bash.bashrc` so non-login interactive bash picks it up after a snapshot. Fish/zsh only get this from login shells (`profile.d`), not from bashrc\n- exposes the mise-installed `node` / `npm` / `npx` / `hk` / `gh` binaries on `/usr/local/bin` (isolated e2e PATH includes that directory, not the agent's shims). Links freeze the version from install time — re-run `.cursor/install.sh` after upgrading those tools\n\nThere is no long-running service to start. Do not put `mise run build` or `mise run test:unit` in `terminals`; those are one-shot commands and would rerun a full build/test on every boot.\n\nThe debug `mise` binary is already on PATH. Prefer `mise run …` for project tasks.\n\n### E2E on Cloud Agents\n\n- Always `mise run test:e2e [test_filename]...` — never execute e2e scripts directly\n- Slow tests (`*_slow`) are skipped unless `TEST_ALL=1`. Do not run the full suite unless asked; pick tests under the feature area you changed\n- Isolated e2e uses `env -i` and a fake `HOME`, so the agent's mise shims are not on PATH. Tests install their own tools. Host packages (zsh, fish, direnv, python3, jq, git) still need to be on `/usr/bin`\n- If GitHub API calls 429, run `export GITHUB_TOKEN=\"$(gh auth token)\"; export MISE_GITHUB_TOKEN=\"$GITHUB_TOKEN\"`\n- A leftover `/tmp/mise.toml` will fail the harness; remove it if that error appears\n\n\u003c/INSTRUCTIONS\u003e","type":"input_text"},{"text":"\u003cenvironment_context\u003e\n  \u003ccwd\u003e/Users/jdx/.codex/worktrees/141d/mise\u003c/cwd\u003e\n  \u003cshell\u003ezsh\u003c/shell\u003e\n  \u003ccurrent_date\u003e2026-09-06\u003c/current_date\u003e\n  \u003ctimezone\u003eAmerica/Chicago\u003c/timezone\u003e\n  \u003cfilesystem\u003e\u003cworkspace_roots\u003e\u003croot\u003e/Users/jdx/.codex/worktrees/141d/mise\u003c/root\u003e\u003croot\u003e/Users/jdx/.codex/visualizations/2026/09/05/01a07177-9855-71a2-89cd-4a9a37cdbfcb\u003c/root\u003e\u003c/workspace_roots\u003e\u003cpermission_profile type=\"disabled\"\u003e\u003cfile_system type=\"unrestricted\" /\u003e\u003c/permission_profile\u003e\u003c/filesystem\u003e\n\u003c/environment_context\u003e","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a078c1-f5bf-7210-be7d-6c954b45edea","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["plugins.recommendations","agents_md.instructions","environments.environment_context"],"turn_id":"01a078ac-bf7f-7050-adcc-4d223220149d"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"the agenti had working on the encryption prs is done so you should include that now in these checks\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a078bd-ed73-7143-ad39-8b7d6042e80b","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788732108.147861,"turn_id":"01a078ac-bf7f-7050-adcc-4d223220149d"},"role":"user","type":"message"}],"window_id":"01a078c1-f5bf-7210-be7d-6c412680ff75","window_number":11}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-06T22:06:12.417Z","ordinal":9351,"type":"world_state","payload":{"full":true,"state":{"agents_md":{"directory":"/Users/jdx/.codex/worktrees/141d/mise","text":"# Global preferences\n\n- Create GitHub pull requests ready for review. Never create draft pull requests unless the user explicitly requests a draft.\n\n--- project-doc ---\n\n# Repository Agent Guide\n\n## mbx build cache\n\n`mise install` installs mbx. `mise run` activates the project's transparent\nCargo wrapper, so compilation-heavy mise tasks and hk checks use ordinary\n`cargo` commands. Standalone Cargo commands require an activated mise shell. If\nthe wrapper fails or creates a development papercut, rerun the exact equivalent\ncommand from `CONTRIBUTING.md` with `MBX_DISABLE=1`; this unblocks work without\nweakening the check. If bypassed Cargo succeeds, surface the mismatch and recommend a\n[mr-boxington Discussion](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/discussions) with\nthe repository and commit, OS, `mbx --version`, `mbx doctor`, and both commands\nand outputs. Redact secrets, absolute cache paths, remote URLs, namespaces, and\nother sensitive or identifying details. Do not permanently disable the wrapper,\nand do not post externally without user authorization.\n\nThis file is the canonical agent guide. `CLAUDE.md` is a symlink to `AGENTS.md` for compatibility with existing tooling.\n\n## Registry Submissions: READ THIS FIRST\n\n**Most new registry additions from agents get rejected.** Before adding a new tool to `registry/`, understand the rules:\n\n- **mise does not host self-written, personal, niche, or low-popularity tools.** The registry is curated for tools that are *already* widely used. \"It works\" or \"it has tests\" is not the bar.\n- **There is a high bar for new registry additions: tools generally need thousands of GitHub stars, not hundreds.** jdx will reject projects that do not meet this popularity bar and will not give a reason. Per [contributing.md](docs/contributing.md): \"@jdx won't explain why a given tool wasn't accepted.\" There is no appeal, no checklist, no second chance — the PR is closed and that's it.\n- **Wasted PRs are the default outcome** for tools the agent or user has not vetted against this bar. Do not submit one speculatively.\n\n### Required check for new registry additions\n\nBefore adding a new tool or shorthand to `registry/`, ALWAYS do the following. This check does not apply when editing an existing registry entry; do not ask the user about popularity for maintenance or fixes to tools already in the registry.\n\n1. **Warn the user clearly and ask:** \"New registry additions have a high popularity bar: jdx generally rejects projects without thousands of GitHub stars and will not give a reason. Is this tool already widely used outside your own projects and does it meet that bar?\" If it's the user's own tool, a fork, an internal/company tool, or something with a small audience, **stop and tell them the PR will be rejected.** Do not submit.\n2. **Actively check popularity for every new registry addition — no exceptions.** Look up real numbers; do not guess. Useful sources:\n   - GitHub stars and fork count (`gh repo view owner/repo --json stargazerCount,forkCount`)\n   - Recent release activity / last commit date (`gh repo view owner/repo --json pushedAt,latestRelease`)\n   - Download counts on relevant package registries (npm `npmjs.com/package/x`, crates.io, PyPI, Homebrew analytics, etc.)\n   - Whether the project shows up in third-party docs, awesome-lists, or other tools\n3. **Apply the bar.** Rough signals — very low numbers are disqualifying:\n   - GitHub stars in the thousands, not hundreds\n   - Active maintenance (recent releases, not abandoned)\n   - Real third-party usage (referenced in docs, blog posts, other tools, package registries)\n   - Recognizable in its ecosystem\n4. **Include the popularity data in the PR description.** Every PR adding a new registry tool or shorthand MUST contain a short section like:\n\n   ```\n   ## Popularity\n   - GitHub: 12.3k stars, 480 forks, last release 2026-04-12\n   - crates.io: 1.2M downloads\n   - Used by: <project A>, <project B>\n   ```\n\n   This is non-negotiable for new additions — it lets the maintainer evaluate the submission without re-doing the research. New-tool PRs without it look speculative and are more likely to be rejected.\n5. **If the tool is borderline or numbers are low, warn the user clearly** that the PR is likely to be rejected without reason, and ask if they still want to proceed. Do not soften this — users have repeatedly been surprised when their PR was closed, and the agent should have warned them up front.\n6. **Suggest the alternative:** users can install any tool themselves via explicit backend syntax (`mise use aqua:owner/repo`, `mise use github:owner/repo`, `mise use cargo:name`, `mise use npm:name`, etc.) or by writing a [tool plugin](https://mise.jdx.dev/tool-plugin-development.html). The registry is *only* for shorthand convenience for popular tools — not for enabling installation.\n\n### Backend choice: packslip (preferred), then aqua, github, or gitlab\n\nFor registry entries the backend tiers are:\n\n- **Version listing is mandatory.** Before adding a registry entry, run `mise ls-remote <backend>` and confirm it returns installable versions. A backend that can install only an explicitly pinned version is not sufficient, even if the package exists in an upstream registry. If the preferred backend cannot list versions, use another accepted backend (for example, a custom `http:` backend with a reliable `version_list_url`) or stop.\n- **Tier 1 — preferred:** `packslip:`. Use it when the project publishes signed release manifests; mise verifies the signer and artifact digests without requiring a plugin or separate package manager.\n- **Tier 2 — routinely accepted:** `aqua:`, `github:`, and `gitlab:`.\n  - **Prefer `aqua:`** when the project does not publish packslips and the tool is in the [aqua registry](https://github.com/aquaproj/aqua-registry). Better UX, SLSA verification, and per-version logic.\n  - **Use `github:`** when the tool isn't in aqua but ships GitHub releases.\n  - **Use `gitlab:`** for tools released through GitLab.\n- **Tier 3 — high bar, but lower than tier 4:** `conda:`. Potentially acceptable when the tool can't be supported via packslip/aqua/github/gitlab. The bar is lower than tier 4 because **the conda backend in mise does not require a separately-installed package manager** — mise downloads and extracts packages directly from anaconda.org via rattler, so users don't need conda/mamba on PATH. Still requires a popular, well-maintained tool.\n- **Tier 4 — extremely high bar, almost never accepted:** `npm:`, `pipx:`, `gem:`, `cargo:`, `go:`, `dotnet:`. These all rely on a separately-installed runtime/toolchain being present on PATH (`node`, `python`, `ruby`, `cargo`, `go`, `dotnet`), which is fragile — the wrong version, a missing install, or PATH ordering quirks all break them. `npm:`/`pipx:`/`gem:` are particularly painful because tools installed via them silently bind to whichever `node`/`python`/`ruby` was on PATH at install time. Don't reach for these for a registry PR unless the user has explicitly confirmed @jdx wants it that way for this specific tool.\n- **Not accepted at all:**\n  - **New `asdf:` plugins** — supply-chain security. Use packslip/aqua/github/gitlab instead.\n  - **New `vfox:` plugins** — same reason. Use packslip/aqua/github/gitlab instead.\n  - **`ubi:`** is deprecated and will not be accepted under any circumstances.\n\nUsers can still install via any backend themselves with explicit syntax (`mise use vfox:...`, `mise use cargo:...`, etc.) — they just don't get a registry shorthand for it.\n\n## Development Commands\n\n### Building and Testing\n- `mise run build` or `mise run b` - Build the project with cargo\n- `target/debug/mise` - Run the built binary directly\n- `mise run test` or `mise run t` - Run all tests (unit + e2e)\n- `mise run test:unit` - Run unit tests only\n- `mise run test:e2e` - Run end-to-end tests only\n- `mise run snapshots` - Update test snapshots with `cargo insta`\n\n### Debugging\n- Use `MISE_DEBUG=1` or `MISE_TRACE=1` environment variables to enable debug output (not `RUST_LOG`)\n\n### Code Quality and Testing\n- `mise run lint` - Run all linting tasks\n- `mise run lint-fix` - Run linting and automatically fix issues\n- `mise run format` - Format code (part of CI task)\n- `mise run ci` - Run format, build, and test\n- `mise run test:e2e [test_filename]...` - Run specific e2e tests (use this instead of executing test files directly)\n- `mise --cd crates/vfox run test` - Run tests for the vfox crate\n- `mise --cd crates/vfox run lint` - Run linting for the vfox crate\n- `mise --cd crates/vfox run lint-fix` - Run linting and fix issues for the vfox crate\n- `mise task ls` - List all available tasks\n\n### Clippy Exclusions\n\n- Do not add `#[allow(clippy::...)]`, `#[expect(clippy::...)]`, Cargo lint levels set to `allow`, or `-A clippy::...` command-line flags.\n- Refactor the code so `cargo clippy --workspace --all-features --all-targets -- -D warnings` passes without exclusions.\n- If a feature or fix needs a preparatory refactor to satisfy Clippy cleanly, put that refactor in a prerequisite PR and stack the behavior change on top of it.\n\n### Documentation and Generation\n- `mise run render` - Generate all documentation and completions\n- `mise run render:usage` - Generate CLI usage documentation\n- `mise run render:completions` - Generate shell completions\n- `mise run docs` - Start documentation dev server\n- `mise run docs:build` - Build documentation\n\n### Development\n- `mise run install-dev` - Install development version locally\n- `mise run clean` - Clean cargo build artifacts\n\n## Code Architecture\n\n### High-Level Structure\nMise is a Rust CLI tool that manages development environments, tools, tasks, and environment variables. The codebase follows a modular architecture:\n\n**Core Components:**\n- `src/main.rs` - Entry point and CLI initialization\n- `src/cli/` - Command-line interface implementation with subcommands\n- `src/config/` - Configuration file parsing and management\n- `src/backend/` - Tool backend implementations (aqua, github, cargo, npm, asdf, vfox, …)\n- `src/toolset/` - Tool version management and installation logic\n- `src/task/` - Task execution system\n- `src/plugins/` - Plugin system for extending tool support\n\n**Key Backend Systems** (`src/backend/`):\n- `packslip.rs` — Signed release manifests (preferred for new registry entries)\n- `aqua.rs` — Aqua registry\n- `github.rs` — GitHub / GitLab / Forgejo releases\n- `http.rs`, `s3.rs` — HTTP and S3 backends\n- `cargo.rs`, `npm.rs` (plus embedded aube), `pipx.rs`, `gem.rs`, `go.rs`, `dotnet.rs`, `conda.rs`, `pkgx.rs`, `spm.rs`\n- `asdf.rs`, `vfox.rs` — plugin compatibility layers\n- `ubi.rs` — deprecated; do not add new registry entries\n\n**Core Tools (Built-in):**\n- `src/plugins/core/` — Node, Python, Go, Ruby, Java, Bun, Deno, Elixir, Erlang, Dotnet, Swift, Zig, Rust\n\n**Configuration System:**\n- `mise.toml` files for project configuration\n- `.tool-versions` files for ASDF compatibility\n- Environment variable management and templating\n- Task definition and execution\n\n### Key Design Patterns\n1. **Backend Architecture**: Tools are implemented through a unified backend interface, allowing multiple sources (ASDF plugins, vfox plugins, cargo, npm, etc.)\n2. **Toolset Management**: The `Toolset` manages collections of tool versions and their installation state\n3. **Configuration Layering**: Config files are loaded hierarchically from system → global → local with environment-specific overrides\n4. **Task System**: Tasks can be defined in TOML files with dependencies, environment variables, and multiple execution modes\n\n### Configuration Files\n- `mise.toml` - Main configuration file format\n- `settings.toml` - Global settings definitions (generates code/docs)\n- `registry/` - Tool registry mappings\n- `tasks.toml` - Project task definitions\n\n### Test Structure\n- Unit tests within source files\n- E2E tests in `e2e/` directory organized by feature area (e.g., `e2e/cli/`, `e2e/backend/`)\n- E2E tests are bash scripts using assertion helpers from `e2e/assert.sh` (e.g., `assert`, `assert_contains`, `assert_fail`)\n- E2E tests do not need cleanup steps (rm, etc.) — the test harness handles that\n- Snapshot tests using `insta` crate for CLI output verification\n- Windows-specific tests in `e2e-win/`\n\n### Build System\n- Rust project using Cargo with workspace for `crates/vfox`\n- Custom build script in `build.rs` for generating metadata\n- Multiple build profiles including `release` and `serious` (with LTO)\n- Cross-compilation support via `Cross.toml`\n\n## Development Guidelines\n\n### Conventional Commits (REQUIRED)\nPR titles MUST follow conventional commit format. Intermediate commit subjects\nSHOULD use the same format:\n\n**Format:** `<type>[optional scope][optional !]: <description>`\n\n**Types:**\n- `feat:` - New features\n- `fix:` - Bug fixes that affect the CLI behavior (not CI, docs, or infrastructure)\n- `refactor:` - Code refactoring\n- `docs:` - Documentation changes\n- `style:` - Code style/formatting (no logic changes)\n- `perf:` - Performance improvements\n- `test:` - Testing changes\n- `chore:` - Maintenance tasks, releases, dependency updates, CI/infrastructure changes\n- `security:` - Security-related changes\n- `registry:` - Any changes to `registry/` (do not add a scope)\n- `ci:` - CI and automation changes\n- `revert:` - Reverting a previous change\n\n**Scopes:**\n- For command-specific changes, use the command name: `install`, `activate`, `use`, `exec`, etc.\n- For subsystem changes: `config`, `backend`, `env`, `task`, `vfox`, `python`, `github`, `release`, `completions`, `http`, `schema`, `doctor`, `shim`, `core`, `deps`, `ci`\n- Use `task` (not `run`) for task-related changes, even if the code lives in `src/cli/run.rs` or `src/cmd.rs`\n\n**Description Style:**\n- Start the description with a lowercase character\n- Use imperative mood (\"add feature\" not \"added feature\")\n- Keep it concise but descriptive\n\n**Examples:**\n- `fix(install): resolve version mismatch for previously installed tools`\n- `feat(activate): add fish shell support`\n- `feat(vfox): add semver Lua module for version sorting`\n- `feat(env): add environment caching with module cacheability support`\n- `docs(contributing): update hk usages`\n- `chore: release 2026.1.6`\n- `chore(ci): add FORGEJO_TOKEN for API authentication`\n- `registry: add miller`\n\nCI validates the pull request title and re-runs when it is edited. Intermediate\ncommit subjects are not checked because pull requests are squash-merged. CI\nmechanically checks the allowed type, syntax, and lowercase-leading description;\nimperative mood and breaking-change details remain review rules.\n\n### Pre-commit Process\n1. Run `mise run lint-fix` and `git add` any lint fixes before committing\n2. Use `mise run test:e2e [test_filename]...` for running specific e2e tests\n3. Never run e2e tests by executing them directly — always use the mise task\n\n`hk.pkl` currently defines `check` and `fix` steps only (no git `pre-commit` hook). `hk install --mise` may print that nothing is installed; that is expected. Use `mise run lint` / `mise run lint-fix` (which run hk) instead.\n\n### hk Agent Workflow\n\n- Prefer the hk MCP server for effect-aware plans, checks, fixes, logs, and captured diffs.\n- When invoking hk directly, use `hk run check --safe --format json` for a complete machine-readable result or `--format jsonl` for streaming lifecycle events.\n- Scope checks to changed files. For an exact file list, pass NUL-delimited paths with `--files0-from`; use `--cd` to target another project directory.\n- Never run a command classified as unknown or destructive without explicit user approval. Review the resulting diff after fixes.\n\n### Dependency Updates\n\n- Use the lowest-specificity dependency requirement that expresses compatibility in `Cargo.toml` (for example, prefer `\"1\"` over `\"1.2.3\"`, and `\"0.12\"` over `\"0.12.1\"` for a pre-1.0 crate).\n- Routine dependency updates should only change `Cargo.lock`. If the existing `Cargo.toml` requirement accepts the target version, do not change it merely to force or record the update; use `cargo update -p <crate> --precise <version>` instead.\n- Keep lockfile updates focused on the requested dependency and its required transitive changes. Remove unrelated resolver churn before committing.\n\n#### Updating embedded aube\n\n- Update `aube` and `aube-registry` together and refresh all aube workspace crates in `Cargo.lock`.\n- Review the upstream changes for embedder API or behavior changes and make any required mise integration changes.\n- Update the standalone `aube` development tool entry in `mise.lock` to the same version so local and CI workflows exercise the version mise embeds.\n- Run these focused checks:\n  - `cargo check --locked`\n  - `cargo test --locked --bin mise aube`\n  - `cargo test --locked --bin mise task::workspace::node::tests`\n  - `mise run test:e2e e2e/backend/test_npm_aube`\n\n## Deprecation Policy\n\nWhen deprecating a feature, backend, or implicit behavior:\n\n1. **Immediately**: Mark it as deprecated in docs (add a warning banner) and display a CLI warning using the `deprecated_at!` macro from `src/output.rs` (`warn_at` is the current version).\n2. **12 months after warn** (`remove_at`): `debug_assert!` in `deprecated_at!` fires, signaling the deprecated code or behavior should be removed.\n\nDelay the CLI warning for up to 6 months only when migration requires a new setting, syntax, or replacement that older supported mise versions would reject or fail to parse. This compatibility window lets users adopt a configuration that works across old and new clients before warnings begin. Do not delay warnings for a behavior change that requires no new configuration, or when the replacement already works in older clients.\n\nUse mise version format for dates (e.g., `deprecated_at!(\"2026.10.0\", \"2027.10.0\", \"id\", \"message\")`).\n\nIf a compatibility window is required, removal remains 12 months after `warn_at`, not 12 months after the initial documentation notice.\n\n## Important Implementation Notes\n\n### Backend System\nWhen implementing new tool backends, follow the pattern in `src/backend/mod.rs`. Each backend must implement the `Backend` trait with methods for listing versions, installing tools, and managing tool metadata.\n\n### DO NOT ASSUME SEMVER\n**Do not assume tool versions follow semver or any other orderable scheme.** mise manages hundreds of tools with wildly different versioning conventions:\n\n- Date-based: `2024.01.15`, `20241015`\n- Pre-release / ref / tag versions: `tip`, `HEAD`, `nightly`, `edge`, `canary`, `ref:main`, `tag:v1`, `sub-X.Y:...`\n- Non-numeric tags: Python `3.12.0a1`, Ruby `3.2.0-preview1`, Go `1.22rc1`, Node `lts/hydrogen`, `lts-iron`\n- Tool-specific meanings of `latest` (e.g. some exclude pre-releases, some don't)\n\n**Rules:**\n1. Do not call `versions::Versioning::new(...)` (or any other semver comparator) at a new call site to pick the \"newest\" version, \"resolve latest\", or sort a version list. That crate silently returns `None` / arbitrary ordering for non-semver strings, which means wrong versions get chosen for many tools.\n2. To resolve a version request (`latest`, a prefix, a channel name), delegate to the backend via `Backend::latest_version`, `Backend::latest_installed_version`, `Backend::list_versions_matching`, or `ToolRequest::resolve` — the backend knows what \"latest\" means for its tool.\n3. To list installed versions in a meaningful order, use `Backend::list_installed_versions_matching` or the toolset's resolved versions. Do not reorder them yourself.\n4. Lockfile version strings must be treated as opaque — compare with `==`, never with a version ordering. Never write a non-concrete string (`latest`, `lts/*`, a prefix) into the lockfile; resolve first.\n\nA few existing call sites (e.g. runtime symlinks) do use `Versioning` ordering today, but that's legacy behavior and arguably also wrong — do not point at them to justify new semver assumptions.\n\nIf you think you need to pick \"the newest installed version\" at a new call site, stop and ask — that call almost always belongs on the backend, not inline.\n\n### Plugin Development\n- Core tools are implemented in `src/plugins/core/`\n- External plugins use ASDF or vfox compatibility layers\n- Plugin metadata is defined in `mise.plugin.toml` files\n\n### Configuration Parsing\nThe configuration system supports multiple file formats and environment-specific configs. Changes to settings require updating `settings.toml` and running `mise run render:schema`.\n\n### Testing Strategy\n- E2E tests are organized by feature area (cli/, config/, backend/, etc.)\n- Use snapshot testing for CLI output verification\n- Backend-specific tests verify tool installation and version management\n- Slow tests (marked with `_slow` suffix) test actual tool compilation/installation\n\n### Cross-Platform Considerations\n- Windows-specific implementations in files ending with `_windows.rs`\n- Platform-specific tool installation logic in core plugins\n- Shim system varies by platform (especially Windows)\n- we don't chmod mise e2e tests to be executable\n\n### Windows PATH boundaries\n\n- Do not invoke subprocesses to translate paths in activation hooks, shims, or `mise x`/task execution.\n- Keep PATH in native Windows form inside mise and when launching child processes, including child MSYS2, Git Bash, or Cygwin processes. Never pre-convert the PATH inherited by a child emulator.\n- Convert PATH only when native Windows mise emits an assignment into an already-running, positively identified MSYS2/Cygwin shell. Only persisted mounts from the runtime defaults, `fstab`, and `fstab.d` are available; session-only `mount` changes cannot be reconstructed without entering the running process.\n- Never infer an emulator from the word `bash` alone. Require MSYS/Cygwin runtime evidence, and explicitly exclude WSL and BusyBox Bash.\n- Windows activation tests must execute a command through the emitted PATH and verify a native Windows grandchild receives a valid semicolon-delimited PATH. Output-shape assertions alone are insufficient.\n\n## GitHub Interactions\n\nNever open pull requests against the `release` branch. Default PRs to `main` unless the user explicitly names a different non-`release` base branch. If a change appears to belong on `release`, stop and ask for the intended branch strategy instead of opening a PR against `release`.\n\nWhen AI contributes GitHub content—including a pull request description, review, pull request\ncomment, or discussion post—append this disclosure:\n\n`*AI-assisted — Tool: <tool>; model: <provider>/<model>; version: <version-or-unavailable>.*`\n\nUse the exact model and version identifiers exposed by the runtime. Never infer or guess them; use\n`unavailable` when either value is not exposed.\n\n## Documentation\n\n### URL Structure\nWhen referencing mise documentation URLs, use the correct path structure based on the `docs/` directory layout:\n\n- **Dev tools & backends**: `mise.jdx.dev/dev-tools/backends/<backend>.html` (e.g., `mise.jdx.dev/dev-tools/backends/s3.html`)\n- **Configuration**: `mise.jdx.dev/configuration/...`\n- **Tasks**: `mise.jdx.dev/tasks/...`\n- **Environments**: `mise.jdx.dev/environments/...`\n- **CLI reference**: `mise.jdx.dev/cli/...`\n\nDo NOT use shortened paths like `mise.jdx.dev/backends/...` - always include the full path matching the `docs/` directory structure.\n\n## Cursor Cloud specific instructions\n\nCloud Agents bootstrap from `.cursor/environment.json`, which runs `.cursor/install.sh`. Draft environment builds often run as `ubuntu` rather than `root`; the script handles both (passwordless sudo, cargo/rustup permissions, world-writable `/tmp/fslock`).\n\nThe install script:\n\n- cds to the repository root derived from the script path before reading `Cargo.toml` or building\n- installs host packages needed to build mise and to run most e2e tests (openssl, pkg-config, zsh, fish, direnv, python3 + venv, jq, git, build-essential, and compile-time libs). It does **not** install a JDK or GUI libraries; those live in `packaging/e2e/Dockerfile`. `apt-get` is invoked as `sudo -n env DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive apt-get …` so the frontend reaches apt when elevation is required\n- selects the Rust toolchain from the root `Cargo.toml` `rust-version`, including `rustfmt` and `clippy` (do not hardcode the MSRV)\n- builds `target/debug/mise` and symlinks it to `/usr/local/bin/mise`\n- keeps `GITHUB_TOKEN`, `MISE_GITHUB_TOKEN`, and `GH_TOKEN` in sync via one `sync_github_tokens` helper (prefer any already-set token; fall back to `gh auth token` only when all three are empty)\n- runs `MISE_SAFE=1 /usr/local/bin/mise install` with the just-built binary so checkout-controlled hooks/templates/`[env]` and tool-level `postinstall` / `install_env` cannot run with those tokens, then `mise trust` for later agent commands\n- runs `hk install --mise` (`hk.pkl` has no git hook, so this may report that nothing is installed)\n- persists mise shims and token sync in one `/etc/profile.d/mise-dev-env.sh` (shims first, then `sync_github_tokens`) and rewrites the Cloud Agent block in `/etc/bash.bashrc` so non-login interactive bash picks it up after a snapshot. Fish/zsh only get this from login shells (`profile.d`), not from bashrc\n- exposes the mise-installed `node` / `npm` / `npx` / `hk` / `gh` binaries on `/usr/local/bin` (isolated e2e PATH includes that directory, not the agent's shims). Links freeze the version from install time — re-run `.cursor/install.sh` after upgrading those tools\n\nThere is no long-running service to start. Do not put `mise run build` or `mise run test:unit` in `terminals`; those are one-shot commands and would rerun a full build/test on every boot.\n\nThe debug `mise` binary is already on PATH. Prefer `mise run …` for project tasks.\n\n### E2E on Cloud Agents\n\n- Always `mise run test:e2e [test_filename]...` — never execute e2e scripts directly\n- Slow tests (`*_slow`) are skipped unless `TEST_ALL=1`. Do not run the full suite unless asked; pick tests under the feature area you changed\n- Isolated e2e uses `env -i` and a fake `HOME`, so the agent's mise shims are not on PATH. Tests install their own tools. Host packages (zsh, fish, direnv, python3, jq, git) still need to be on `/usr/bin`\n- If GitHub API calls 429, run `export GITHUB_TOKEN=\"$(gh auth token)\"; export MISE_GITHUB_TOKEN=\"$GITHUB_TOKEN\"`\n- A leftover `/tmp/mise.toml` will fail the harness; remove it if that error appears\n"},"apps_instructions":false,"collaboration_mode":{"mode":"default","model":"gpt-6-astra","instructions":"f6a6d5eb73bbc12c3bdfeb3b3170124c19ff3a77"},"context_window_guidance":"","environments":{"environments":{"local":{"cwd":"/Users/jdx/.codex/worktrees/141d/mise","status":"available","shell":"zsh"}},"current_date":"2026-09-06","timezone":"America/Chicago","filesystem":"<filesystem><workspace_roots><root>/Users/jdx/.codex/worktrees/141d/mise</root><root>/Users/jdx/.codex/visualizations/2026/09/05/01a07177-9855-71a2-89cd-4a9a37cdbfcb</root></workspace_roots><permission_profile type=\"disabled\"><file_system type=\"unrestricted\" /></permission_profile></filesystem>"},"environments_instructions":false,"git_attribution":false,"host_skills":{"body":"\n## Skills\nA skill is a set of local instructions to follow that is stored in a `SKILL.md` file. Below is the list of skills that can be used. Each entry includes a name, description, and a short path that can be expanded into an absolute path using the skill roots table.\n### Skill roots\n- `r0` = `/Users/jdx/.codex/skills/.system`\n- `r1` = `/Users/jdx/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-bundled/sites/0.1.57/skills`\n- `r2` = `/Users/jdx/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-bundled`\n- `r3` = `/Users/jdx/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/cloudflare/0.1.2/skills`\n- `r4` = `/Users/jdx/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote`\n- `r5` = `/Users/jdx/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/google-drive/0.1.16/skills`\n- `r6` = `/Users/jdx/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-primary-runtime`\n- `r7` = `/Users/jdx/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-primary-runtime/spreadsheets/26.904.11930/skills`\n- `r8` = `/Users/jdx/.codex/worktrees/141d/mise/.agents/skills`\n### Available skills\n- imagegen: Generate or edit raster images when the task benefits from AI-created bitmap visuals such as photos, illustrations, textures, sprites, mockups, or transparent-background cutouts. Use when Codex should create a brand-new image, transform an existing image, or derive visual variants from references, and the output should be a bitmap asset rather than repo-native code or vector. Do not use when the task is better handled by editing existing SVG/vector/code-native assets, extending an established icon or logo system, or building the visual directly in HTML/CSS/canvas. (file: r0/imagegen/SKILL.md)\n- openai-docs: Use for Codex models/pricing, scheduled tasks, skills, settings, setup, troubleshooting, customization, automations, and self-knowledge—including 'you,' 'your,' 'this app,' or 'this coding agent' when they refer to Codex—and for OpenAI APIs/products and ChatGPT Work. Also use for model choice/migration, prompting, SDKs, Responses, Realtime, agents, evals, and Chat/Work/Codex comparisons. Do not use for generic app/software tasks that merely mention Codex. (file: r0/openai-docs/SKILL.md)\n- plugin-creator: Create and scaffold plugin directories for Codex with a required `.codex-plugin/plugin.json`, optional plugin folders/files, valid manifest defaults, and personal-marketplace entries by default. Use when Codex needs to create a new personal plugin, add optional plugin structure, generate or update marketplace entries for plugin ordering and availability metadata, or update an existing local plugin during development with the CLI-driven cachebuster and reinstall flow. (file: r0/plugin-creator/SKILL.md)\n- skill-creator: Create or update a Codex skill with appropriately scoped instructions and any needed supporting resources. (file: r0/skill-creator/SKILL.md)\n- skill-installer: Install Codex skills into $CODEX_HOME/skills from a curated list or a GitHub repo path. Use when a user asks to list installable skills, install a curated skill, or install a skill from another repo (including private repos). (file: r0/skill-installer/SKILL.md)\n- entire-search: Search Entire checkpoint history and transcripts with `entire search --json`. Use proactively when the user asks about previous work, commits, sessions, prompts, or historical context in this repository. (file: r8/entire-search/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare:agents-sdk: Build AI agents on Cloudflare Workers using the Agents SDK. Load when creating stateful agents, durable workflows, real-time WebSocket apps, scheduled tasks, MCP servers, or chat applications. Covers Agent class, state management, callable RPC, Workflows integration, and React hooks. Biases towards retrieval from Cloudflare docs over pre-trained knowledge. (file: r3/agents-sdk/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare:building-ai-agent-on-cloudflare: Builds AI agents on Cloudflare using the Agents SDK with state management, real-time WebSockets, scheduled tasks, tool integration, and chat capabilities. Generates production-ready agent code deployed to Workers. Use when: user wants to \"build an agent\", \"AI agent\", \"chat agent\", \"stateful agent\", mentions \"Agents SDK\", needs \"real-time AI\", \"WebSocket AI\", or asks about agent \"state management\", \"scheduled tasks\", or \"tool calling\". Biases towards retrieval from Cloudflare docs over pre-trained knowledge. (file: r3/building-ai-agent-on-cloudflare/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare:building-mcp-server-on-cloudflare: Builds remote MCP (Model Context Protocol) servers on Cloudflare Workers with tools, OAuth authentication, and production deployment. Generates server code, configures auth providers, and deploys to Workers. Use when: user wants to \"build MCP server\", \"create MCP tools\", \"remote MCP\", \"deploy MCP\", add \"OAuth to MCP\", or mentions Model Context Protocol on Cloudflare. Also triggers on \"MCP authentication\" or \"MCP deployment\". Biases towards retrieval from Cloudflare docs over pre-trained knowledge. (file: r3/building-mcp-server-on-cloudflare/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare:cloudflare: Comprehensive Cloudflare platform skill covering Workers, Pages, storage (KV, D1, R2), AI (Workers AI, Vectorize, Agents SDK), networking (Tunnel, Spectrum), security (WAF, DDoS), and infrastructure-as-code (Terraform, Pulumi). Use for any Cloudflare development task. Biases towards retrieval from Cloudflare docs over pre-trained knowledge. (file: r3/cloudflare/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare:durable-objects: Create and review Cloudflare Durable Objects. Use when building stateful coordination (chat rooms, multiplayer games, booking systems), implementing RPC methods, SQLite storage, alarms, WebSockets, or reviewing DO code for best practices. Covers Workers integration, wrangler config, and testing with Vitest. Biases towards retrieval from Cloudflare docs over pre-trained knowledge. (file: r3/durable-objects/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare:sandbox-sdk: Build sandboxed applications for secure code execution. Load when building AI code execution, code interpreters, CI/CD systems, interactive dev environments, or executing untrusted code. Covers Sandbox SDK lifecycle, commands, files, code interpreter, and preview URLs. Biases towards retrieval from Cloudflare docs over pre-trained knowledge. (file: r3/sandbox-sdk/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare:web-perf: Analyzes web performance using Chrome DevTools MCP. Measures Core Web Vitals (FCP, LCP, TBT, CLS, Speed Index), identifies render-blocking resources, network dependency chains, layout shifts, caching issues, and accessibility gaps. Use when asked to audit, profile, debug, or optimize page load performance, Lighthouse scores, or site speed. Biases towards retrieval from current documentation over pre-trained knowledge. (file: r3/web-perf/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare:workers-best-practices: Reviews and authors Cloudflare Workers code against production best practices. Load when writing new Workers, reviewing Worker code, configuring wrangler.jsonc, or checking for common Workers anti-patterns (streaming, floating promises, global state, secrets, bindings, observability). Biases towards retrieval from Cloudflare docs over pre-trained knowledge. (file: r3/workers-best-practices/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare:wrangler: Cloudflare Workers CLI for deploying, developing, and managing Workers, KV, R2, D1, Vectorize, Hyperdrive, Workers AI, Containers, Queues, Workflows, Pipelines, and Secrets Store. Load before running wrangler commands to ensure correct syntax and best practices. Biases towards retrieval from Cloudflare docs over pre-trained knowledge. (file: r3/wrangler/SKILL.md)\n- deep-research-work:deep-research: Conduct thorough research using authoritative sources and produce evidence-backed findings with citations. Use this skill only when the user asks specifically for Deep research, uses $deep-research, or selects Deep research in Work mode. Do not use it for ordinary research requests. (file: r4/deep-research-work/0.1.14/skills/deep-research/SKILL.md)\n- documents:documents: Create, edit, redline, and comment on `.docx`, Word, and Google Docs-targeted document artifacts inside the container, with a strict render-and-verify workflow. Use `render_docx.py` to generate page PNGs (and optional PDF) for visual QA, then iterate until layout is flawless before delivering the final document. (file: r6/documents/26.904.11930/skills/documents/SKILL.md)\n- google-drive:google-docs: Prompt- and template-complete Google Docs creation and editing with explicit-instruction-authoritative structural preservation, including semantic roles, relationships, comparison dimensions, and instructed extensions; full-topology native-copy routing; source-grounded per-tab adaptation for past/example references; style-preserving hyperlink and table edits; canonical smart-chip-first authoring for dates and relevant supported people or Google resources; a file-backed advisory trusted read before existing-document writes; automatic protected-control awareness; direct connector APIs by default; DOCX-first import only when no supplied Google Doc template/reference constrains the output; and checked-in code mode only for exact native dropdown mutation. Use when Codex must create, edit, fill, adapt, redesign, or verify Google Docs without overriding explicit user/template instructions, adding unrequested document scope, or carrying stale reference facts into a new deliverable. (file: r5/google-docs/SKILL.md)\n- google-drive:google-drive: Use connected Google Drive as the single entrypoint for Drive, Docs, Sheets, and Slides work. Use when the user wants to find, fetch, organize, share, export, copy, or delete Drive files, or summarize and edit Google Docs, Google Sheets, and Google Slides through one unified Google Drive plugin. (file: r5/google-drive/SKILL.md)\n- google-drive:google-drive-comments: Write, reply to, and resolve Google Drive comments on Docs, Sheets, Slides, and Drive files with evidence-backed location context. Use when the user asks to leave comments, review a file with comments, respond to comment threads, or resolve Drive comments. (file: r5/google-drive-comments/SKILL.md)\n- google-drive:google-sheets: Analyze and edit connected Google Sheets with range precision. Use when the user wants to create Google Sheets, find a spreadsheet, inspect tabs or ranges, search rows, plan formulas, create or repair charts, clean or restructure tables, write concise summaries, or make explicit cell-range updates. (file: r5/google-sheets/SKILL.md)\n- google-drive:google-slides: Route Google Slides authoring requests and derive a design system from a native template or reference deck. Use this skill when the user provides an existing native Google Slides deck as a template, reference, or prior-period source, or asks to edit, update, repair, restyle, or clean up an existing native Google Slides deck. Use the Presentations skill instead for net-new presentation creation when no existing native Google Slides deck must be followed. (file: r5/google-slides/SKILL.md)\n- pdf:pdf: Read, create, inspect, render, and verify PDF files where visual layout matters, including fillable AcroForms. Use Poppler rendering plus Python tools such as reportlab, pdfplumber, and pypdf for generation and extraction. (file: r6/pdf/26.904.11930/skills/pdf/SKILL.md)\n- plugin-management:plugin-management: Discover and suggest relevant plugins, inspect app permissions and dependencies, and manage plugin connections or removal. Use when the user asks about plugins or when a task would materially benefit from an external app, account, service, or data source that available tools cannot access. (file: r4/plugin-management/0.1.0/skills/plugin-management/SKILL.md)\n- presentations:Presentations: Read, create or edit PowerPoint or Google Slides decks. Use for presentation, slide deck, PowerPoint, PPT, PPTX, or Google Slides requests. (file: r6/presentations/26.904.11930/skills/presentations/SKILL.md)\n- sites:sites-building: Use Sites to build websites, including landing pages, portfolios, dashboards, portals, trackers, hubs, and internal tools. Always use Sites when the project contains `.openai/hosting.json`. (file: r1/sites-building/SKILL.md)\n- sites:sites-hosting: Host websites with Sites. Always use after `sites-building`, and use for website publishing, deployment, hosting management, or projects containing `.openai/hosting.json`. (file: r1/sites-hosting/SKILL.md)\n- spreadsheets:Spreadsheets: Create, edit, analyze, and verify standalone spreadsheet files or Google Sheets-ready workbooks, including .xlsx, .xls, .csv, and .tsv. Do not use for live controlling Microsoft Excel app or a live Excel session. (file: r7/spreadsheets/SKILL.md)\n- spreadsheets:excel-live-control: Control an open or active Microsoft Excel workbook through the ChatGPT add-in or connected session. Use when the user tags the Microsoft Excel app in Codex or follows up on an established live Excel task. Do not use for standalone spreadsheet files or Google Sheets. (file: r7/excel-live-control/SKILL.md)\n- template-creator:template-creator: Create or update a reusable personal Codex artifact-template skill. Use when the user invokes $template-creator or asks in natural language to create a reusable template from a reference document, presentation, spreadsheet, Google Docs, Slides, or Sheets link, ImageGen or Product Design image, email, Slack message, or Site project, or explicitly asks to edit or update a passed artifact-template skill. Do not use for one-off creation from an existing template. (file: r6/template-creator/26.904.11930/skills/template-creator/SKILL.md)\n- visualize:visualize: Create visualizations and interactive tools directly in conversation. Proactively use to show how something works; explore 'what happens when', 'what changes', or 'help me understand'; compare or inspect; create simulations, maps, charts, graphs, and mockups. Use standard tools for static scientific figures. (file: r2/visualize/1.0.29/skills/visualize/SKILL.md)\n","includeInstructions":true},"managed_developer_instructions":{},"model":"gpt-6-astra","multi_agent_mode":{"mode":"explicitRequestOnly","usage_hint_hash":"30fb77255ffd0abcecb08a8897e242564dfe1192"},"multi_agent_usage_hint":"30fb77255ffd0abcecb08a8897e242564dfe1192","orchestrator_skills":{"includeInstructions":true,"enabled":false},"permissions":{"instructions":"32ca25d6d85edaeb6f1e19b2a5cc4e08d2a631cd","approved_command_prefixes":[["cargo","+1.96.0","test"],["cargo","test"],["gh","pr","create"],["git","add"],["git","commit"],["git","push"],["git","switch"]]},"persistent_mode":{},"personality":{"model":"gpt-6-astra","personality":"friendly"},"plugins_instructions":false,"realtime":{"active":false},"skills":{"includeInstructions":true}}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-06T22:06:12.417Z","ordinal":9352,"type":"turn_context","payload":{"turn_id":"01a078ac-bf7f-7050-adcc-4d223220149d","root_turn_id":"01a078ac-bf7f-7050-adcc-4d223220149d","cwd":"/Users/jdx/.codex/worktrees/141d/mise","workspace_roots":["/Users/jdx/.codex/worktrees/141d/mise","/Users/jdx/.codex/visualizations/2026/09/05/01a07177-9855-71a2-89cd-4a9a37cdbfcb"],"current_date":"2026-09-06","timezone":"America/Chicago","approval_policy":"never","approvals_reviewer":"user","sandbox_policy":{"type":"danger-full-access"},"permission_profile":{"type":"disabled"},"model":"gpt-6-astra","comp_hash":"3000","personality":"friendly","collaboration_mode":{"mode":"default","settings":{"model":"gpt-6-astra","reasoning_effort":"medium","developer_instructions":"# Collaboration Mode: Default\n\nYou are now in Default mode. Any previous instructions for other modes (e.g. Plan mode) are no longer active.\n\nYour active mode changes only when new developer instructions with a different `<collaboration_mode>...</collaboration_mode>` change it; user requests or tool descriptions do not change mode by themselves. Known mode names are Default and Plan.\n\n## request_user_input availability\n\nUse the `request_user_input` tool only when it is listed in the available tools for this turn.\n\nIn Default mode, strongly prefer making reasonable assumptions and executing the user's request rather than stopping to ask questions.\n\nUse the `request_user_input` tool only for optional questions where the answer would materially improve the quality of the work.\n\nIf `request_user_input` returns no answers, continue with best judgment instead of asking again or treating the turn as blocked.\n\nNever use the `request_user_input` tool for permission requests or permission-related escalations.\n\nIf explicit user input is required for another reason before progress can safely continue, do not use the `request_user_input` tool. Ask the user directly with one concise plain-text question instead. Never write a multiple choice question as a textual assistant message.\n\n## Heartbeats\n\nOccasionally you will see a user message surrounded with a `<heartbeat>` XML tag. This is a special heartbeat message. It is not actually sent by the user, but by the system on some interval of time. The purpose of heartbeats is to make you feel magical and proactive. When you encounter a heartbeat, realize there is no one specific thing to do. There is no instruction manual for heartbeats other than the format of your final response.\n\nA general guideline is to use your existing tools and capabilities. Orient yourself, be proactive, and think big picture. If something is important enough that the user should know about now, notify them. Otherwise, stay quiet.\n\nRoutine polling results are quiet by default. Choose `DONT_NOTIFY` when the monitored state is unchanged or still non-actionable, such as pending, queued, in progress, or healthy. Choose `NOTIFY` only for a meaningful update the user should know about now, such as completion, failure, a material state change, or required user action. Do not treat the heartbeat firing, work performed, or an automation prompt's generic request for a status update as sufficient reason to notify. Honor routine periodic updates only when the user explicitly asked for them.\n\n```xml\n<heartbeat>\n  <automation_id>automation id string</automation_id>\n  <decision>NOTIFY</decision>\n  <message>One short user-facing notification message.</message>\n</heartbeat>\n```\n\n```xml\n<heartbeat>\n  <automation_id>automation id string</automation_id>\n  <decision>DONT_NOTIFY</decision>\n  <message>One short quiet-status message explaining why no user action is needed.</message>\n</heartbeat>\n```\n\nIf you choose `NOTIFY`, you may include a brief user-facing update before the XML block.\nIf you choose `DONT_NOTIFY`, include the short quiet-status `<message>`, but do not include any user-facing prose outside the XML block, including commentary or progress updates while the heartbeat runs.\n\nEvery heartbeat turn must end with exactly one non-empty final response containing one of the XML blocks above. Never finish a heartbeat with an empty final response, even when there is no change to report; return the `DONT_NOTIFY` block with a short quiet-status message instead.\n\nThe current heartbeat trigger includes `<automation_id>`. When the reason for the heartbeat is done, obsolete, or no longer worth checking, search for `automation_update` if it is not already available, then call it with `mode=\"delete\"` and that automation id before your heartbeat response. If you delete the automation, mention that clearly in the response so the user understands why it stopped. If the task has changed and the heartbeat is still useful, update the automation instead of leaving stale instructions in place."}},"multi_agent_version":"v2","realtime_active":false,"effort":"medium","summary":"auto"}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-06T22:06:12.417Z","ordinal":9353,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"thread_settings_applied","thread_id":"01a07177-9855-71a2-89cd-4a9a37cdbfcb","thread_settings":{"model":"gpt-6-astra","model_provider_id":"openai","service_tier":"default","approval_policy":"never","approvals_reviewer":"user","permission_profile":{"type":"disabled"},"cwd":"/Users/jdx/.codex/worktrees/141d/mise","reasoning_effort":"medium","reasoning_summary":"none","personality":"friendly","collaboration_mode":{"mode":"default","settings":{"model":"gpt-6-astra","reasoning_effort":"medium","developer_instructions":"# Collaboration Mode: Default\n\nYou are now in Default mode. Any previous instructions for other modes (e.g. Plan mode) are no longer active.\n\nYour active mode changes only when new developer instructions with a different `<collaboration_mode>...</collaboration_mode>` change it; user requests or tool descriptions do not change mode by themselves. Known mode names are Default and Plan.\n\n## request_user_input availability\n\nUse the `request_user_input` tool only when it is listed in the available tools for this turn.\n\nIn Default mode, strongly prefer making reasonable assumptions and executing the user's request rather than stopping to ask questions.\n\nUse the `request_user_input` tool only for optional questions where the answer would materially improve the quality of the work.\n\nIf `request_user_input` returns no answers, continue with best judgment instead of asking again or treating the turn as blocked.\n\nNever use the `request_user_input` tool for permission requests or permission-related escalations.\n\nIf explicit user input is required for another reason before progress can safely continue, do not use the `request_user_input` tool. Ask the user directly with one concise plain-text question instead. Never write a multiple choice question as a textual assistant message.\n\n## Heartbeats\n\nOccasionally you will see a user message surrounded with a `<heartbeat>` XML tag. This is a special heartbeat message. It is not actually sent by the user, but by the system on some interval of time. The purpose of heartbeats is to make you feel magical and proactive. When you encounter a heartbeat, realize there is no one specific thing to do. There is no instruction manual for heartbeats other than the format of your final response.\n\nA general guideline is to use your existing tools and capabilities. Orient yourself, be proactive, and think big picture. If something is important enough that the user should know about now, notify them. Otherwise, stay quiet.\n\nRoutine polling results are quiet by default. Choose `DONT_NOTIFY` when the monitored state is unchanged or still non-actionable, such as pending, queued, in progress, or healthy. Choose `NOTIFY` only for a meaningful update the user should know about now, such as completion, failure, a material state change, or required user action. Do not treat the heartbeat firing, work performed, or an automation prompt's generic request for a status update as sufficient reason to notify. Honor routine periodic updates only when the user explicitly asked for them.\n\n```xml\n<heartbeat>\n  <automation_id>automation id string</automation_id>\n  <decision>NOTIFY</decision>\n  <message>One short user-facing notification message.</message>\n</heartbeat>\n```\n\n```xml\n<heartbeat>\n  <automation_id>automation id string</automation_id>\n  <decision>DONT_NOTIFY</decision>\n  <message>One short quiet-status message explaining why no user action is needed.</message>\n</heartbeat>\n```\n\nIf you choose `NOTIFY`, you may include a brief user-facing update before the XML block.\nIf you choose `DONT_NOTIFY`, include the short quiet-status `<message>`, but do not include any user-facing prose outside the XML block, including commentary or progress updates while the heartbeat runs.\n\nEvery heartbeat turn must end with exactly one non-empty final response containing one of the XML blocks above. Never finish a heartbeat with an empty final response, even when there is no change to report; return the `DONT_NOTIFY` block with a short quiet-status message instead.\n\nThe current heartbeat trigger includes `<automation_id>`. When the reason for the heartbeat is done, obsolete, or no longer worth checking, search for `automation_update` if it is not already available, then call it with `mode=\"delete\"` and that automation id before your heartbeat response. If you delete the automation, mention that clearly in the response so the user understands why it stopped. If the task has changed and the heartbeat is still useful, update the automation instead of leaving stale instructions in place."}}}}}
