{"timestamp":"2026-09-06T22:31:16.361Z","ordinal":10050,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a07177-9855-71a2-89cd-4a9a37cdbfcb","turn_id":"01a078d8-8fe1-7512-8383-08263a40e40e","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-d06de873-a329-46b7-bf37-697691ea1d06","process_id":"10321","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","gh pr view 12905 --repo jdx/mise --json body,mergeable,headRefOid"],"cwd":"file:///Users/jdx/.codex/worktrees/141d/mise","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"gh pr view 12905 --repo jdx/mise --json body,mergeable,headRefOid"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"{\"body\":\"A backup upload succeeding does not prove that files can be recovered after conflicts, a crash, or loss of a machine. Add one acceptance journey using three isolated homes and a local Git remote, plus a user-facing recovery drill that distinguishes repository authentication from the decryption identity.\\n\\nThe test covers independent offline edits, conflict resolution, interruption after a live file is written but before the outcome checkpoint is recorded, stale-operation recovery and undo, fresh-machine bootstrap, refusal without a decryption key, and restoration of a recovery-only file using an independent age identity. It verifies the restored 0600 permissions and that the file never entered the shared branch. The process interruption uses a Git shim scoped to the test child; no production fault-injection hooks are added.\\n\\nStacked on #12904 (`codex/dotfiles-command-capture`). Linux discovers the test automatically, and it is explicitly included in macOS CI.\\n\\nValidation: `mise run test:e2e e2e/cli/test_dotfiles_recovery_journey` passed, along with scoped safe hk checks. This uses software age identities and a local Git remote; live hosting authentication and hardware identity plugins are not exercised.\\n\\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Codex; model: openai/unavailable; version: unavailable.*\\n\\n<!-- CURSOR_SUMMARY -->\\n---\\n\\n> [!NOTE]\\n> **Low Risk**\\n> Changes are limited to a new e2e script, user documentation, and macOS workflow wiring; no production dotfiles or sync logic is modified.\\n> \\n> **Overview**\\n> Adds an end-to-end **recovery journey** that simulates three isolated homes against a local bare Git remote, exercising dotfiles sync, conflict handling, crash mid-pull, undo, and encrypted cross-machine rollback—not just successful backup upload.\\n> \\n> The new `e2e/cli/test_dotfiles_recovery_journey` script tracks shared and `--no-share` paths, enables `--encrypt-backups` with a second recovery age recipient, and walks through offline divergent edits, paused pull with `--take-remote`, a child-scoped `git` shim that kills the process after the live file updates but before the outcome checkpoint, then `history --pending`, `save`, and `undo`. A third machine bootstraps from the origin, cannot restore a private backup without a key, then recovers via `MISE_AGE_KEY` and `rollback --to laptop/latest`, checking mode `600` and that the private file never landed on the shared setup branch.\\n> \\n> **Docs** gain a “Test recovery before you need it” section in `docs/history.md`, separating repo auth from decryption identity and showing a spare-machine drill (`sync`, `machines`, `rollback` with `--dry-run`). **CI** explicitly runs this test on the macOS unit job alongside the other dotfiles e2e targets.\\n> \\n> <sup>Reviewed by [Cursor Bugbot](https://cursor.com/bugbot) for commit c627ea6233fba82722e031c4e3ceda97f833319d. Bugbot is set up for automated code reviews on this repo. Configure [here](https://www.cursor.com/dashboard/bugbot).</sup>\\n<!-- /CURSOR_SUMMARY -->\\n\\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\\n\\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\\n\\n- **Documentation**\\n  - Added guidance for testing encrypted backup recovery before it is needed.\\n  - Documented how to restore machine-specific files, verify permissions, configure a recovery identity, and safely undo a restore.\\n\\n- **Tests**\\n  - Expanded end-to-end coverage for dotfile recovery, including encrypted backups, sync conflicts, interrupted operations, undo behavior, and recovery on another machine.\\n\\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\",\"headRefOid\":\"c627ea6233fba82722e031c4e3ceda97f833319d\",\"mergeable\":\"MERGEABLE\"}\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"{\"body\":\"A backup upload succeeding does not prove that files can be recovered after conflicts, a crash, or loss of a machine. Add one acceptance journey using three isolated homes and a local Git remote, plus a user-facing recovery drill that distinguishes repository authentication from the decryption identity.\\n\\nThe test covers independent offline edits, conflict resolution, interruption after a live file is written but before the outcome checkpoint is recorded, stale-operation recovery and undo, fresh-machine bootstrap, refusal without a decryption key, and restoration of a recovery-only file using an independent age identity. It verifies the restored 0600 permissions and that the file never entered the shared branch. The process interruption uses a Git shim scoped to the test child; no production fault-injection hooks are added.\\n\\nStacked on #12904 (`codex/dotfiles-command-capture`). Linux discovers the test automatically, and it is explicitly included in macOS CI.\\n\\nValidation: `mise run test:e2e e2e/cli/test_dotfiles_recovery_journey` passed, along with scoped safe hk checks. This uses software age identities and a local Git remote; live hosting authentication and hardware identity plugins are not exercised.\\n\\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Codex; model: openai/unavailable; version: unavailable.*\\n\\n<!-- CURSOR_SUMMARY -->\\n---\\n\\n> [!NOTE]\\n> **Low Risk**\\n> Changes are limited to a new e2e script, user documentation, and macOS workflow wiring; no production dotfiles or sync logic is modified.\\n> \\n> **Overview**\\n> Adds an end-to-end **recovery journey** that simulates three isolated homes against a local bare Git remote, exercising dotfiles sync, conflict handling, crash mid-pull, undo, and encrypted cross-machine rollback—not just successful backup upload.\\n> \\n> The new `e2e/cli/test_dotfiles_recovery_journey` script tracks shared and `--no-share` paths, enables `--encrypt-backups` with a second recovery age recipient, and walks through offline divergent edits, paused pull with `--take-remote`, a child-scoped `git` shim that kills the process after the live file updates but before the outcome checkpoint, then `history --pending`, `save`, and `undo`. A third machine bootstraps from the origin, cannot restore a private backup without a key, then recovers via `MISE_AGE_KEY` and `rollback --to laptop/latest`, checking mode `600` and that the private file never landed on the shared setup branch.\\n> \\n> **Docs** gain a “Test recovery before you need it” section in `docs/history.md`, separating repo auth from decryption identity and showing a spare-machine drill (`sync`, `machines`, `rollback` with `--dry-run`). **CI** explicitly runs this test on the macOS unit job alongside the other dotfiles e2e targets.\\n> \\n> <sup>Reviewed by [Cursor Bugbot](https://cursor.com/bugbot) for commit c627ea6233fba82722e031c4e3ceda97f833319d. Bugbot is set up for automated code reviews on this repo. Configure [here](https://www.cursor.com/dashboard/bugbot).</sup>\\n<!-- /CURSOR_SUMMARY -->\\n\\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\\n\\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\\n\\n- **Documentation**\\n  - Added guidance for testing encrypted backup recovery before it is needed.\\n  - Documented how to restore machine-specific files, verify permissions, configure a recovery identity, and safely undo a restore.\\n\\n- **Tests**\\n  - Expanded end-to-end coverage for dotfile recovery, including encrypted backups, sync conflicts, interrupted operations, undo behavior, and recovery on another machine.\\n\\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\",\"headRefOid\":\"c627ea6233fba82722e031c4e3ceda97f833319d\",\"mergeable\":\"MERGEABLE\"}\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":275272709},"formatted_output":"{\"body\":\"A backup upload succeeding does not prove that files can be recovered after conflicts, a crash, or loss of a machine. Add one acceptance journey using three isolated homes and a local Git remote, plus a user-facing recovery drill that distinguishes repository authentication from the decryption identity.\\n\\nThe test covers independent offline edits, conflict resolution, interruption after a live file is written but before the outcome checkpoint is recorded, stale-operation recovery and undo, fresh-machine bootstrap, refusal without a decryption key, and restoration of a recovery-only file using an independent age identity. It verifies the restored 0600 permissions and that the file never entered the shared branch. The process interruption uses a Git shim scoped to the test child; no production fault-injection hooks are added.\\n\\nStacked on #12904 (`codex/dotfiles-command-capture`). Linux discovers the test automatically, and it is explicitly included in macOS CI.\\n\\nValidation: `mise run test:e2e e2e/cli/test_dotfiles_recovery_journey` passed, along with scoped safe hk checks. This uses software age identities and a local Git remote; live hosting authentication and hardware identity plugins are not exercised.\\n\\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Codex; model: openai/unavailable; version: unavailable.*\\n\\n<!-- CURSOR_SUMMARY -->\\n---\\n\\n> [!NOTE]\\n> **Low Risk**\\n> Changes are limited to a new e2e script, user documentation, and macOS workflow wiring; no production dotfiles or sync logic is modified.\\n> \\n> **Overview**\\n> Adds an end-to-end **recovery journey** that simulates three isolated homes against a local bare Git remote, exercising dotfiles sync, conflict handling, crash mid-pull, 