{"timestamp":"2026-09-06T22:45:32.513Z","ordinal":10394,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a07177-9855-71a2-89cd-4a9a37cdbfcb","turn_id":"01a078d9-c0cd-7843-901a-b76dfa554ca0","item":{"type":"FileChange","id":"exec-24beb1ec-ec57-4234-8b45-46ca6969265f","changes":{"/Users/jdx/.codex/worktrees/dotfiles-watcher/mise/e2e/cli/test_dotfiles_watch_pending":{"type":"update","unified_diff":"@@ -13,3 +13,6 @@\n watcher=$!\n-cleanup() { kill \"$watcher\" 2>/dev/null || true; }\n+cleanup() {\n+  kill \"$watcher\" 2>/dev/null || true\n+  wait \"$watcher\" || true\n+}\n trap cleanup EXIT\n","move_path":null}},"status":"completed","stdout":"Success. Updated the following files:\nM /Users/jdx/.codex/worktrees/dotfiles-watcher/mise/e2e/cli/test_dotfiles_watch_pending\n","stderr":""},"started_at_ms":1788734732512,"completed_at_ms":1788734732513}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-06T22:45:32.646Z","ordinal":10395,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a07177-9855-71a2-89cd-4a9a37cdbfcb","turn_id":"01a078d9-c0cd-7843-901a-b76dfa554ca0","session_id":"01a07177-9855-71a2-89cd-4a9a37cdbfcb","root_turn_id":"01a078d9-c0cd-7843-901a-b76dfa554ca0","response_id":"resp_02be90ffdd5dff4a016a9ded01640487d0949670ded98e1bd8","usage":{"input_tokens":93173,"cached_input_tokens":88704,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":272,"reasoning_output_tokens":13,"total_tokens":93445},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":6910761,"cached_input_tokens":6794752,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":16467,"reasoning_output_tokens":3884,"total_tokens":6927228},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":183759015,"cached_input_tokens":179436672,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":479979,"reasoning_output_tokens":94454,"total_tokens":184238994}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-06T22:45:35.195Z","ordinal":10396,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a07177-9855-71a2-89cd-4a9a37cdbfcb","turn_id":"01a078d9-c0cd-7843-901a-b76dfa554ca0","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-743f6a01-7f4a-4c03-baba-380ef1a2ca1c","process_id":"68480","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","gh api repos/jdx/mise/pulls/12904 --jq '{title,body}'; gh api repos/jdx/mise/pulls/12905 --jq '{title,body}'"],"cwd":"file:///Users/jdx/.codex/worktrees/141d/mise","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"gh api repos/jdx/mise/pulls/12904 --jq '{title,body}'; gh api repos/jdx/mise/pulls/12905 --jq '{title,body}'"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"{\"body\":\"External updates and agent commands can edit tracked files without leaving a reliable before/after boundary. `mise bootstrap dotfiles capture --label \\\"omarchy update\\\" -- omarchy-update` now records a linked checkpoint pair and the command's outcome while preserving its exit status. Capture failures warn without preventing the external command from running.\\n\\n`history diff --operation [ref]` compares that operation with its recorded protective checkpoint rather than whichever save happens to precede it; without a reference it finds the newest operation even after later saves. `history --label` filters labeled checkpoints. No-op runs, failures, and crash recovery retain their labels and file state, including manual-save files.\\n\\nStacked on #12903 (`codex/dotfiles-launch-readiness`). Capture observes tracked files over an interval, including concurrent edits; it does not journal external writes individually or reverse package/service effects. Restore selected files from the recorded before checkpoint. Arbitrary command execution is explicitly unclassified in the command-effects catalog.\\n\\nValidation: capture and existing bootstrap-history E2E passed, including exit 37, signal exit 143, spawn failure, disabled history, no-op capture, later saves, and SIGKILL recovery. All 66 history unit tests and 5 command-effects tests passed. Build, CLI/man generation, scoped safe hk checks, and strict workspace Clippy (`--workspace --all-features --all-targets -- -D warnings`) passed. Added macOS CI coverage; Linux discovers the test automatically.\\n\\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Codex; model: openai/unavailable; version: unavailable.*\\n\\n\\n\\u003c!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai --\\u003e\\n\\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\\n\\n- **New Features**\\n  - Added `mise bootstrap dotfiles capture` to record tracked-file changes before and after running a command.\\n  - Added optional labels for dotfiles history filtering.\\n  - Added `history diff --operation` to compare an operation with its protective checkpoint.\\n  - Added `track --private` support.\\n  - When `origin set --sync` is omitted, mise now prompts instead of syncing by default.\\n\\n- **Documentation**\\n  - Added CLI reference and usage guidance for capture operations and related history options.\\n\\n- **Tests**\\n  - Added end-to-end coverage for capture, rollback, failures, interruptions, and recovery.\\n\\n\\u003c!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai --\\u003e\",\"title\":\"feat(dotfiles): capture history around external commands\"}\n{\"body\":\"A backup upload succeeding does not prove that files can be recovered after conflicts, a crash, or loss of a machine. Add one acceptance journey using three isolated homes and a local Git remote, plus a user-facing recovery drill that distinguishes repository authentication from the decryption identity.\\n\\nThe test covers independent offline edits, conflict resolution, interruption after a live file is written but before the outcome checkpoint is recorded, stale-operation recovery and undo, fresh-machine bootstrap, refusal without a decryption key, and restoration of a recovery-only file using an independent age identity. It verifies the restored 0600 permissions and that the file never entered the shared branch. The process interruption uses a Git shim scoped to the test child; no production fault-injection hooks are added.\\n\\nStacked on #12904 (`codex/dotfiles-command-capture`). Linux discovers the test automatically, and it is explicitly included in macOS CI.\\n\\nValidation: `mise run test:e2e e2e/cli/test_dotfiles_recovery_journey` passed, along with scoped safe hk checks. This uses software age identities and a local Git remote; live hosting authentication and hardware identity plugins are not exercised.\\n\\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Codex; model: openai/unavailable; version: unavailable.*\\n\\n\\u003c!-- CURSOR_SUMMARY --\\u003e\\n---\\n\\n\\u003e [!NOTE]\\n\\u003e **Low Risk**\\n\\u003e Changes are limited to a new e2e script, user documentation, and macOS workflow wiring; no production dotfiles or sync logic is modified.\\n\\u003e \\n\\u003e **Overview**\\n\\u003e Adds an end-to-end **recovery journey** that simulates three isolated homes against a local bare Git remote, exercising dotfiles sync, conflict handling, crash mid-pull, undo, and encrypted cross-machine rollback—not just successful backup upload.\\n\\u003e \\n\\u003e The new `e2e/cli/test_dotfiles_recovery_journey` script tracks shared and `--no-share` paths, enables `--encrypt-backups` with a second recovery age recipient, and walks through offline divergent edits, paused pull with `--take-remote`, a child-scoped `git` shim that kills the process after the live file updates but before the outcome checkpoint, then `history --pending`, `save`, and `undo`. A third machine bootstraps from the origin, cannot restore a private backup without a key, then recovers via `MISE_AGE_KEY` and `rollback --to laptop/latest`, checking mode `600` and that the private file never landed on the shared setup branch.\\n\\u003e \\n\\u003e **Docs** gain a “Test recovery before you need it” section in `docs/history.md`, separating repo auth from decryption identity and showing a spare-machine drill (`sync`, `machines`, `rollback` with `--dry-run`). **CI** explicitly runs this test on the macOS unit job alongside the other dotfiles e2e targets.\\n\\u003e \\n\\u003e \\u003csup\\u003eReviewed by [Cursor Bugbot](https://cursor.com/bugbot) for commit c627ea6233fba82722e031c4e3ceda97f833319d. Bugbot is set up for automated code reviews on this repo. Configure [here](https://www.cursor.com/dashboard/bugbot).\\u003c/sup\\u003e\\n\\u003c!-- /CURSOR_SUMMARY --\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003c!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai --\\u003e\\n\\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\\n\\n- **Documentation**\\n  - Added guidance for testing encrypted backup recovery before it is needed.\\n  - Documented how to restore machine-specific files, verify permissions, configure a recovery identity, and safely undo a restore.\\n\\n- **Tests**\\n  - Expanded end-to-end coverage for dotfile recovery, including encrypted backups, sync conflicts, interrupted operations, undo behavior, and recovery on another machine.\\n\\n\\u003c!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai --\\u003e\",\"title\":\"test(dotfiles): exercise recovery across three machines\"}\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"{\"body\":\"External updates and agent commands can edit tracked files without leaving a reliable before/after boundary. `mise bootstrap dotfiles capture --label \\\"omarchy update\\\" -- omarchy-update` now records a linked checkpoint pair and the command's outcome while preserving its exit status. Capture failures warn without preventing the external command from running.\\n\\n`history diff --operation [ref]` compares that operation with its recorded protective checkpoint rather than whichever save happens to precede it; without a reference it finds the newest operation even after later saves. `history --label` filters labeled checkpoints. No-op runs, failures, and crash recovery retain their labels and file state, including manual-save files.\\n\\nStacked on #12903 (`codex/dotfiles-launch-readiness`). Capture observes tracked files over an interval, including concurrent edits; it does not journal external writes individually or reverse package/service effects. Restore selected files from the recorded before checkpoint. Arbitrary command execution is explicitly unclassified in the command-effects catalog.\\n\\nValidation: capture and existing bootstrap-history E2E passed, including exit 37, signal exit 143, spawn failure, disabled history, no-op capture, later saves, and SIGKILL recovery. All 66 history unit tests and 5 command-effects tests passed. Build, CLI/man generation, scoped safe hk checks, and strict workspace Clippy (`--workspace --all-features --all-targets -- -D warnings`) passed. Added macOS CI coverage; Linux discovers the test automatically.\\n\\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Codex; model: openai/unavailable; version: unavailable.*\\n\\n\\n\\u003c!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai --\\u003e\\n\\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\\n\\n- **New Features**\\n  - Added `mise bootstrap dotfiles capture` to record tracked-file changes before and after running a command.\\n  - Added optional labels for dotfiles history filtering.\\n  - Added `history diff --operation` to compare an operation with its protective checkpoint.\\n  - Added `track --private` support.\\n  - When `origin set --sync` is omitted, mise now prompts instead of syncing by default.\\n\\n- **Documentation**\\n  - Added CLI reference and usage guidance for capture operations and related history options.\\n\\n- **Tests**\\n  - Added end-to-end coverage for capture, rollback, failures, interruptions, and recovery.\\n\\n\\u003c!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai --\\u003e\",\"title\":\"feat(dotfiles): capture history around external commands\"}\n{\"body\":\"A backup upload succeeding does not prove that files can be recovered after conflicts, a crash, or loss of a machine. Add one acceptance journey using three isolated homes and a local Git remote, plus a user-facing recovery drill that distinguishes repository authentication from the decryption identity.\\n\\nThe test covers independent offline edits, conflict resolution, interruption after a live file is written but before the outcome checkpoint is recorded, stale-operation recovery and undo, fresh-machine bootstrap, refusal without a decryption key, and restoration of a recovery-only file using an independent age identity. It verifies the restored 0600 permissions and that the file never entered the shared branch. The process interruption uses a Git shim scoped to the test child; no production fault-injection hooks are added.\\n\\nStacked on #12904 (`codex/dotfiles-command-capture`). Linux discovers the test automatically, and it is explicitly included in macOS CI.\\n\\nValidation: `mise run test:e2e e2e/cli/test_dotfiles_recovery_journey` passed, along with scoped safe hk checks. This uses software age identities and a local Git remote; live hosting authentication and hardware identity plugins are not exercised.\\n\\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Codex; model: openai/unavailable; version: unavailable.*\\n\\n\\u003c!-- CURSOR_SUMMARY --\\u003e\\n---\\n\\n\\u003e [!NOTE]\\n\\u003e **Low Risk**\\n\\u003e Changes are limited to a new e2e script, user documentation, and macOS workflow wiring; no production dotfiles or sync logic is modified.\\n\\u003e \\n\\u003e **Overview**\\n\\u003e Adds an end-to-end **recovery journey** that simulates three isolated homes against a local bare Git remote, exercising dotfiles sync, conflict handling, crash mid-pull, undo, and encrypted cross-machine rollback—not just successful backup upload.\\n\\u003e \\n\\u003e The new `e2e/cli/test_dotfiles_recovery_journey` script tracks shared and `--no-share` paths, enables `--encrypt-backups` with a second recovery age recipient, and walks through offline divergent edits, paused pull with `--take-remote`, a child-scoped `git` shim that kills the process after the live file updates but before the outcome checkpoint, then `history --pending`, `save`, and `undo`. A third machine bootstraps from the origin, cannot restore a private backup without a key, then recovers via `MISE_AGE_KEY` and `rollback --to laptop/latest`, checking mode `600` and that the private file never landed on the shared setup branch.\\n\\u003e \\n\\u003e **Docs** gain a “Test recovery before you need it” section in `docs/history.md`, separating repo auth from decryption identity and showing a spare-machine drill (`sync`, `machines`, `rollback` with `--dry-run`). **CI** explicitly runs this test on the macOS unit job alongside the other dotfiles e2e targets.\\n\\u003e \\n\\u003e \\u003csup\\u003eReviewed by [Cursor Bugbot](https://cursor.com/bugbot) for commit c627ea6233fba82722e031c4e3ceda97f833319d. Bugbot is set up for automated code reviews on this repo. Configure [here](https://www.cursor.com/dashboard/bugbot).\\u003c/sup\\u003e\\n\\u003c!-- /CURSOR_SUMMARY --\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003c!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai --\\u003e\\n\\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\\n\\n- **Documentation**\\n  - Added guidance for testing encrypted backup recovery before it is needed.\\n  - Documented how to restore machine-specific files, verify permissions, configure a recovery identity, and safely undo a restore.\\n\\n- **Tests**\\n  - Expanded end-to-end coverage for dotfile recovery, including encrypted backups, sync conflicts, interrupted operations, undo behavior, and recovery on another machine.\\n\\n\\u003c!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai --\\u003e\",\"title\":\"test(dotfiles): exercise recovery across three machines\"}\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":1,"nanos":278637459},"formatted_output":"{\"body\":\"External updates and agent commands can edit tracked files without leaving a reliable before/after boundary. `mise bootstrap dotfiles capture --label \\\"omarchy 